1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,400
[JEFF] Welcome to Backbone. I'm Jeff Keltner.

2
00:00:02,400 --> 00:00:04,000
[CYRUS] And I'm Cyrus Mistry.

3
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:08,600
[JEFF] And this is the show where we go looking for the technologies that hold the

4
00:00:08,600 --> 00:00:13,200
[JEFF] modern world up — the stuff you never think about until the day it stops

5
00:00:13,200 --> 00:00:17,800
[JEFF] working. And Cyrus, I have to say, for the first one, I think we picked

6
00:00:17,800 --> 00:00:22,400
[JEFF] well.

7
00:00:22,400 --> 00:00:26,400
[CYRUS] [dry] We picked the most boring object in your house.

8
00:00:26,400 --> 00:00:32,200
[JEFF] We did. And I mean that as the highest compliment, because I don't think either

9
00:00:32,200 --> 00:00:38,000
[JEFF] of us is going to look at it the same way again.

10
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:44,000
[CYRUS] A hundred percent. I've now told my kids more about this than they ever wanted

11
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:50,000
[CYRUS] to know. They take it for granted. Like, there's fridges now. That's just... the world.

12
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:54,800
[JEFF] And the thing is, it wasn't. For most of human history, cold was weather. It

13
00:00:54,800 --> 00:00:59,600
[JEFF] happened to you. Nobody sold it. And the first guy who tried to sell it

14
00:00:59,600 --> 00:01:04,400
[JEFF] got laughed at in the newspaper.

15
00:01:04,400 --> 00:01:08,000
[CYRUS] [curious] Okay. I want to hear about that guy.

16
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:13,428
[JEFF] [reflective] Okay. So it's February, eighteen oh six. Boston Harbor. And a small two-masted ship,

17
00:01:13,428 --> 00:01:18,857
[JEFF] a brig, has just cleared customs, headed for Martinique. Which is a French sugar island

18
00:01:18,857 --> 00:01:24,285
[JEFF] in the Caribbean. Three weeks south. Two thousand miles. And the town paper, the Boston

19
00:01:24,285 --> 00:01:29,714
[JEFF] Gazette, runs a little notice about it. I'm going to read it, because it might

20
00:01:29,714 --> 00:01:35,142
[JEFF] be my favorite sentence in the whole story. Quote: "No joke. A vessel has cleared

21
00:01:35,142 --> 00:01:40,571
[JEFF] at the Custom House for Martinique with a cargo of ice. We hope this will

22
00:01:40,571 --> 00:01:46,000
[JEFF] not prove a slippery speculation."

23
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:50,200
[CYRUS] Slippery speculation. That is a two-hundred-and-twenty-year-old burn. And it still lands. Like, whoever wrote that

24
00:01:50,200 --> 00:01:54,400
[CYRUS] went home happy that night.

25
00:01:54,400 --> 00:02:00,266
[JEFF] Oh, absolutely. And the whole town thought this was insane. The ship is carrying about

26
00:02:00,266 --> 00:02:06,133
[JEFF] eighty tons of ice. Pond ice. Cut off a family farm north of Boston, in

27
00:02:06,133 --> 00:02:12,000
[JEFF] a town called Saugus. And it belongs to a twenty-three-year-old named Frederic Tudor.

28
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:13,200
[CYRUS] [genuinely surprised] Twenty-three?

29
00:02:13,200 --> 00:02:19,200
[JEFF] Yeah. And here's the thing you have to understand about Frederic Tudor. He is a

30
00:02:19,200 --> 00:02:25,200
[JEFF] Boston Brahmin kid. His father was a lawyer — studied law in John Adams's office,

31
00:02:25,200 --> 00:02:31,200
[JEFF] was George Washington's first Judge Advocate General in the Continental Army. Serious family. His older

32
00:02:31,200 --> 00:02:37,200
[JEFF] brother William went to Harvard, class of seventeen ninety-six, and later founded the North American

33
00:02:37,200 --> 00:02:43,200
[JEFF] Review. And Frederic... is the one son who didn't go to Harvard. He left school

34
00:02:43,200 --> 00:02:49,200
[JEFF] at thirteen. Thirteen! Went to work as the youngest apprentice in a counting house on

35
00:02:49,200 --> 00:02:55,200
[JEFF] State Street. And his own family did not think much of him. There's a journal

36
00:02:55,200 --> 00:03:01,200
[JEFF] entry from his older brother, in eighteen oh one, that says — and I'm paraphrasing

37
00:03:01,200 --> 00:03:07,200
[JEFF] only slightly — "It is the greatest difficulty I can get him to do anything.

38
00:03:07,200 --> 00:03:13,200
[JEFF] I hope there will be a time when I shall be able to rouse him."

39
00:03:13,200 --> 00:03:15,600
[CYRUS] [dry] So he's the family disappointment.

40
00:03:15,600 --> 00:03:21,120
[JEFF] Completely. And on top of that, his father has just lost a big chunk of

41
00:03:21,120 --> 00:03:26,640
[JEFF] the family fortune speculating on land in South Boston. So you've got a kid who

42
00:03:26,640 --> 00:03:32,160
[JEFF] opted out of the approved path, whose own brother thinks he's lazy, whose dad just

43
00:03:32,160 --> 00:03:37,680
[JEFF] lost the money. And that kid decides the thing he's going to do with his

44
00:03:37,680 --> 00:03:43,200
[JEFF] life... is ship frozen pond water to the tropics.

45
00:03:43,200 --> 00:03:48,000
[CYRUS] [skeptical] Okay, but where does the idea even come from? Like, nobody in Boston is

46
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:52,800
[CYRUS] sitting around going, you know what the Caribbean needs?

47
00:03:52,800 --> 00:03:57,933
[JEFF] Yeah, that's the thing — the record doesn't really tell us. What we do have

48
00:03:57,933 --> 00:04:03,066
[JEFF] is his diary. August first, eighteen oh five: "William and myself have this day determined

49
00:04:03,066 --> 00:04:08,200
[JEFF] to get together what property we have and embark on the undertaking of carrying ice

50
00:04:08,200 --> 00:04:13,333
[JEFF] to the West Indies." And then, later that same year, this line, which I love:

51
00:04:13,333 --> 00:04:18,466
[JEFF] "People only laugh and believe me not when I tell them I'm going to the

52
00:04:18,466 --> 00:04:23,600
[JEFF] West Indies."

53
00:04:23,600 --> 00:04:29,600
[CYRUS] So he knows. He knows he's the joke before the ship ever leaves.

54
00:04:29,600 --> 00:04:35,200
[JEFF] He's writing it down! And it gets better — nobody in Boston would carry the

55
00:04:35,200 --> 00:04:40,800
[JEFF] cargo. Not one ship owner. Because eighty tons of ice melts, the meltwater floods your

56
00:04:40,800 --> 00:04:46,400
[JEFF] hold, rots your timbers, ruins your ship. So Tudor buys his own ship. He'd raised

57
00:04:46,400 --> 00:04:52,000
[JEFF] about ten thousand dollars, most of it borrowed, and he spends nearly half of it

58
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:57,600
[JEFF] — forty-seven hundred and fifty dollars — on the brig.

59
00:04:57,600 --> 00:05:02,800
[CYRUS] Half the money on the boat before he's sold a pound of ice.

60
00:05:02,800 --> 00:05:08,750
[JEFF] Yeah. And then the ice goes in, and he sails. Three weeks. And the cargo

61
00:05:08,750 --> 00:05:14,700
[JEFF] actually does okay on the voyage — a lot of it survives. The problem is

62
00:05:14,700 --> 00:05:20,650
[JEFF] what happens when he gets there. Because there's no icehouse in Martinique. His brother and

63
00:05:20,650 --> 00:05:26,600
[JEFF] a cousin had gone ahead to build one and line up buyers, and they'd basically

64
00:05:26,600 --> 00:05:32,550
[JEFF] failed at both. So the ice is sitting on a dock in the Caribbean, melting,

65
00:05:32,550 --> 00:05:38,500
[JEFF] while Frederic Tudor goes looking for anyone who wants it. And he prints a handbill.

66
00:05:38,500 --> 00:05:44,450
[JEFF] March seventh, eighteen oh six. "The price is ten cents a pound. It is necessary

67
00:05:44,450 --> 00:05:50,400
[JEFF] to bring a wool cloth or a piece of covering to wrap the ice."

68
00:05:50,400 --> 00:05:54,400
[CYRUS] [incredulous] He has to tell people to bring a blanket.

69
00:05:54,400 --> 00:05:59,066
[JEFF] Because nobody there has anywhere to keep it! His biographer quotes his diary: customers wrapping

70
00:05:59,066 --> 00:06:03,733
[JEFF] their chunk of ice in a blanket, waddling home, and wondering why it's gone by

71
00:06:03,733 --> 00:06:08,400
[JEFF] the time they get there.

72
00:06:08,400 --> 00:06:10,400
[CYRUS] Of course it's gone.

73
00:06:10,400 --> 00:06:16,000
[JEFF] The one thing that does sell is ice cream. He makes it himself, and a

74
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:21,600
[JEFF] local businessman clears a small fortune in one night. But that's it. And there's a

75
00:06:21,600 --> 00:06:27,200
[JEFF] line in his diary from that trip that I think is the whole emotional core

76
00:06:27,200 --> 00:06:32,800
[JEFF] of the guy: "I found myself without money and without friends and with only a

77
00:06:32,800 --> 00:06:38,400
[JEFF] cargo of ice in a torrid zone to depend on."

78
00:06:38,400 --> 00:06:39,200
[CYRUS] Oof.

79
00:06:39,200 --> 00:06:45,200
[JEFF] He loses about forty-five hundred dollars on the voyage. Nearly half his stake — almost

80
00:06:45,200 --> 00:06:51,200
[JEFF] exactly what the ship cost. And then it gets worse. He tries Havana, loses more.

81
00:06:51,200 --> 00:06:57,200
[JEFF] Then Jefferson's embargo in eighteen oh seven wrecks the whole West Indies trade, and he's

82
00:06:57,200 --> 00:07:03,200
[JEFF] tens of thousands of dollars in debt. And within a few years — more than

83
00:07:03,200 --> 00:07:09,200
[JEFF] once, between eighteen oh nine and eighteen thirteen — Frederic Tudor is in debtors' prison.

84
00:07:09,200 --> 00:07:13,400
[CYRUS] [exhales] Which we don't even have a modern equivalent for. You failed at a business,

85
00:07:13,400 --> 00:07:17,600
[CYRUS] and they put you in jail.

86
00:07:17,600 --> 00:07:23,600
[JEFF] Right. And in his journal, through all of this, there's one line that keeps showing

87
00:07:23,600 --> 00:07:29,600
[JEFF] up. And I don't know exactly when he first wrote it — nobody does —

88
00:07:29,600 --> 00:07:35,600
[JEFF] but he wrote it, and he came back to it. "Let those laugh who win."

89
00:07:35,600 --> 00:07:38,800
[CYRUS] [reflective] That's the whole guy in five words.

90
00:07:38,800 --> 00:07:43,200
[JEFF] That should be on the wall of every founder's office. Honestly.

91
00:07:43,200 --> 00:07:49,000
[CYRUS] I mean — this guy would fit right in in Silicon Valley. Like, drop him

92
00:07:49,000 --> 00:07:54,800
[CYRUS] in Palo Alto in twenty twenty-six and he has a pitch deck by Tuesday.

93
00:07:54,800 --> 00:07:56,400
[JEFF] [skeptical] Okay, receipts. Why?

94
00:07:56,400 --> 00:08:02,200
[CYRUS] Look at the shape of it. Dropout. Family thinks he's lazy. Product nobody's asked for.

95
00:08:02,200 --> 00:08:08,000
[CYRUS] Burns half his capital on infrastructure before revenue. And — this is the part —

96
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:13,800
[CYRUS] his twenty years of losses were financed. That's family money absorbing the hits. That's not

97
00:08:13,800 --> 00:08:19,600
[CYRUS] grit alone, that's a seed round from your parents that keeps getting extended.

98
00:08:19,600 --> 00:08:26,000
[JEFF] That's fair. Although most founders I know would've quit after the second prison stint.

99
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:29,600
[CYRUS] [deadpan] The good ones would've pivoted to ice cream.

100
00:05:42,483 --> 00:05:46,616
[JEFF] So, okay. Before we follow him out of jail — I think we have to

101
00:05:46,616 --> 00:05:50,749
[JEFF] answer the newspaper. Because the Gazette wasn't being dumb. Everybody in Boston thought this was

102
00:05:50,749 --> 00:05:54,882
[JEFF] funny.

103
00:05:54,882 --> 00:06:00,482
[CYRUS] Right, and that's the question I kept coming back to. Why was it a joke?

104
00:06:00,482 --> 00:06:06,082
[CYRUS] We're sitting here in a world where cold is the most boring thing imaginable. What

105
00:06:06,082 --> 00:06:11,682
[CYRUS] did the world look like that made a boatload of ice funny?

106
00:06:11,682 --> 00:06:17,682
[JEFF] Yeah — and you went deeper on this than I did. What did you find?

107
00:06:17,682 --> 00:06:23,362
[CYRUS] So this is the part I found genuinely hard to picture. In eighteen oh six,

108
00:06:23,362 --> 00:06:29,042
[CYRUS] cold is a season. It's not a thing you have — it's a thing that

109
00:06:29,042 --> 00:06:34,722
[CYRUS] happens to you, for a few months, if you live far enough north. Outside of

110
00:06:34,722 --> 00:06:40,402
[CYRUS] that belt, it basically doesn't exist. My favorite example is Calcutta. Before American ice showed

111
00:06:40,402 --> 00:06:46,082
[CYRUS] up there, the local ice was made by laying porous clay pots of water out

112
00:06:46,082 --> 00:06:51,762
[CYRUS] on straw in shallow trenches on cold, clear winter nights. The pots sweat, the straw

113
00:06:51,762 --> 00:06:57,442
[CYRUS] insulates you from the ground, a clear night radiates heat to the sky, and by

114
00:06:57,442 --> 00:07:03,122
[CYRUS] morning there's a skin of ice you can scrape off. It works a few nights

115
00:07:03,122 --> 00:07:08,802
[CYRUS] a year. And what you get is gritty, slushy, and expensive. That was ice, in

116
00:07:08,802 --> 00:07:14,482
[CYRUS] one of the biggest cities on earth.

117
00:07:14,482 --> 00:07:15,682
[JEFF] And in Boston?

118
00:07:15,682 --> 00:07:20,682
[CYRUS] In Boston you had winter, and then you had ways to stretch winter. A springhouse

119
00:07:20,682 --> 00:07:25,682
[CYRUS] over a cold stream. A root cellar. Rich estates had icehouses — dig a pit,

120
00:07:25,682 --> 00:07:30,682
[CYRUS] pack it in January, hope it lasts to July. And mostly, you ate in season,

121
00:07:30,682 --> 00:07:35,682
[CYRUS] because there was no alternative.

122
00:07:35,682 --> 00:07:40,482
[JEFF] Right. And there's a version of this that's kind of charming — oh, everybody ate

123
00:07:40,482 --> 00:07:45,282
[JEFF] seasonally, how lovely. And then there's the other version.

124
00:07:45,282 --> 00:07:47,282
[CYRUS] [deliberate] Which is the babies.

125
00:07:47,282 --> 00:07:51,982
[JEFF] Yeah. So — the lethal version of no cold is summer, in a city, with

126
00:07:51,982 --> 00:07:56,682
[JEFF] an infant. In eighteen ninety-one, in New York City, infant mortality was about two hundred

127
00:07:56,682 --> 00:08:01,382
[JEFF] and forty per thousand births. That is roughly one baby in four. Not surviving the

128
00:08:01,382 --> 00:08:06,082
[JEFF] first year.

129
00:08:06,082 --> 00:08:07,682
[CYRUS] One in four.

130
00:08:07,682 --> 00:08:12,582
[JEFF] And it spiked every July and August. They called it the summer complaint. And the

131
00:08:12,582 --> 00:08:17,482
[JEFF] reason is milk. Heat spoils milk fastest, and the babies who died were disproportionately the

132
00:08:17,482 --> 00:08:22,382
[JEFF] bottle-fed ones. Did you come across the Jacob Riis passage on this? How the Other

133
00:08:22,382 --> 00:08:27,282
[JEFF] Half Lives, eighteen ninety?

134
00:08:27,282 --> 00:08:31,282
[CYRUS] I did not get to Riis. Walk me through it.

135
00:08:31,282 --> 00:08:36,768
[JEFF] So Riis is a reporter, not a doctor, but he's in the tenements, and he

136
00:08:36,768 --> 00:08:42,254
[JEFF] writes: "Life in the tenements in July and August spells death to an army of

137
00:08:42,254 --> 00:08:47,740
[JEFF] little ones whom the doctor's skill is powerless to save." And the Board of Health,

138
00:08:47,740 --> 00:08:53,225
[JEFF] every summer, sends fifty specially trained doctors — he calls them the summer doctors —

139
00:08:53,225 --> 00:08:58,711
[JEFF] into the tenements with free medicine. Fifty doctors, every summer. And there's a line about

140
00:08:58,711 --> 00:09:04,197
[JEFF] the little coffins stacked on the deck of the charity boat that goes out to

141
00:09:04,197 --> 00:09:09,683
[JEFF] the city cemetery twice a week.

142
00:09:09,683 --> 00:09:12,483
[CYRUS] A scheduled boat. For the coffins.

143
00:09:12,483 --> 00:09:17,843
[JEFF] And there's an ugly detail underneath it, which is where a city's milk actually came

144
00:09:17,843 --> 00:09:23,203
[JEFF] from. Whiskey distillers kept cows in sheds next to the stills, fed them the leftover

145
00:09:23,203 --> 00:09:28,563
[JEFF] mash, and sold the milk — thin and bluish, so they'd whiten it with plaster

146
00:09:28,563 --> 00:09:33,923
[JEFF] of Paris. When the papers exposed it in eighteen fifty-eight, the Times put the toll

147
00:09:33,923 --> 00:09:39,283
[JEFF] in the thousands of babies a year.

148
00:09:39,283 --> 00:09:42,083
[CYRUS] [incredulous] Plaster of Paris. In the milk.

149
00:09:42,083 --> 00:09:48,003
[JEFF] To make it look like milk. And it's easy to make the distillery guys into

150
00:09:48,003 --> 00:09:53,923
[JEFF] monsters, but that was the only urban dairy model that worked. If milk can't survive

151
00:09:53,923 --> 00:09:59,843
[JEFF] a hot day's cart ride from the countryside, the cows have to be in the

152
00:09:59,843 --> 00:10:05,763
[JEFF] city, and the cheapest place to keep a cow in the city is next to

153
00:10:05,763 --> 00:10:11,683
[JEFF] the thing producing tons of leftover grain mash. The horror isn't personal. It's structural.

154
00:10:11,683 --> 00:10:17,416
[CYRUS] And that's the number that moves, by the way. Like — that's the number this

155
00:10:17,416 --> 00:10:23,149
[CYRUS] whole story is about moving. By nineteen fourteen, New York's rate is down to seventy-one

156
00:10:23,149 --> 00:10:28,883
[CYRUS] per thousand. Today in the United States it's about five and a half.

157
00:10:28,883 --> 00:10:30,883
[JEFF] From two hundred and forty.

158
00:10:30,883 --> 00:10:35,416
[CYRUS] [deliberate] Now — can I do the other half of this? Because I think there's

159
00:10:35,416 --> 00:10:39,949
[CYRUS] a version of the world before that's actually kind of wonderful, and it's the part

160
00:10:39,949 --> 00:10:44,483
[CYRUS] I'm weirdly grateful for.

161
00:10:44,483 --> 00:10:48,483
[JEFF] [amused] Please. I have a personal stake in this half.

162
00:10:48,483 --> 00:10:54,197
[CYRUS] So here's the thing. Humanity had already solved "no cold." Not with cold — with

163
00:10:54,197 --> 00:10:59,911
[CYRUS] salt, smoke, and time. And basically every cured food you love exists because somebody needed

164
00:10:59,911 --> 00:11:05,625
[CYRUS] summer's abundance to survive the winter without refrigeration. Bacon. Ham. Salami. Salt cod. Prosciutto. The

165
00:11:05,625 --> 00:11:11,340
[CYRUS] oldest recipe I found is Cato the Elder, a Roman, around one hundred sixty B-C,

166
00:11:11,340 --> 00:11:17,054
[CYRUS] and it's a recipe for salting hams. Layer them in salt so that "meat does

167
00:11:17,054 --> 00:11:22,768
[CYRUS] not touch meat," leave them twelve days, hang them in a draft, smoke them. And

168
00:11:22,768 --> 00:11:28,483
[CYRUS] he ends with, "No moths or worms will touch them."

169
00:11:28,483 --> 00:11:31,283
[JEFF] That's prosciutto. He's describing prosciutto.

170
00:11:31,283 --> 00:11:36,583
[CYRUS] That is functionally a country ham, written down twenty centuries before anybody built a compressor.

171
00:11:36,583 --> 00:11:41,883
[CYRUS] Salt doesn't kill the bacteria so much as it steals their water. You pack the

172
00:11:41,883 --> 00:11:47,183
[CYRUS] surface with salt, and osmosis pulls the water out of everything — including the microbes.

173
00:11:47,183 --> 00:11:52,483
[CYRUS] Get to around twenty percent salt and almost nothing can live in it. Wood smoke

174
00:11:52,483 --> 00:11:57,783
[CYRUS] is full of phenols, which slow the fat from going rancid and slow the bacteria

175
00:11:57,783 --> 00:12:03,083
[CYRUS] — but smoke doesn't penetrate very far into meat, so you salt first and smoke

176
00:12:03,083 --> 00:12:08,383
[CYRUS] second. Smoke is mostly a flavor with a preservative side hustle. Salt does the heavy

177
00:12:08,383 --> 00:12:13,683
[CYRUS] lifting.

178
00:12:13,683 --> 00:12:19,483
[JEFF] And I'd always assumed — this is the thing everybody says — that the whole

179
00:12:19,483 --> 00:12:25,283
[JEFF] spice trade was about covering up rotten meat. Right? That's why they wanted pepper.

180
00:12:25,283 --> 00:12:30,083
[CYRUS] [skeptical] Yeah, so I went looking for that, and the food historians basically say no.

181
00:12:30,083 --> 00:12:34,883
[CYRUS] Spices were incredibly expensive. Nobody was wasting saffron on a bad roast. People who could

182
00:12:34,883 --> 00:12:39,683
[CYRUS] afford fresh meat got fresh meat; everybody else had perfectly good preservation. The rotten-meat thing

183
00:12:39,683 --> 00:12:44,483
[CYRUS] is a myth.

184
00:12:44,483 --> 00:12:48,483
[JEFF] Huh. Okay, I'm going to have to retire that one.

185
00:12:48,483 --> 00:12:53,683
[CYRUS] [warmly] I know. I was sad too. But salt cod is real, and it's my

186
00:12:53,683 --> 00:12:58,883
[CYRUS] favorite. Basque fishermen were working the Newfoundland coast by fifteen seventeen, and dried, salted cod

187
00:12:58,883 --> 00:13:04,083
[CYRUS] keeps for years. A fish caught off Canada, eaten in Portugal months later. A global

188
00:13:04,083 --> 00:13:09,283
[CYRUS] food trade with zero cold in it.

189
00:13:09,283 --> 00:13:13,683
[JEFF] Yeah. And, I mean — thank God we had a couple thousand years without refrigeration,

190
00:13:13,683 --> 00:13:18,083
[JEFF] so we learned how to make pepperoni.

191
00:13:18,083 --> 00:13:21,483
[CYRUS] Some of my favorite things in the world exist because of the lack of refrigeration.

192
00:13:21,483 --> 00:13:24,883
[CYRUS] Literally.

193
00:13:24,883 --> 00:13:30,616
[JEFF] Right. So that's the world Tudor's ship is sailing into. Cold is a season. Milk

194
00:13:30,616 --> 00:13:36,349
[JEFF] kills babies every August. And people have gotten really, really good at living without it

195
00:13:36,349 --> 00:13:42,083
[JEFF] — so good that the idea of buying cold sounds like a joke.

196
00:13:42,083 --> 00:13:47,283
[CYRUS] [reflective] Right. You can't sell people a thing they don't know they lack.

197
00:13:47,283 --> 00:13:52,163
[JEFF] Yeah. And here's the thing that got me, reading all of this. That ridiculous voyage

198
00:13:52,163 --> 00:13:57,043
[JEFF] is the opening move in a story that runs two hundred years — and every

199
00:13:57,043 --> 00:14:01,923
[JEFF] single time somebody wins a round of it, the win plants the next problem. And

200
00:14:01,923 --> 00:14:06,803
[JEFF] somewhere down this road, Albert Einstein is going to try to build a refrigerator. And

201
00:14:06,803 --> 00:14:11,683
[JEFF] fail.

202
00:14:11,683 --> 00:14:12,483
[CYRUS] [curious] Einstein.

203
00:14:12,483 --> 00:14:18,483
[JEFF] But first, we've got a guy in debtors' prison with a pond and a motto.

204
00:11:33,762 --> 00:11:38,662
[JEFF] So Frederic Tudor gets out of debtors' prison. And the thing I find fascinating about

205
00:11:38,662 --> 00:11:43,562
[JEFF] the next fifteen years is that he doesn't change the idea. The idea was fine.

206
00:11:43,562 --> 00:11:48,462
[JEFF] What he'd learned, the hard way, is that he had two problems and neither one

207
00:11:48,462 --> 00:11:53,362
[JEFF] of them was cold.

208
00:11:53,362 --> 00:11:54,562
[CYRUS] Melt and demand.

209
00:11:54,562 --> 00:12:00,562
[JEFF] Right. The ice melts before you can sell it, and when it gets there, nobody

210
00:12:00,562 --> 00:12:06,562
[JEFF] knows what to do with it. And he attacks both. And the melt one is

211
00:12:06,562 --> 00:12:12,562
[JEFF] the piece I didn't really get until I read your notes. Why does hay fail?

212
00:12:12,562 --> 00:12:18,095
[CYRUS] [curious] Yeah, this one I loved, because it's so counterintuitive. The first cargo reportedly went

213
00:12:18,095 --> 00:12:23,628
[CYRUS] out packed in hay, and here's the thing — the insulation is never the stuff.

214
00:12:23,628 --> 00:12:29,162
[CYRUS] It's the air trapped in the stuff. Still air is a terrible conductor of heat,

215
00:12:29,162 --> 00:12:34,695
[CYRUS] which is what you want. But hay and straw compress, they mat down, they get

216
00:12:34,695 --> 00:12:40,228
[CYRUS] wet, and wet insulation conducts heat right through. Worse, the meltwater channels through it. So

217
00:12:40,228 --> 00:12:45,762
[CYRUS] you've built a wet blanket around your ice.

218
00:12:45,762 --> 00:12:46,562
[JEFF] And sawdust?

219
00:12:46,562 --> 00:12:52,322
[CYRUS] Sawdust — and the other one they used was spent tan bark, the waste from

220
00:12:52,322 --> 00:12:58,082
[CYRUS] tanneries — packs into a dense mass of tiny dead-air pockets, stays granular when it

221
00:12:58,082 --> 00:13:03,842
[CYRUS] gets damp, and drains. The water runs off instead of sitting against the ice. The

222
00:13:03,842 --> 00:13:09,602
[CYRUS] period manuals are very specific: eight inches of sawdust along the sides of the ship,

223
00:13:09,602 --> 00:13:15,362
[CYRUS] and the filling has to be dry. Dry. That's the whole rule.

224
00:13:15,362 --> 00:13:21,362
[JEFF] And here's the part I love. Sawdust is garbage. It's what comes out the back

225
00:13:21,362 --> 00:13:27,362
[JEFF] of every sawmill in New England. Tanneries were throwing the bark away. So one industry's

226
00:13:27,362 --> 00:13:33,362
[JEFF] disposal problem becomes another industry's most important input, at basically zero cost. Nobody planned that.

227
00:13:33,362 --> 00:13:35,362
[CYRUS] [dry] Free markets at work.

228
00:13:35,362 --> 00:13:41,362
[JEFF] It's like finding out your key input is somebody else's trash. And he does the

229
00:13:41,362 --> 00:13:47,362
[JEFF] same thing at the other end. Eighteen fifteen, he borrows twenty-one hundred dollars and builds

230
00:13:47,362 --> 00:13:53,362
[JEFF] an icehouse in Havana. Double walls packed with dry tan bark, drainage so the meltwater

231
00:13:53,362 --> 00:13:59,362
[JEFF] never pools, and you get in from the roof so you're not opening a door

232
00:13:59,362 --> 00:14:05,362
[JEFF] into warm air all day. He'd figured out you can't sell ice off a dock.

233
00:14:05,362 --> 00:14:10,362
[CYRUS] So now walk me through the plow, because I think the plow is the biggest

234
00:14:10,362 --> 00:14:15,362
[CYRUS] thing in this whole stretch and nobody's heard of it.

235
00:14:15,362 --> 00:14:21,122
[JEFF] [reflective] Yeah, so this is a guy named Nathaniel Wyeth, and he's the operations story

236
00:14:21,122 --> 00:14:26,882
[JEFF] behind the visionary. He grew up on the shore of Fresh Pond, in Cambridge, running

237
00:14:26,882 --> 00:14:32,642
[JEFF] his family's hotel there when Tudor hired him to manage the harvest. Tudor's diary has

238
00:14:32,642 --> 00:14:38,402
[JEFF] this wonderful entry from January eighteen twenty-eight — "I found Wyeth wandering about the woods

239
00:14:38,402 --> 00:14:44,162
[JEFF] at Fresh Pond in all the lonely perturbation of invention and contrivance."

240
00:14:44,162 --> 00:14:46,962
[CYRUS] The lonely perturbation of invention.

241
00:14:46,962 --> 00:14:50,162
[JEFF] Isn't that great? And what he'd invented, a few years before, was a horse-drawn ice

242
00:14:50,162 --> 00:14:53,362
[JEFF] cutter.

243
00:14:53,362 --> 00:14:59,133
[CYRUS] So picture what harvesting looked like before this. Men with hand saws, cutting irregular chunks

244
00:14:59,133 --> 00:15:04,904
[CYRUS] off a pond. Slow, and every block a different shape. Wyeth's tool is a plow

245
00:15:04,904 --> 00:15:10,676
[CYRUS] with two iron blades about twenty inches apart. A horse drags it across the pond

246
00:15:10,676 --> 00:15:16,447
[CYRUS] and it scores a groove; a guide arm rides in that groove while you cut

247
00:15:16,447 --> 00:15:22,219
[CYRUS] the next one, so every line is parallel. Then you do it again at right

248
00:15:22,219 --> 00:15:27,990
[CYRUS] angles. Now the whole pond is a grid of uniform blocks, and men with iron

249
00:15:27,990 --> 00:15:33,761
[CYRUS] bars just snap them off and float them to the icehouse.

250
00:15:33,761 --> 00:15:35,761
[JEFF] And the number on it.

251
00:15:35,761 --> 00:15:41,111
[CYRUS] It cut the cost of harvesting from about thirty cents a ton to ten. Same

252
00:15:41,111 --> 00:15:46,461
[CYRUS] man, same horse — three tons where he used to deliver one. But honestly the

253
00:15:46,461 --> 00:15:51,811
[CYRUS] cost is the smaller effect. The bigger one is standardization. A uniform block is a

254
00:15:51,811 --> 00:15:57,161
[CYRUS] unit. You can price it. You can stack it in a ship's hold with no

255
00:15:57,161 --> 00:16:02,511
[CYRUS] air gaps — and air gaps are melt. Thoreau, watching this at Walden, says they

256
00:16:02,511 --> 00:16:07,861
[CYRUS] stacked the blocks "as surely as so many barrels of flour." That's the moment ice

257
00:16:07,861 --> 00:16:13,211
[CYRUS] stops being a thing you scrape off a pond and becomes a commodity in the

258
00:16:13,211 --> 00:16:18,561
[CYRUS] literal sense.

259
00:16:18,561 --> 00:16:23,761
[JEFF] And Tudor, being Tudor, locks up the rights to Wyeth's tools. So he controls the

260
00:16:23,761 --> 00:16:28,961
[JEFF] harvest technology, the ships, and the icehouses at the far end.

261
00:16:28,961 --> 00:16:33,761
[CYRUS] [deadpan] The founder locks up the ops IP. Of course he does.

262
00:16:33,761 --> 00:16:37,361
[JEFF] Quick aside, because you're going to bring it up anyway — the opening scene of

263
00:16:37,361 --> 00:16:40,961
[JEFF] Frozen.

264
00:16:40,961 --> 00:16:46,161
[CYRUS] [deadpan] Which I have seen seventy times. The very first scene — before any princesses

265
00:16:46,161 --> 00:16:51,361
[CYRUS] — is a crew of men on a frozen lake, sawing blocks in rhythm and

266
00:16:51,361 --> 00:16:56,561
[CYRUS] loading them onto a sled. That's Wyeth's harvest. Disney opened a billion-dollar movie on the

267
00:16:56,561 --> 00:17:01,761
[CYRUS] pre-refrigeration economy and none of us noticed.

268
00:17:01,761 --> 00:17:07,761
[JEFF] Okay. So melt is solved. Now — demand. And this is where I think Tudor

269
00:17:07,761 --> 00:17:13,761
[JEFF] is genuinely ahead of his time, because he understands that his product isn't ice. His

270
00:17:13,761 --> 00:17:19,761
[JEFF] product is the habit of ice. He gets an icehouse into Charleston in eighteen twenty,

271
00:17:19,761 --> 00:17:25,761
[JEFF] New Orleans the year after. And in New Orleans he tells his agent to pick

272
00:17:25,761 --> 00:17:31,762
[JEFF] four saloons. Just four. Give them ice for free for a year, teach the bartenders

273
00:17:31,762 --> 00:17:37,762
[JEFF] how to store it, teach them cocktails — and let them get their customers hooked.

274
00:17:37,762 --> 00:17:42,162
[CYRUS] He's running a freemium model on bartenders in eighteen twenty-one.

275
00:17:42,162 --> 00:17:47,762
[JEFF] He is! And he explains the theory himself in a letter to his icehouse keeper

276
00:17:47,762 --> 00:17:53,362
[JEFF] in Martinique. Get this: "A man who has drank his drinks cold at the same

277
00:17:53,362 --> 00:17:58,962
[JEFF] expense for one week can never be presented with them warm again."

278
00:17:58,962 --> 00:18:03,095
[CYRUS] [deliberate] That's the entire consumer-behavior playbook in one sentence. Same price, better experience, one week,

279
00:18:03,095 --> 00:18:07,228
[CYRUS] and you can never go back, right? That's, like, every subscription business that has ever

280
00:18:07,228 --> 00:18:11,361
[CYRUS] existed.

281
00:18:11,361 --> 00:18:16,295
[JEFF] And he was right! By eighteen nineteen he's writing to a creditor that he has

282
00:18:16,295 --> 00:18:21,228
[JEFF] sold nearly thirty thousand dollars' worth of ice that season and that he is —

283
00:18:21,228 --> 00:18:26,161
[JEFF] his words — "inevitably and unavoidably rich."

284
00:18:26,161 --> 00:18:27,361
[CYRUS] [skeptical] Was he?

285
00:18:27,361 --> 00:18:32,828
[JEFF] On paper. He was in and out of debt his whole life. But the demand

286
00:18:32,828 --> 00:18:38,295
[JEFF] was real. And not everybody wanted it. So — here's the first person who looked

287
00:18:38,295 --> 00:18:43,761
[JEFF] at this stuff and said no. Tudor's agents get a cargo into New Orleans, which

288
00:18:43,761 --> 00:18:49,228
[JEFF] is a city that should want ice more than anywhere in America. Hot, humid, rich,

289
00:18:49,228 --> 00:18:54,695
[JEFF] and a yellow-fever town — buries people by the hundreds most summers. And the mayor

290
00:18:54,695 --> 00:19:00,162
[JEFF] takes one look at it.

291
00:19:00,162 --> 00:19:01,362
[CYRUS] [curious] The mayor?

292
00:19:01,362 --> 00:19:06,733
[JEFF] His name is Louis Philippe de Roffignac. A French aristocrat — his godparents were the

293
00:19:06,733 --> 00:19:12,104
[JEFF] Duke and Duchess of Orléans — who'd fled the Revolution and spent two terms trying

294
00:19:12,104 --> 00:19:17,476
[JEFF] to civilize New Orleans. Levees, paved streets, gas lamps. Serious reformer. And the story New

295
00:19:17,476 --> 00:19:22,847
[JEFF] Orleans has told about him for a hundred and fifty years is that he looked

296
00:19:22,847 --> 00:19:28,219
[JEFF] at this boatload of frozen water and decided it was — quote — "dangerous to

297
00:19:28,219 --> 00:19:33,590
[JEFF] the health of people inhabiting so warm a clime." And reportedly had the whole cargo

298
00:19:33,590 --> 00:19:38,962
[JEFF] dumped in the Mississippi.

299
00:19:38,962 --> 00:19:41,762
[CYRUS] [incredulous] He threw it in the river.

300
00:19:41,762 --> 00:19:47,428
[JEFF] Reportedly. Nobody's found a paper from the eighteen twenties that confirms it, so I'll say

301
00:19:47,428 --> 00:19:53,095
[JEFF] it the way the city says it. But here's why I like it even with

302
00:19:53,095 --> 00:19:58,762
[JEFF] the hedge: he wasn't a fool. Nobody in the eighteen twenties knew what a block

303
00:19:58,762 --> 00:20:04,428
[JEFF] of frozen water did to a body in August. In a city where fever killed

304
00:20:04,428 --> 00:20:10,095
[JEFF] thousands, a public-health instinct against a strange new thing off a ship from the north

305
00:20:10,095 --> 00:20:15,762
[JEFF] — that's not crazy. That's a reasonable man being wrong.

306
00:20:15,762 --> 00:20:16,562
[CYRUS] And then?

307
00:20:16,562 --> 00:20:22,362
[JEFF] And then within a couple of decades New Orleans is one of the biggest ice

308
00:20:22,362 --> 00:20:28,162
[JEFF] markets on the planet. And — the punchline — fifty years after he left office,

309
00:20:28,162 --> 00:20:33,962
[JEFF] a cocktail served over ice gets named after him. The Roffignac. Brandy, red syrup, soda,

310
00:20:33,962 --> 00:20:39,762
[JEFF] and a big glass of the thing he supposedly threw in the river.

311
00:20:39,762 --> 00:20:44,562
[CYRUS] That is a very New Orleans way to win an argument.

312
00:20:44,562 --> 00:20:48,828
[JEFF] [warmly] It really is. Okay — now the big one. Because by eighteen thirty-three Tudor

313
00:20:48,828 --> 00:20:53,095
[JEFF] has been at this twenty-seven years. And instead of the Caribbean, he loads a ship

314
00:20:53,095 --> 00:20:57,362
[JEFF] for India.

315
00:20:57,362 --> 00:21:01,162
[CYRUS] So I went deep on Calcutta, and I have to know if you found the

316
00:21:01,162 --> 00:21:04,962
[CYRUS] same thing I did.

317
00:21:04,962 --> 00:21:08,362
[JEFF] Please. This was my favorite piece of the whole story going in, so I want

318
00:21:08,362 --> 00:21:11,762
[JEFF] your version.

319
00:21:11,762 --> 00:21:16,895
[CYRUS] [deliberate] Okay. May twelfth, eighteen thirty-three. Tudor's diary: "Sailed this day the ship Tuscany, Captain

320
00:21:16,895 --> 00:21:22,028
[CYRUS] Littlefield, for Calcutta with one hundred eighty tons of ice — an experiment I have

321
00:21:22,028 --> 00:21:27,162
[CYRUS] been desirous of making for twenty years." Twenty years he'd wanted to try this. And

322
00:21:27,162 --> 00:21:32,295
[CYRUS] the route is Boston, down around the Cape of Good Hope, across the Indian Ocean,

323
00:21:32,295 --> 00:21:37,428
[CYRUS] up the Hooghly River to Calcutta. Sixteen thousand miles. Four months at sea. Across the

324
00:21:37,428 --> 00:21:42,562
[CYRUS] equator twice.

325
00:21:42,562 --> 00:21:48,962
[JEFF] Four months — and the ice is just sitting in the hold that whole time?

326
00:21:48,962 --> 00:21:54,628
[CYRUS] Packed in tan bark, stowed so the meltwater drains straight to the pump well, and

327
00:21:54,628 --> 00:22:00,295
[CYRUS] the crew pumps the whole way. And when it gets there in September — and

328
00:22:00,295 --> 00:22:05,962
[CYRUS] this is where I found the primary source, a London journal that reprinted the Calcutta

329
00:22:05,962 --> 00:22:11,628
[CYRUS] newspapers — the colonial government does two things immediately. It lets the ice be landed

330
00:22:11,628 --> 00:22:17,295
[CYRUS] after sunset, so it's not sitting in the sun. And it declares ice duty-free. Any

331
00:22:17,295 --> 00:22:22,962
[CYRUS] ice, from any country, under any flag, from then on.

332
00:22:22,962 --> 00:22:27,762
[JEFF] [genuinely surprised] Wait, they wrote a tariff exemption for ice? On arrival?

333
00:22:27,762 --> 00:22:33,282
[CYRUS] On arrival. And then the residents build him an icehouse. Near a landing on the

334
00:22:33,282 --> 00:22:38,802
[CYRUS] river — Brightman's Ghaut, north of the Strand Mills — and they raise the money

335
00:22:38,802 --> 00:22:44,322
[CYRUS] by public subscription. The customers build the infrastructure. And he sells the ice at four

336
00:22:44,322 --> 00:22:49,842
[CYRUS] annas a seer, which works out to about threepence a pound — half the price

337
00:22:49,842 --> 00:22:55,362
[CYRUS] of the local slush ice from the clay pans.

338
00:22:55,362 --> 00:23:00,962
[JEFF] [incredulous] He undercuts the locals. With ice that's been at sea for four months.

339
00:23:00,962 --> 00:23:04,962
[CYRUS] Right. Now — did you come across the crowd stories?

340
00:23:04,962 --> 00:23:08,362
[JEFF] I did, and I want to know how much you trust them. The hand, and

341
00:23:08,362 --> 00:23:11,762
[JEFF] the trees.

342
00:23:11,762 --> 00:23:17,590
[CYRUS] So here's the honest version. The stories that came back to Boston — repeated for

343
00:23:17,590 --> 00:23:23,419
[CYRUS] a hundred and fifty years without anybody finding the original — are that people on

344
00:23:23,419 --> 00:23:29,247
[CYRUS] the riverbank pressed their palms to the blocks, and one man pulled his hand away

345
00:23:29,247 --> 00:23:35,076
[CYRUS] and cried out that he'd been burned. And that somebody asked the captain whether ice

346
00:23:35,076 --> 00:23:40,904
[CYRUS] grew on trees in America. They're not in the newspaper accounts. But they're the kind

347
00:23:40,904 --> 00:23:46,733
[CYRUS] of thing that's true even if the specific man didn't exist, because think about what

348
00:23:46,733 --> 00:23:52,562
[CYRUS] it means to touch ice for the first time in your life.

349
00:23:52,562 --> 00:23:58,295
[JEFF] [awed] Yeah. That's the thing I can't get my head around. Not the shipping. That

350
00:23:58,295 --> 00:24:04,028
[JEFF] there were millions of people, in a city that size, for whom cold was simply

351
00:24:04,028 --> 00:24:09,762
[JEFF] not a category. You'd have no way to predict what it feels like.

352
00:24:09,762 --> 00:24:15,762
[CYRUS] Right. And the trade is real. The first cargo clears about ten thousand dollars in

353
00:24:15,762 --> 00:24:21,762
[CYRUS] profit. Regular sailings from eighteen thirty-five, icehouses in Madras and Bombay by the forties. And

354
00:24:21,762 --> 00:24:27,762
[CYRUS] it saves him — because in eighteen thirty-four, he'd blown himself up a second time.

355
00:24:27,762 --> 00:24:34,162
[JEFF] Yeah, this is the part that makes me think he'd fit in Silicon Valley.

356
00:24:34,162 --> 00:24:39,428
[CYRUS] Now you're making my argument. He speculated in coffee futures. And by eighteen thirty-four he

357
00:24:39,428 --> 00:24:44,695
[CYRUS] is two hundred and ten thousand dollars in debt — more than forty of his

358
00:24:44,695 --> 00:24:49,962
[CYRUS] first ships. And he doesn't default. He pays back every penny, with interest. The total

359
00:24:49,962 --> 00:24:55,228
[CYRUS] is two hundred eighty thousand, one hundred fifty-four dollars and fifty-nine cents. Takes him fifteen

360
00:24:55,228 --> 00:25:00,495
[CYRUS] years. He's finally clear in eighteen forty-nine, at sixty-five. And the thing that dug him

361
00:25:00,495 --> 00:25:05,762
[CYRUS] out was India.

362
00:25:05,762 --> 00:25:11,362
[JEFF] The fifty-nine cents is what sells me. That's a man who kept a ledger.

363
00:25:11,362 --> 00:25:17,362
[CYRUS] [amused] Kept a ledger, and kept score. By mid-century Boston is calling him the Ice

364
00:25:17,362 --> 00:25:23,362
[CYRUS] King, and Boston's ships carry something like a hundred and fifty thousand tons a year.

365
00:25:23,362 --> 00:25:29,247
[JEFF] So — I want to read you one thing, because the trade by now is

366
00:25:29,247 --> 00:25:35,133
[JEFF] big enough that it shows up in the most unlikely place. Thoreau. Walden. Winter of

367
00:25:35,133 --> 00:25:41,019
[JEFF] eighteen forty-six, forty-seven, Tudor's crews come to cut Walden Pond, and Thoreau watches from his

368
00:25:41,019 --> 00:25:46,904
[JEFF] cabin for sixteen days. "A hundred Irishmen, with Yankee overseers, came from Cambridge every day

369
00:25:46,904 --> 00:25:52,790
[JEFF] to get out the ice." A thousand tons a day. And then: "The sweltering inhabitants

370
00:25:52,790 --> 00:25:58,676
[JEFF] of Charleston and New Orleans, of Madras and Bombay and Calcutta, drink at my well.

371
00:25:58,676 --> 00:26:04,562
[JEFF] The pure Walden water is mingled with the sacred water of the Ganges."

372
00:26:04,562 --> 00:26:05,762
[CYRUS] That's beautiful.

373
00:26:05,762 --> 00:26:10,362
[JEFF] It is. And his larger point is right — the scale of this thing by

374
00:26:10,362 --> 00:26:14,962
[JEFF] the eighteen eighties is genuinely hard to picture.

375
00:26:14,962 --> 00:26:20,962
[CYRUS] [deliberate] So let me give you the denominators, because the numbers on this trade get

376
00:26:20,962 --> 00:26:26,962
[CYRUS] thrown around loosely. Eighteen forty-seven: three hundred and fifty-three ice-laden ships clear Boston Harbor in

377
00:26:26,962 --> 00:26:32,962
[CYRUS] a single year. By eighteen fifty-five, a period estimate has two million tons of ice

378
00:26:32,962 --> 00:26:38,962
[CYRUS] in storage in the United States. And then the Hudson Valley becomes an ice factory.

379
00:26:38,962 --> 00:26:44,962
[CYRUS] In the eighteen eighties, between New York City and Albany, there are about a hundred

380
00:26:44,962 --> 00:26:50,962
[CYRUS] and thirty-five big icehouses employing something like twenty thousand men every winter. New York City

381
00:26:50,962 --> 00:26:56,962
[CYRUS] alone is eating close to three million tons of ice a year by eighteen ninety.

382
00:26:56,962 --> 00:26:58,562
[JEFF] And the national number?

383
00:26:58,562 --> 00:27:03,628
[CYRUS] The national numbers everybody quotes — ninety thousand people and twenty-five thousand horses — come

384
00:27:03,628 --> 00:27:08,695
[CYRUS] from an eighteen ninety-three trade manual, and they include seasonal labor. It's the industry's own

385
00:27:08,695 --> 00:27:13,762
[CYRUS] estimate, not a census. But even discounted, it's a real industry. And there's a line

386
00:27:13,762 --> 00:27:18,828
[CYRUS] from mid-century that I love but can't fully source, so I'll say it the way

387
00:27:18,828 --> 00:27:23,895
[CYRUS] it was said: that Boston's ships carried more ice by weight than any cargo except

388
00:27:23,895 --> 00:27:28,962
[CYRUS] cotton.

389
00:27:28,962 --> 00:27:34,962
[JEFF] [amused] Frozen pond water, behind only cotton. I want that to be true so badly.

390
00:27:34,962 --> 00:27:40,762
[CYRUS] So did I, which is why I'm hedging it. And here's what that industry does

391
00:27:40,762 --> 00:27:46,562
[CYRUS] to daily life. It teaches whole cities to expect cold. Every day. By the eighteen

392
00:27:46,562 --> 00:27:52,362
[CYRUS] nineties there's a guy — the iceman — who comes down your street with a

393
00:27:52,362 --> 00:27:58,162
[CYRUS] wagon and a wet sack over his shoulder. You've got a square card in your

394
00:27:58,162 --> 00:28:03,962
[CYRUS] window with twenty-five, fifty, seventy-five, and a hundred printed on the corners, and whichever number

395
00:28:03,962 --> 00:28:09,762
[CYRUS] you turn upright is how many pounds you want. He reads it from the street,

396
00:28:09,762 --> 00:28:15,562
[CYRUS] cuts the block, slides it into the zinc-lined icebox in your kitchen. And if you

397
00:28:15,562 --> 00:28:21,362
[CYRUS] forget to empty the drip pan underneath, you flood the floor.

398
00:28:21,362 --> 00:28:24,562
[JEFF] [amused] Every kid in America had that chore.

399
00:28:24,562 --> 00:28:30,162
[CYRUS] That's the daily rhythm of an American household for fifty years. Cocktails on the rocks.

400
00:28:30,162 --> 00:28:35,762
[CYRUS] Ice cream as a business. Lager brewed all summer. So — a huge industry, a

401
00:28:35,762 --> 00:28:41,362
[CYRUS] daily habit, and a whole country's worth of capital in ponds, ships, and icehouses. And

402
00:28:41,362 --> 00:28:46,962
[CYRUS] that's exactly the moment somebody shows up with a better idea.

403
00:28:46,962 --> 00:28:52,895
[JEFF] [reflective] Yeah. And this is the story I think about most from this whole stretch.

404
00:28:52,895 --> 00:28:58,828
[JEFF] So — that brings us to a doctor in Florida named John Gorrie. Apalachicola, a

405
00:28:58,828 --> 00:29:04,762
[JEFF] big cotton port on the Gulf. Gorrie is the town's fever doctor. Yellow fever. And

406
00:29:04,762 --> 00:29:10,695
[JEFF] he's working from the medical theory of the day, which is that heat and bad

407
00:29:10,695 --> 00:29:16,628
[JEFF] air spread disease. So his treatment is to cool the sickroom — he hangs pans

408
00:29:16,628 --> 00:29:22,562
[JEFF] of ice above the patients' beds and lets the air flow down over them.

409
00:29:22,562 --> 00:29:24,562
[CYRUS] Ice he's getting from —

410
00:29:24,562 --> 00:29:29,962
[JEFF] From Boston. Tudor's ice. And in summer, when the ships don't come, there isn't any.

411
00:29:29,962 --> 00:29:35,362
[JEFF] So this doctor, with fever patients dying in the heat, decides he's going to build

412
00:29:35,362 --> 00:29:40,762
[JEFF] a machine that makes it. Eighteen forty-four, he makes ice mechanically. Eighteen forty-five he gives

413
00:29:40,762 --> 00:29:46,162
[JEFF] up medicine to work on the machine full time.

414
00:29:46,162 --> 00:29:52,162
[CYRUS] [curious] And it actually made ice? What was he doing in there — one line.

415
00:29:52,162 --> 00:29:57,842
[JEFF] One line: compress air, it gets hot, dump the heat, let the air expand, it

416
00:29:57,842 --> 00:30:03,522
[JEFF] gets cold, run that cold air over brine and freeze water. It's not the cycle

417
00:30:03,522 --> 00:30:09,202
[JEFF] that eventually wins. But it makes ice. And here's the scene I love. July fourteenth,

418
00:30:09,202 --> 00:30:14,882
[JEFF] eighteen fifty. Bastille Day. There's a French cotton buyer in town named Rosan, who's also

419
00:30:14,882 --> 00:30:20,562
[JEFF] the French consul, and he's throwing a dinner for the Northern cotton brokers at the

420
00:30:20,562 --> 00:30:26,242
[JEFF] Mansion House — the biggest hotel in Florida. The summer ice shipment hasn't come. And

421
00:30:26,242 --> 00:30:31,922
[JEFF] when a guest wishes out loud for ice for his wine, Rosan, who has seen

422
00:30:31,922 --> 00:30:37,602
[JEFF] the machine, bets a basket of champagne he can produce it. And at the toast

423
00:30:37,602 --> 00:30:43,282
[JEFF] to the Republic, the waiters walk in carrying champagne buckets full of ice, made in

424
00:30:43,282 --> 00:30:48,962
[JEFF] a back room by John Gorrie's machine.

425
00:30:48,962 --> 00:30:51,762
[CYRUS] That is a great party trick.

426
00:30:51,762 --> 00:30:56,562
[JEFF] In Florida. In July. In eighteen fifty. A friend remembered Gorrie being "excited and nervous,"

427
00:30:56,562 --> 00:31:01,362
[JEFF] which — of course he was. The next year, May sixth, eighteen fifty-one, he gets

428
00:31:01,362 --> 00:31:06,162
[JEFF] a United States patent for it.

429
00:31:06,162 --> 00:31:12,162
[CYRUS] And this is the detail I flagged — the patent number. Eight thousand and eighty.

430
00:31:12,162 --> 00:31:18,162
[CYRUS] That is such a young patent number. There are more than eleven million now. Also

431
00:31:18,162 --> 00:31:24,162
[CYRUS] — the patent lists him as "John Gorrie, of New Orleans," which tells you something.

432
00:31:24,162 --> 00:31:29,228
[JEFF] It tells you he'd gone to New Orleans to raise money. And he finds it.

433
00:31:29,228 --> 00:31:34,295
[JEFF] He sells a half interest to a Boston investor who pledges enough capital to build

434
00:31:34,295 --> 00:31:39,362
[JEFF] a full-scale plant. And then the investor dies.

435
00:31:39,362 --> 00:31:40,562
[CYRUS] [exhales] Oh no.

436
00:31:40,562 --> 00:31:46,562
[JEFF] The investor dies. And we don't even know his name — the histories say "a

437
00:31:46,562 --> 00:31:52,562
[JEFF] man from Boston, whose name is not known." And after that, nobody will fund him.

438
00:31:52,562 --> 00:31:58,562
[JEFF] Gorrie becomes convinced the natural-ice interests are running a press campaign against him — he

439
00:31:58,562 --> 00:32:04,562
[JEFF] thought Tudor himself was behind it. There's no document that proves that. But there is

440
00:32:04,562 --> 00:32:10,562
[JEFF] a New York paper that sneered — the line has been quoted for a century,

441
00:32:10,562 --> 00:32:16,562
[JEFF] even if nobody's found the clipping — that "there is a crank down in Apalachicola,

442
00:32:16,562 --> 00:32:22,562
[JEFF] Florida, that thinks he can make ice by his machine as good as God Almighty."

443
00:32:22,562 --> 00:32:24,962
[CYRUS] [scoffs] And the crank was right.

444
00:32:24,962 --> 00:32:29,762
[JEFF] And he dies in eighteen fifty-five. Broke. In seclusion. His health gone. The official description

445
00:32:29,762 --> 00:32:34,562
[JEFF] at the Capitol says "humiliated by criticism, financially ruined, and his health broken." Florida later

446
00:32:34,562 --> 00:32:39,362
[JEFF] gave him a marble statue — it stands in the U-S Capitol today, in the

447
00:32:39,362 --> 00:32:44,162
[JEFF] Hall of Columns.

448
00:32:44,162 --> 00:32:49,990
[CYRUS] [deliberate] So here's the structural read, because I think it's the important one. Gorrie didn't

449
00:32:49,990 --> 00:32:55,819
[CYRUS] fail because the machine didn't work. It worked. He failed because the incumbent was real.

450
00:32:55,819 --> 00:33:01,647
[CYRUS] In eighteen fifty-five there are two million tons of ice in storage in this country,

451
00:33:01,647 --> 00:33:07,476
[CYRUS] three hundred and fifty ships a year out of Boston, a workforce, wharf rights, icehouses

452
00:33:07,476 --> 00:33:13,304
[CYRUS] in twenty ports. Every dollar of that is a reason not to fund a guy

453
00:33:13,304 --> 00:33:19,133
[CYRUS] who makes ice in a back room. The technology that wins isn't always the one

454
00:33:19,133 --> 00:33:24,962
[CYRUS] that works. It's the one with the existing capital on its side.

455
00:33:24,962 --> 00:33:30,562
[JEFF] He was right — just forty years early. And he died ruined for it.

456
00:33:30,562 --> 00:33:36,562
[CYRUS] And I found the receipt on the "forty years." In June of eighteen ninety, the

457
00:33:36,562 --> 00:33:42,562
[CYRUS] Savannah newspaper lists the city's machine-ice makers. One of them is called the Gorrie Ice

458
00:33:42,562 --> 00:33:48,562
[CYRUS] Company. Thirty-five years after he died broke, Southern ice factories are naming themselves after him.

459
00:33:48,562 --> 00:33:50,562
[JEFF] That's a hard one.

460
00:33:50,562 --> 00:33:55,362
[CYRUS] It is. And by the way — the ice trade didn't have to conspire against

461
00:33:55,362 --> 00:34:00,162
[CYRUS] him. It just had to exist. Tudor solved the problem so well that his solution

462
00:34:00,162 --> 00:34:04,962
[CYRUS] built the capital that kept the better solution out for a generation. Nobody had to

463
00:34:04,962 --> 00:34:09,762
[CYRUS] be a villain.

464
00:34:09,762 --> 00:34:13,762
[JEFF] Although — once you have an industry that big —

465
00:34:13,762 --> 00:34:17,762
[CYRUS] — it attracts a different kind of capitalist. Yeah. Who?

466
00:34:17,762 --> 00:34:23,112
[JEFF] [amused] His name is Charles W. Morse. He's from Bath, Maine — his family shipped

467
00:34:23,112 --> 00:34:28,462
[JEFF] ice and lumber — and in the eighteen nineties he starts rolling up New York's

468
00:34:28,462 --> 00:34:33,812
[JEFF] ice supply. And in eighteen ninety-nine he forms the American Ice Company — capitalized at

469
00:34:33,812 --> 00:34:39,162
[JEFF] sixty million dollars, which the papers at the time called grossly overcapitalized. And the key

470
00:34:39,162 --> 00:34:44,512
[JEFF] thing he controls isn't the ice. It's the piers. His company is essentially the only

471
00:34:44,512 --> 00:34:49,862
[JEFF] one allowed to land ice at New York's docks. An independent dealer up in Albany

472
00:34:49,862 --> 00:34:55,212
[JEFF] told the Tribune, "He can ruin any independent dealer in this town and we all

473
00:34:55,212 --> 00:35:00,562
[JEFF] know it."

474
00:35:00,562 --> 00:35:02,562
[CYRUS] So he owns the chokepoint.

475
00:35:02,562 --> 00:35:07,922
[JEFF] And then — May first, nineteen hundred. Right as the heat is coming. The American

476
00:35:07,922 --> 00:35:13,282
[JEFF] Ice Company notifies its customers that the price of ice for the season will be

477
00:35:13,282 --> 00:35:18,642
[JEFF] sixty cents per hundred pounds. It had been twenty-five, maybe thirty. So the price doubles.

478
00:35:18,642 --> 00:35:24,002
[JEFF] And — this is the detail that gives it moral weight — the company announces

479
00:35:24,002 --> 00:35:29,362
[JEFF] that no small pieces will be sold.

480
00:35:29,362 --> 00:35:30,562
[CYRUS] [skeptical] Meaning what?

481
00:35:30,562 --> 00:35:35,695
[JEFF] Meaning the five-cent piece and the ten-cent piece. Which is the only ice a tenement

482
00:35:35,695 --> 00:35:40,828
[JEFF] family can buy. No room for a block, no money for one. They buy a

483
00:35:40,828 --> 00:35:45,962
[JEFF] nickel's worth, every day, and it goes in a pan next to the baby's milk

484
00:35:45,962 --> 00:35:51,095
[JEFF] in a room with no cross-breeze in July. So abolishing the nickel piece — that

485
00:35:51,095 --> 00:35:56,228
[JEFF] is the price increase for the poor, specifically. Everybody else just pays more. They get

486
00:35:56,228 --> 00:36:01,362
[JEFF] cut off.

487
00:36:01,362 --> 00:36:06,062
[CYRUS] [deliberate] Okay, and my first reaction reading this was, that's surge pricing. Demand spikes, price

488
00:36:06,062 --> 00:36:10,762
[CYRUS] doubles — that's just Uber on a rainy night. But honestly, when I read the

489
00:36:10,762 --> 00:36:15,462
[CYRUS] Tribune reporting, it's worse than surge pricing. Surge pricing is one price that goes up

490
00:36:15,462 --> 00:36:20,162
[CYRUS] for everyone.

491
00:36:20,162 --> 00:36:25,990
[JEFF] Right — so the Tribune goes out in late June and finds the Trust selling

492
00:36:25,990 --> 00:36:31,819
[JEFF] ice at, quote, "as many prices as the fakirs in Fulton Street." Sixty-five cents to

493
00:36:31,819 --> 00:36:37,647
[JEFF] the rich. As low as thirty-three cents in the tenements, once the heat is on

494
00:36:37,647 --> 00:36:43,476
[JEFF] them. One ice cart in Harlem sold at four different prices along the length of

495
00:36:43,476 --> 00:36:49,304
[JEFF] a single block. And then — the kicker — the Trust bid to supply the

496
00:36:49,304 --> 00:36:55,133
[JEFF] federal buildings downtown at twenty-five cents a hundred. So they could sell at twenty-five and

497
00:36:55,133 --> 00:37:00,962
[JEFF] make money. The paper's line is, "the trust is practically the supply."

498
00:37:00,962 --> 00:37:05,495
[CYRUS] So that's not supply and demand. That's price discrimination by a monopolist who owns the

499
00:37:05,495 --> 00:37:10,028
[CYRUS] piers. Whatever each customer will bear, block by block. You can only do that when

500
00:37:10,028 --> 00:37:14,562
[CYRUS] you are the supply.

501
00:37:14,562 --> 00:37:20,322
[JEFF] And the press goes to war. Hearst's Journal, Pulitzer's World, the Tribune, the Times. The

502
00:37:20,322 --> 00:37:26,082
[JEFF] World goes to court and gets a judge — Justice Gaynor, in Brooklyn — to

503
00:37:26,082 --> 00:37:31,842
[JEFF] open an inquiry, and hires the lawyer who prosecuted Boss Tweed to do the questioning.

504
00:37:31,842 --> 00:37:37,602
[JEFF] And in the courtroom, alongside Morse and the ice barons, are the city's dock commissioners.

505
00:37:37,602 --> 00:37:43,362
[JEFF] Because the piers were the monopoly. And the Mayor of New York.

506
00:37:43,362 --> 00:37:45,762
[CYRUS] [incredulous] The mayor's in the room?

507
00:37:45,762 --> 00:37:51,028
[JEFF] The mayor is on the stand. June ninth, nineteen hundred. Robert Van Wyck, Tammany's man,

508
00:37:51,028 --> 00:37:56,295
[JEFF] first mayor of the consolidated city. And under oath he admits he owns thousands of

509
00:37:56,295 --> 00:38:01,562
[JEFF] shares of American Ice stock — worth around six hundred and eighty thousand dollars at

510
00:38:01,562 --> 00:38:06,828
[JEFF] par. On a salary of fifteen thousand a year. He swears he borrowed the money

511
00:38:06,828 --> 00:38:12,095
[JEFF] from a bank — and the bank's president gets up and swears he never lent

512
00:38:12,095 --> 00:38:17,362
[JEFF] the mayor a cent.

513
00:38:17,362 --> 00:38:19,762
[CYRUS] Under oath. Both of them.

514
00:38:19,762 --> 00:38:24,895
[JEFF] Which tells you how nervous the room was. And Richard Croker, the boss of Tammany

515
00:38:24,895 --> 00:38:30,028
[JEFF] Hall, whose family holds a pile of the stock in his wife's name, writes a

516
00:38:30,028 --> 00:38:35,162
[JEFF] public letter that same week: "If it were in my power or in the power

517
00:38:35,162 --> 00:38:40,295
[JEFF] of Tammany Hall to give the poor ice for nothing we would do so." The

518
00:38:40,295 --> 00:38:45,428
[JEFF] paper prints it right above the list of his family's shares. And then he sails

519
00:38:45,428 --> 00:38:50,562
[JEFF] for England.

520
00:38:50,562 --> 00:38:52,162
[CYRUS] [deadpan] As one does.

521
00:38:52,162 --> 00:38:58,095
[JEFF] And here's the part I find genuinely interesting. Charges go to the governor to remove

522
00:38:58,095 --> 00:39:04,028
[JEFF] the mayor. And the governor is Theodore Roosevelt — the reformer. And in October, Roosevelt

523
00:39:04,028 --> 00:39:09,962
[JEFF] dismisses the charges, because owning the stock, by itself, wasn't legally sufficient cause. The scandal

524
00:39:09,962 --> 00:39:15,895
[JEFF] had no legal handle. It got resolved by voters instead: Van Wyck doesn't run again,

525
00:39:15,895 --> 00:39:21,828
[JEFF] and the next year a reform ticket under Seth Low takes City Hall. The Ice

526
00:39:21,828 --> 00:39:27,762
[JEFF] Trust damaged Tammany badly. Though Tammany came back two years after that.

527
00:39:27,762 --> 00:39:30,162
[CYRUS] They always come back. And Morse?

528
00:39:30,162 --> 00:39:35,362
[JEFF] Morse walks away from ice with something like twelve million dollars, eventually gets fifteen years

529
00:39:35,362 --> 00:39:40,562
[JEFF] for bank fraud — and gets pardoned by President Taft after Army doctors diagnose a

530
00:39:40,562 --> 00:39:45,762
[JEFF] fatal kidney disease he had faked by drinking soapsuds.

531
00:39:45,762 --> 00:39:49,362
[CYRUS] [incredulous] He faked a fatal disease by drinking soap?

532
00:39:49,362 --> 00:39:55,162
[JEFF] And lived another twenty years. And the nickel piece? It came back within a couple

533
00:39:55,162 --> 00:40:00,962
[JEFF] of weeks, under the press pressure — but only at the depots, and only if

534
00:40:00,962 --> 00:40:06,762
[JEFF] you showed a certificate from a charity proving you were poor. The Tribune called it

535
00:40:06,762 --> 00:40:12,562
[JEFF] "a miserable subterfuge, almost fiendish in the forfeiture of self-respect which it inflicts."

536
00:40:12,562 --> 00:40:16,695
[CYRUS] So — step back. What New York just learned in one summer is that its

537
00:40:16,695 --> 00:40:20,828
[CYRUS] cold comes from two places. It comes from the weather. And it comes from Tammany

538
00:40:20,828 --> 00:40:24,962
[CYRUS] Hall.

539
00:40:24,962 --> 00:40:30,095
[JEFF] [reflective] And both of those had been on display ten years earlier, if anybody had

540
00:40:30,095 --> 00:40:35,228
[JEFF] been looking. Because the winter of eighteen eighty-nine, ninety, was one of the warmest on

541
00:40:35,228 --> 00:40:40,362
[JEFF] record. The Hudson barely froze. January seventh, eighteen ninety, the Jersey City paper's front page:

542
00:40:40,362 --> 00:40:45,495
[JEFF] "An Ice Famine at Hand. None in the storage houses to meet present demands. It

543
00:40:45,495 --> 00:40:50,628
[JEFF] may rise to twenty dollars per ton. Agents are scouring the country and Canada for

544
00:40:50,628 --> 00:40:55,762
[JEFF] a supply."

545
00:40:55,762 --> 00:41:01,247
[CYRUS] And here's the thing nobody in New York was paying attention to. Same year —

546
00:41:01,247 --> 00:41:06,733
[CYRUS] June, eighteen ninety — the Savannah paper runs a story. Ice is wholesaling in New

547
00:41:06,733 --> 00:41:12,219
[CYRUS] York at ten dollars a ton. Cincinnati, twenty. Savannah? Five to seven-fifty. And the editor

548
00:41:12,219 --> 00:41:17,704
[CYRUS] is very pleased with himself about it: "It is some satisfaction to know, when the

549
00:41:17,704 --> 00:41:23,190
[CYRUS] mercury is up in the nineties, that ice is selling lower in Savannah than in

550
00:41:23,190 --> 00:41:28,676
[CYRUS] the north and west." And then the headline — "The natural ice trade gone and

551
00:41:28,676 --> 00:41:34,162
[CYRUS] machine ice the entire supply now."

552
00:41:34,162 --> 00:41:40,162
[JEFF] [genuinely surprised] Wait — the South is already fully on machine ice in eighteen ninety?

553
00:41:40,162 --> 00:41:45,362
[CYRUS] Fully. Every corner grocery in Savannah sells it. And that's the natural experiment. Same country,

554
00:41:45,362 --> 00:41:50,562
[CYRUS] same month, a two-to-four-times price gap — and the entire difference is whether you were

555
00:41:50,562 --> 00:41:55,762
[CYRUS] making cold or harvesting it. The thing Gorrie died trying to sell is, by eighteen

556
00:41:55,762 --> 00:42:00,962
[CYRUS] ninety, ordinary, everywhere it never snowed. And a machine in Munich had been running a

557
00:42:00,962 --> 00:42:06,162
[CYRUS] brewery's cellars since eighteen seventy-six.

558
00:42:06,162 --> 00:42:10,828
[JEFF] So the next part of this isn't about cutting ice at all. It's about making

559
00:42:10,828 --> 00:42:15,495
[JEFF] it. And then — harder — moving it. Getting cold from a machine to a

560
00:42:15,495 --> 00:42:20,162
[JEFF] plate a thousand miles away.

561
00:32:04,705 --> 00:32:10,305
[JEFF] So here's what surprised me most about this part. The machine in Munich in eighteen

562
00:32:10,305 --> 00:32:15,905
[JEFF] seventy-six — that's not the beginning of the story. That's the end of forty years

563
00:32:15,905 --> 00:32:21,505
[JEFF] of people getting the physics completely right and the business completely wrong.

564
00:32:21,505 --> 00:32:24,305
[CYRUS] [curious] How far back does it go?

565
00:32:24,305 --> 00:32:29,705
[JEFF] Seventeen forty-eight. Glasgow. A doctor named William Cullen puts ether in a jar, pumps the

566
00:32:29,705 --> 00:32:35,105
[JEFF] air out so it boils at a low temperature, and the ether pulls heat out

567
00:32:35,105 --> 00:32:40,505
[JEFF] of everything around it and makes a little ice. He publishes, he demonstrates it in

568
00:32:40,505 --> 00:32:45,905
[JEFF] Edinburgh, and — nothing. No commercial use at all.

569
00:32:45,905 --> 00:32:49,105
[CYRUS] Because nobody had a problem worth the machine.

570
00:32:49,105 --> 00:32:54,590
[JEFF] Right. Then eighteen oh five, an American named Oliver Evans draws a closed cycle on

571
00:32:54,590 --> 00:33:00,076
[JEFF] paper — never builds it. And then eighteen thirty-four, a guy named Jacob Perkins actually

572
00:33:00,076 --> 00:33:05,562
[JEFF] builds one. Perkins is an American banknote engraver who moved to London — his firm

573
00:33:05,562 --> 00:33:11,047
[JEFF] printed the Penny Black, the world's first postage stamp — and on August fourteenth, eighteen

574
00:33:11,047 --> 00:33:16,533
[JEFF] thirty-four, he gets the first patent for what we'd now call the vapor-compression cycle. The

575
00:33:16,533 --> 00:33:22,019
[JEFF] one in your kitchen. An engineer builds it and demonstrates it the next year. And

576
00:33:22,019 --> 00:33:27,505
[JEFF] it never sells a single unit.

577
00:33:27,505 --> 00:33:28,705
[CYRUS] [skeptical] Why not?

578
00:33:28,705 --> 00:33:34,705
[JEFF] Because London can get perfectly good ice by ship — first from a pond in

579
00:33:34,705 --> 00:33:40,705
[JEFF] Massachusetts, then from Norway. And there's a distinction IBM used to draw between invention and

580
00:33:40,705 --> 00:33:46,705
[JEFF] innovation. Invention is building the new thing. Innovation is making it scalable, practical, and commercially

581
00:33:46,705 --> 00:33:52,705
[JEFF] viable. Perkins invented it. He did not innovate it. And then Gorrie, who we just

582
00:33:52,705 --> 00:33:58,705
[JEFF] talked about, dies broke in eighteen fifty-five. And then comes the one that gets me.

583
00:33:58,705 --> 00:33:59,505
[CYRUS] [curious] Harrison?

584
00:33:59,505 --> 00:34:04,865
[JEFF] James Harrison. A Scottish-born printer who emigrates to Australia and founds a newspaper in Geelong,

585
00:34:04,865 --> 00:34:10,225
[JEFF] near Melbourne — and who reportedly got the idea cleaning type with ether and noticing

586
00:34:10,225 --> 00:34:15,585
[JEFF] the metal went cold. By eighteen fifty-four he has a commercial ice plant — the

587
00:34:15,585 --> 00:34:20,945
[JEFF] world's first — and a brewery in Bendigo is running on it. He reportedly blew

588
00:34:20,945 --> 00:34:26,305
[JEFF] himself up at least twice doing this.

589
00:34:26,305 --> 00:34:28,305
[CYRUS] Of course he did.

590
00:34:28,305 --> 00:34:33,790
[JEFF] [dry] Occupational hazard. And in January eighteen seventy-three — by this point he's editor of

591
00:34:33,790 --> 00:34:39,276
[JEFF] the Melbourne Age — he wins the gold medal at the Melbourne Exhibition for preserving

592
00:34:39,276 --> 00:34:44,762
[JEFF] meat by freezing. The papers say the process costs "a farthing a pound." Australia has

593
00:34:44,762 --> 00:34:50,247
[JEFF] millions of sheep, Britain has millions of hungry people, and now you can freeze one

594
00:34:50,247 --> 00:34:55,733
[JEFF] and ship it to the other. So that July, he bets everything on it. He

595
00:34:55,733 --> 00:35:01,219
[JEFF] loads twenty-odd tons of frozen beef and mutton onto a ship called the Norfolk, bound

596
00:35:01,219 --> 00:35:06,705
[JEFF] for London. And he's on board.

597
00:35:06,705 --> 00:35:09,505
[CYRUS] [genuinely surprised] Wait — with a machine?

598
00:35:09,505 --> 00:35:15,438
[JEFF] No. That's the thing. He didn't have the money for a machine, so he used

599
00:35:15,438 --> 00:35:21,371
[JEFF] ice-and-salt tanks — pack the meat in cold rooms with a freezing mixture and hope

600
00:35:21,371 --> 00:35:27,305
[JEFF] it lasts. And the tanks were badly made. The brine leaked, the ice melted faster

601
00:35:27,305 --> 00:35:33,238
[JEFF] than he'd calculated, and on the thirty-fourth day out most of the meat had to

602
00:35:33,238 --> 00:35:39,171
[JEFF] be thrown into the sea. They kept one ton, hoping to land a sample. Off

603
00:35:39,171 --> 00:35:45,105
[JEFF] the Azores, the last of the ice gave out, and that went overboard too.

604
00:35:45,105 --> 00:35:48,305
[CYRUS] [exhales] He watched it go over the side.

605
00:35:48,305 --> 00:35:53,425
[JEFF] Standing on the deck. Gold medal in January, meat in the ocean by September. It

606
00:35:53,425 --> 00:35:58,545
[JEFF] ruins him, and it ruins public confidence in frozen meat for years. His own explanation

607
00:35:58,545 --> 00:36:03,665
[JEFF] was "too much hurry in the preparations." And the lesson the trade took from it

608
00:36:03,665 --> 00:36:08,785
[JEFF] — which turns out to be exactly right — is that the machine has to

609
00:36:08,785 --> 00:36:13,905
[JEFF] travel with the meat.

610
00:36:13,905 --> 00:36:17,905
[CYRUS] And meanwhile there's a whole other path being built. Carré.

611
00:36:17,905 --> 00:36:23,505
[JEFF] Yeah — Ferdinand Carré, in France, eighteen fifty-nine, builds what's called an absorption machine. Ammonia

612
00:36:23,505 --> 00:36:29,105
[JEFF] dissolved in water, driven by heat instead of a compressor. Hold onto that one, because

613
00:36:29,105 --> 00:36:34,705
[JEFF] it comes back in a big way. And the list goes on. Twining in New

614
00:36:34,705 --> 00:36:40,305
[JEFF] Haven, who got the principle adopted everywhere and never made a dime. Tellier in France,

615
00:36:40,305 --> 00:36:45,905
[JEFF] who shipped meat from Argentina in eighteen seventy-six and died poor. Pictet in Geneva. There

616
00:36:45,905 --> 00:36:51,505
[JEFF] are probably ten more whose names we don't know.

617
00:36:51,505 --> 00:36:56,785
[CYRUS] [deliberate] And here's what I think is the actual point of that list. None of

618
00:36:56,785 --> 00:37:02,065
[CYRUS] them stopped because the last one failed. Every one of these guys could see the

619
00:37:02,065 --> 00:37:07,345
[CYRUS] same prize — a market that was already paying real money for Boston ice, sitting

620
00:37:07,345 --> 00:37:12,625
[CYRUS] there. There's a market, there's a problem, and there's a return waiting for whoever cracks

621
00:37:12,625 --> 00:37:17,905
[CYRUS] it. So the next person tries.

622
00:37:17,905 --> 00:37:21,105
[JEFF] That's capitalism doing what it's supposed to do.

623
00:37:21,105 --> 00:37:24,705
[CYRUS] A hundred percent. And the first place it actually gets cracked at scale is not

624
00:37:24,705 --> 00:37:28,305
[CYRUS] where you'd expect.

625
00:37:28,305 --> 00:37:34,247
[JEFF] No. It's New Orleans, and the reason is a war. So — the Civil War.

626
00:37:34,247 --> 00:37:40,190
[JEFF] The Union blockades the Southern ports. And the thing nobody thinks about is that the

627
00:37:40,190 --> 00:37:46,133
[JEFF] South had been buying Boston ice for forty years. Every hospital, every saloon in New

628
00:37:46,133 --> 00:37:52,076
[JEFF] Orleans. And the blockade cuts it off. So in eighteen sixty-three, two French entrepreneurs —

629
00:37:52,076 --> 00:37:58,019
[JEFF] Bujac and Girardey — run Carré's ammonia machines through the Union blockade into New Orleans.

630
00:37:58,019 --> 00:38:03,962
[JEFF] The Times-Picayune goes to look at their test facility in the summer of eighteen sixty-four

631
00:38:03,962 --> 00:38:09,905
[JEFF] and writes: "This was ice made with the thermometer at ninety-three in the shade."

632
00:38:09,905 --> 00:38:13,905
[CYRUS] [incredulous] In New Orleans. In the middle of a war.

633
00:38:13,905 --> 00:38:19,447
[JEFF] One of the machines goes to a Confederate hospital in Augusta for fever patients —

634
00:38:19,447 --> 00:38:24,990
[JEFF] same instinct as Gorrie. After the war, a former Confederate engineer named Daniel Holden buys

635
00:38:24,990 --> 00:38:30,533
[JEFF] it, hauls it to San Antonio, and starts making the ice with distilled water. Which

636
00:38:30,533 --> 00:38:36,076
[JEFF] means the ice is clear. For the first time, machine ice looks like ice you'd

637
00:38:36,076 --> 00:38:41,619
[JEFF] want in your drink. And on May eighteenth, eighteen sixty-eight, the Louisiana Ice Manufacturing Company

638
00:38:41,619 --> 00:38:47,162
[JEFF] opens on Tchoupitoulas Street in New Orleans with six ten-ton machines. It's been called the

639
00:38:47,162 --> 00:38:52,705
[JEFF] first industrial ice plant in the world.

640
00:38:52,705 --> 00:38:58,105
[CYRUS] [deliberate] So, like, think about what that means structurally. The American machine-ice industry is a

641
00:38:58,105 --> 00:39:03,505
[CYRUS] war baby. Not a eureka moment — a naval blockade. The South had no pond

642
00:39:03,505 --> 00:39:08,905
[CYRUS] ice, so no local incumbent, and the blockade removed the imported one. A market with

643
00:39:08,905 --> 00:39:14,305
[CYRUS] a real problem and nobody defending the old way, right? Of course it adopted the

644
00:39:14,305 --> 00:39:19,705
[CYRUS] machine twenty years before the North did. The South got the future first because it

645
00:39:19,705 --> 00:39:25,105
[CYRUS] got cut off from the past.

646
00:39:25,105 --> 00:39:28,305
[JEFF] [reflective] Which is a strange kind of luck.

647
00:39:28,305 --> 00:39:33,805
[CYRUS] It's the only kind there is in these stories. Okay — so now the machine

648
00:39:33,805 --> 00:39:39,305
[CYRUS] that actually becomes the machine. Because everything so far — Perkins, Harrison, Carré — those

649
00:39:39,305 --> 00:39:44,805
[CYRUS] worked. What they didn't do was run all summer without breaking. And the guy who

650
00:39:44,805 --> 00:39:50,305
[CYRUS] solves that is a professor in Munich. Carl von Linde.

651
00:39:50,305 --> 00:39:52,305
[JEFF] And the customer is beer.

652
00:39:52,305 --> 00:39:57,505
[CYRUS] [amused] Which is one of my favorite details in this whole story. Lager has to

653
00:39:57,505 --> 00:40:02,705
[CYRUS] ferment and be stored cold. And in Bavaria there was a law — from fifteen

654
00:40:02,705 --> 00:40:07,905
[CYRUS] fifty-three until eighteen fifty, three hundred years — that made it illegal to brew beer

655
00:40:07,905 --> 00:40:13,105
[CYRUS] between Saint George's Day in late April and Michaelmas at the end of September. Because

656
00:40:13,105 --> 00:40:18,305
[CYRUS] summer heat spoiled the fermentation.

657
00:40:18,305 --> 00:40:21,105
[JEFF] There was a legal brewing season.

658
00:40:21,105 --> 00:40:26,505
[CYRUS] For three hundred years! And even after the law went away, summer brewing meant buying

659
00:40:26,505 --> 00:40:31,905
[CYRUS] ice. So the head of the Spaten brewery in Munich, Gabriel Sedlmayr, gives this young

660
00:40:31,905 --> 00:40:37,305
[CYRUS] engineering professor money and space in his lagering cellars to build a machine. Eighteen seventy-three.

661
00:40:37,305 --> 00:40:42,705
[CYRUS] Linde's first machine uses dimethyl ether, with a mercury seal where the shaft comes out

662
00:40:42,705 --> 00:40:48,105
[CYRUS] of the compressor. And it works — poorly. The seal leaks. So he goes back,

663
00:40:48,105 --> 00:40:53,505
[CYRUS] and in eighteen seventy-six, with an engineer named Friedrich Schipper, he redesigns the compressor for

664
00:40:53,505 --> 00:40:58,905
[CYRUS] ammonia, with a better seal. And the first one he sells goes to the Dreher

665
00:40:58,905 --> 00:41:04,305
[CYRUS] brewery in Trieste.

666
00:41:04,305 --> 00:41:06,305
[JEFF] And how'd that one do?

667
00:41:06,305 --> 00:41:09,905
[CYRUS] It ran from eighteen seventy-seven to nineteen oh eight.

668
00:41:09,905 --> 00:41:11,505
[JEFF] [genuinely surprised] Thirty-one years.

669
00:41:11,505 --> 00:41:17,219
[CYRUS] In a brewery basement, with nobody babysitting it. And that's the whole story of Linde

670
00:41:17,219 --> 00:41:22,933
[CYRUS] in one sentence: his first machine failed on its seal, his second succeeded on its

671
00:41:22,933 --> 00:41:28,647
[CYRUS] seal, and the second one ran for three decades. He didn't invent the cycle —

672
00:41:28,647 --> 00:41:34,362
[CYRUS] Perkins did that forty years earlier. He made it reliable. He quits his professorship in

673
00:41:34,362 --> 00:41:40,076
[CYRUS] eighteen seventy-nine to start a company, with Sedlmayr as an investor, and by eighteen ninety

674
00:41:40,076 --> 00:41:45,790
[CYRUS] there are hundreds of Linde machines running, most of them in breweries. That company is

675
00:41:45,790 --> 00:41:51,505
[CYRUS] still one of the largest industrial gas companies on earth.

676
00:41:51,505 --> 00:41:57,505
[JEFF] So the brewer didn't want a demonstration. He wanted a machine that ran all summer.

677
00:41:57,505 --> 00:42:02,105
[CYRUS] That's it. The seal, not the cycle, was the invention. Okay, so — I want

678
00:42:02,105 --> 00:42:06,705
[CYRUS] to actually do this properly, because the machine humming in your kitchen right now is,

679
00:42:06,705 --> 00:42:11,305
[CYRUS] in its bones, Linde's machine. And I want to explain how it works. Not hand-wave

680
00:42:11,305 --> 00:42:15,905
[CYRUS] it.

681
00:42:15,905 --> 00:42:21,555
[JEFF] Yeah, and let me start with the thing that took me longest to get my

682
00:42:21,555 --> 00:42:27,205
[JEFF] head around, which is that you don't make cold. There's no such thing. You move

683
00:42:27,205 --> 00:42:32,855
[JEFF] heat. Heat is energy, and the first law of thermodynamics says you can't destroy energy,

684
00:42:32,855 --> 00:42:38,505
[JEFF] only move it around. And the second law says heat won't flow uphill — from

685
00:42:38,505 --> 00:42:44,155
[JEFF] a colder thing to a warmer thing — on its own. So a refrigerator is

686
00:42:44,155 --> 00:42:49,805
[JEFF] a machine that pays, with a motor, to push heat from a cold box into

687
00:42:49,805 --> 00:42:55,455
[JEFF] a warm kitchen. Uphill. That's why the coils on the back are warm. That warmth

688
00:42:55,455 --> 00:43:01,105
[JEFF] is the inside of your fridge, moved outside.

689
00:43:01,105 --> 00:43:06,305
[CYRUS] Right. And everything else is plumbing. So let me start with the analogy and then

690
00:43:06,305 --> 00:43:11,505
[CYRUS] I'm going to correct the analogy, because it's a little wrong. The analogy is a

691
00:43:11,505 --> 00:43:16,705
[CYRUS] sponge. You soak up water inside the box, carry the sponge outside, squeeze it out,

692
00:43:16,705 --> 00:43:21,905
[CYRUS] carry it back in empty. The refrigerant soaks up heat inside, gets carried out, gets

693
00:43:21,905 --> 00:43:27,105
[CYRUS] squeezed, and comes back empty.

694
00:43:27,105 --> 00:43:29,505
[JEFF] [curious] Okay. Where does it break?

695
00:43:29,505 --> 00:43:34,625
[CYRUS] Here's where. Squeezing doesn't push the heat out. What squeezing actually does is make the

696
00:43:34,625 --> 00:43:39,745
[CYRUS] heat flow in the right direction. And to see that, you have to know the

697
00:43:39,745 --> 00:43:44,865
[CYRUS] real thing, which is this: there is a liquid in there. Not just a gas.

698
00:43:44,865 --> 00:43:49,985
[CYRUS] There's a liquid, and the entire trick is that it keeps changing back and forth

699
00:43:49,985 --> 00:43:55,105
[CYRUS] between liquid and gas.

700
00:43:55,105 --> 00:43:57,105
[JEFF] [curious] So what is it?

701
00:43:57,105 --> 00:44:02,533
[CYRUS] It's a fluid chosen for one property above everything else: it boils far below kitchen

702
00:44:02,533 --> 00:44:07,962
[CYRUS] temperature. Linde's ammonia boils at about minus thirty-three Celsius — twenty-seven below zero Fahrenheit. The

703
00:44:07,962 --> 00:44:13,390
[CYRUS] isobutane in a modern fridge boils around minus twelve Celsius. And here's the physics that

704
00:44:13,390 --> 00:44:18,819
[CYRUS] makes it work: boiling absorbs an enormous amount of heat. Think about a kettle. Bringing

705
00:44:18,819 --> 00:44:24,247
[CYRUS] water from cold to a boil takes a certain amount of energy. Boiling that kettle

706
00:44:24,247 --> 00:44:29,676
[CYRUS] completely dry takes five or six times as much. Turning a liquid into a gas

707
00:44:29,676 --> 00:44:35,105
[CYRUS] is where the energy goes.

708
00:44:35,105 --> 00:44:38,305
[JEFF] So the refrigerant is boiling. Inside my fridge.

709
00:44:38,305 --> 00:44:43,571
[CYRUS] Inside the box, yes. Behind the back wall, there's a coil — the evaporator. Low-pressure

710
00:44:43,571 --> 00:44:48,838
[CYRUS] liquid refrigerant comes into it and boils, exactly like water on a stove, except this

711
00:44:48,838 --> 00:44:54,105
[CYRUS] liquid boils at twenty-five below zero. So the "stove" is your leftovers. The heat to

712
00:44:54,105 --> 00:44:59,371
[CYRUS] boil it comes out of the food and the air in the box. That's where

713
00:44:59,371 --> 00:45:04,638
[CYRUS] cold is made — a liquid boiling below freezing, and pulling the heat it needs

714
00:45:04,638 --> 00:45:09,905
[CYRUS] out of your dinner.

715
00:45:09,905 --> 00:45:14,105
[JEFF] Okay, so now you've got a gas that's full of heat. But it's still cold

716
00:45:14,105 --> 00:45:18,305
[JEFF] — it's still, what, twenty below?

717
00:45:18,305 --> 00:45:24,171
[CYRUS] Right. And that's the problem. Heat won't flow from a twenty-below gas into a seventy-degree

718
00:45:24,171 --> 00:45:30,038
[CYRUS] kitchen. Wrong direction. So — the compressor. Physically, it's a welded steel dome, and inside

719
00:45:30,038 --> 00:45:35,905
[CYRUS] is an electric motor bolted to a small piston in a cylinder. It's a one-cylinder

720
00:45:35,905 --> 00:45:41,771
[CYRUS] engine run backwards. About thirty-five hundred strokes a minute. It sucks in the low-pressure gas,

721
00:45:41,771 --> 00:45:47,638
[CYRUS] squeezes it to a fraction of its volume, and pushes it out. And squeezing a

722
00:45:47,638 --> 00:45:53,505
[CYRUS] gas heats it — that's the bicycle pump getting hot in your hand.

723
00:45:53,505 --> 00:45:59,505
[JEFF] [skeptical] But that's just heating it more. How does making it hotter get it colder?

724
00:45:59,505 --> 00:46:04,571
[CYRUS] Because you've done two things at once. You've made the gas hot. And — this

725
00:46:04,571 --> 00:46:09,638
[CYRUS] is the key — you've raised the pressure, and at high pressure a fluid condenses

726
00:46:09,638 --> 00:46:14,705
[CYRUS] back to liquid at a much higher temperature. Isobutane at kitchen pressure boils at twelve

727
00:46:14,705 --> 00:46:19,771
[CYRUS] below. Squeeze it to about six atmospheres and it condenses at forty-five Celsius — a

728
00:46:19,771 --> 00:46:24,838
[CYRUS] hundred and thirteen Fahrenheit. So now, relative to that gas, your kitchen is the cold

729
00:46:24,838 --> 00:46:29,905
[CYRUS] thing.

730
00:46:29,905 --> 00:46:33,905
[JEFF] [genuinely surprised] Huh. So you flip which side is cold.

731
00:46:33,905 --> 00:46:39,905
[CYRUS] You flip it. Now the hot gas runs through the coil on the back of

732
00:46:39,905 --> 00:46:45,905
[CYRUS] the fridge — the condenser — and the kitchen air carries the heat away, and

733
00:46:45,905 --> 00:46:51,905
[CYRUS] the gas condenses back into a warm liquid. Think about steam hitting a cold window

734
00:46:51,905 --> 00:46:57,905
[CYRUS] and turning into water. Condensing releases exactly the heat that boiling absorbed. So the heat

735
00:46:57,905 --> 00:47:03,905
[CYRUS] that was in your leftovers comes out the back of the box into your kitchen.

736
00:47:03,905 --> 00:47:04,705
[JEFF] And then?

737
00:47:04,705 --> 00:47:10,705
[CYRUS] Then you have to get the pressure back down. So the warm liquid runs through

738
00:47:10,705 --> 00:47:16,705
[CYRUS] a capillary tube — a very long, very thin tube. It's a controlled leak. The

739
00:47:16,705 --> 00:47:22,705
[CYRUS] pressure collapses by about ten to one, some of the liquid flashes into vapor, the

740
00:47:22,705 --> 00:47:28,705
[CYRUS] temperature crashes to twenty-five below, and it enters the evaporator again as a cold liquid.

741
00:47:28,705 --> 00:47:34,705
[CYRUS] And around it goes, thousands of times a minute. Low pressure, cold, inside. High pressure,

742
00:47:34,705 --> 00:47:40,705
[CYRUS] hot, outside. The compressor makes the pressure difference; the capillary tube lets it back down.

743
00:47:40,705 --> 00:47:46,705
[JEFF] [skeptical] Okay. So — hang on. Because if I'm following the accounting here. The heat

744
00:47:46,705 --> 00:47:52,705
[JEFF] from inside the box comes out the back. Plus the compressor is a motor doing

745
00:47:52,705 --> 00:47:58,705
[JEFF] work, and that work turns into heat too. So the back of my refrigerator is

746
00:47:58,705 --> 00:48:04,705
[JEFF] dumping the heat from inside the fridge plus the heat from running the motor into

747
00:48:04,705 --> 00:48:10,705
[JEFF] my kitchen. My refrigerator is warming my house by more than it's cooling the food.

748
00:48:10,705 --> 00:48:16,216
[CYRUS] [warmly] Yes. Exactly right. And by exactly the size of your electric bill. Heat out

749
00:48:16,216 --> 00:48:21,727
[CYRUS] equals heat in plus the work you paid for. You can feel it on a

750
00:48:21,727 --> 00:48:27,238
[CYRUS] hot day — stand behind the fridge. And there are two consequences of that. One:

751
00:48:27,238 --> 00:48:32,749
[CYRUS] an air conditioner is the same machine with the condenser coil hung outside the wall

752
00:48:32,749 --> 00:48:38,260
[CYRUS] instead of in the room. That's the only difference. Two: cooling is a real line

753
00:48:38,260 --> 00:48:43,771
[CYRUS] item on the planet's electricity bill. A big one. But — and this is the

754
00:48:43,771 --> 00:48:49,282
[CYRUS] part that makes it elegant — the box moves more heat than the electricity it

755
00:48:49,282 --> 00:48:54,793
[CYRUS] eats. Well above one to one. Because it's moving heat, not making cold. You're just

756
00:48:54,793 --> 00:49:00,305
[CYRUS] paying for the pump.

757
00:49:00,305 --> 00:49:03,105
[JEFF] [reflective] So the sponge was half right.

758
00:49:03,105 --> 00:49:08,571
[CYRUS] Exactly half. And here's what sets up the next fifty years. The fluid in that

759
00:49:08,571 --> 00:49:14,038
[CYRUS] loop has to do five things. Boil at the right temperature. Carry a lot of

760
00:49:14,038 --> 00:49:19,504
[CYRUS] heat per pound — ammonia carries about six times what the later fluids carry, which

761
00:49:19,504 --> 00:49:24,971
[CYRUS] is why Linde picked it and why it never left industrial cold storage. Not eat

762
00:49:24,971 --> 00:49:30,438
[CYRUS] the metal. Not burn. And not kill you if it leaks. Ammonia fails "not kill

763
00:49:30,438 --> 00:49:35,904
[CYRUS] you." Ether — Harrison's fluid — fails "not burn." That fifth requirement is the next

764
00:49:35,904 --> 00:49:41,371
[CYRUS] fifty years of this story. Also — Linde's shaft came out of the cylinder, which

765
00:49:41,371 --> 00:49:46,838
[CYRUS] is why the seal was the whole problem. Somebody is going to weld the entire

766
00:49:46,838 --> 00:49:52,304
[CYRUS] thing shut. Later.

767
00:49:52,304 --> 00:49:53,904
[JEFF] [mischievously] I know who.

768
00:49:53,904 --> 00:49:56,704
[CYRUS] I know you know. Don't spoil it.

769
00:49:56,704 --> 00:50:02,504
[JEFF] Okay — so the machine is running in breweries. And beer, not meat, is the

770
00:50:02,504 --> 00:50:08,304
[JEFF] first mass customer in America too: Adolphus Busch in Saint Louis pasteurizes his bottled beer

771
00:50:08,304 --> 00:50:14,104
[JEFF] in eighteen seventy-two, buys five refrigerated railcars in seventy-six, and has eight hundred and fifty

772
00:50:14,104 --> 00:50:19,904
[JEFF] of them by eighty-eight. That's how Budweiser becomes the first national beer brand.

773
00:50:19,904 --> 00:50:23,904
[CYRUS] Beer builds the cold chain and meat rides on it.

774
00:50:23,904 --> 00:50:29,562
[JEFF] [reflective] And the meat story is, to me, the most American story of the bunch.

775
00:50:29,562 --> 00:50:35,219
[JEFF] Gustavus Swift. Born on Cape Cod in eighteen thirty-nine to a family that raised and

776
00:50:35,219 --> 00:50:40,876
[JEFF] slaughtered animals. At fourteen he's working in his brother's butcher shop. At sixteen he opens

777
00:50:40,876 --> 00:50:46,533
[JEFF] his own, with four hundred dollars from an uncle. He moves to Chicago in eighteen

778
00:50:46,533 --> 00:50:52,190
[JEFF] seventy-five, where the Union Stock Yards are. And he looks at how the business works

779
00:50:52,190 --> 00:50:57,847
[JEFF] — buy a live steer in Chicago, ship it to Boston, slaughter it there —

780
00:50:57,847 --> 00:51:03,504
[JEFF] and sees the thing only a butcher would see.

781
00:51:03,504 --> 00:51:07,504
[CYRUS] [amused] Which is how much of the animal nobody eats.

782
00:51:07,504 --> 00:51:12,933
[JEFF] Roughly forty to forty-five percent of a live steer — hide, bone, organs, waste —

783
00:51:12,933 --> 00:51:18,362
[JEFF] never reaches a butcher's counter. And the railroad charges you freight on all of it.

784
00:51:18,362 --> 00:51:23,790
[JEFF] Plus the animals lose weight on the trip, they get bruised, some of them die.

785
00:51:23,790 --> 00:51:29,219
[JEFF] So Swift's answer isn't a better cattle car. It's don't ship the animal. Slaughter in

786
00:51:29,219 --> 00:51:34,647
[JEFF] Chicago, ship the meat. He tries it in winter with ten boxcars running with their

787
00:51:34,647 --> 00:51:40,076
[JEFF] doors taken off, and in eighteen seventy-eight he hires an engineer named Andrew Chase to

788
00:51:40,076 --> 00:51:45,504
[JEFF] design a real refrigerator car.

789
00:51:45,504 --> 00:51:50,204
[CYRUS] And the design is smart — ice in compartments at the top of the car,

790
00:51:50,204 --> 00:51:54,904
[CYRUS] meat packed low. Cold air sinks, so the whole car becomes a convection loop with

791
00:51:54,904 --> 00:51:59,604
[CYRUS] no fan. Hold onto that idea, because it's about to almost kill a man on

792
00:51:59,604 --> 00:52:04,304
[CYRUS] a ship.

793
00:52:04,304 --> 00:52:09,371
[JEFF] Noted. It's like the whole business is a series of people discovering that cold air

794
00:52:09,371 --> 00:52:14,438
[JEFF] sinks. So Swift has the car designed. He goes to the railroads to build them.

795
00:52:14,438 --> 00:52:19,504
[JEFF] And every major railroad says no.

796
00:52:19,504 --> 00:52:21,104
[CYRUS] [skeptical] All of them?

797
00:52:21,104 --> 00:52:26,571
[JEFF] Every one. Because the railroads owned the stockyards along their lines, the cattle pens, the

798
00:52:26,571 --> 00:52:32,038
[JEFF] feedlots. Hauling live cattle was a huge business, and a refrigerator car threatened all of

799
00:52:32,038 --> 00:52:37,504
[JEFF] that capital. So Swift builds his own cars — finances them himself — and routes

800
00:52:37,504 --> 00:52:42,971
[JEFF] them over the one line without a cattle business, the Grand Trunk, up through Michigan

801
00:52:42,971 --> 00:52:48,438
[JEFF] and Canada. Within a year he has nearly two hundred cars and he's moving about

802
00:52:48,438 --> 00:52:53,904
[JEFF] three thousand carcasses a week into Boston.

803
00:52:53,904 --> 00:52:56,304
[CYRUS] The founder routes around the incumbent.

804
00:52:56,304 --> 00:53:01,664
[JEFF] [amused] And it works. Dressed beef undercuts the local butcher by a wide margin, and

805
00:53:01,664 --> 00:53:07,024
[JEFF] retail meat prices in this country fall by roughly a third over the eighteen eighties.

806
00:53:07,024 --> 00:53:12,384
[JEFF] By eighteen ninety, Swift and three other Chicago packers — Armour, Morris, Hammond — are

807
00:53:12,384 --> 00:53:17,744
[JEFF] slaughtering eighty-nine percent of the cattle that come through Chicago, and selling everything but the

808
00:53:17,744 --> 00:53:23,104
[JEFF] squeal — lard, soap, glue, buttons, fertilizer.

809
00:53:23,104 --> 00:53:27,904
[CYRUS] [deliberate] Which is creative destruction in its purest form. And I have a whole thing

810
00:53:27,904 --> 00:53:32,704
[CYRUS] about creative destruction that I'll spare you for now.

811
00:53:32,704 --> 00:53:38,504
[JEFF] [dry] I've heard the thing. It's a good thing. Okay — but so far the

812
00:53:38,504 --> 00:53:44,304
[JEFF] chain runs Chicago to Boston. And here's the thing about a chain of cold. It's

813
00:53:44,304 --> 00:53:50,104
[JEFF] only as good as its weakest link. And in February of eighteen eighty-two, the weakest

814
00:53:50,104 --> 00:53:55,904
[JEFF] link was the air inside the hold of a ship called the Dunedin.

815
00:53:55,904 --> 00:54:01,447
[CYRUS] So — one piece of physics first, because it matters in a minute. The Dunedin's

816
00:54:01,447 --> 00:54:06,990
[CYRUS] machine was not a Linde machine. It was a Bell-Coleman machine, which is a cold-air

817
00:54:06,990 --> 00:54:12,533
[CYRUS] machine — Gorrie's idea, basically, done properly. No liquid refrigerant at all. You compress air,

818
00:54:12,533 --> 00:54:18,076
[CYRUS] cool it, let it expand, and it comes out freezing. Which means the air itself

819
00:54:18,076 --> 00:54:23,619
[CYRUS] is the working fluid, and it moves through wooden trunks into the hold. And cold

820
00:54:23,619 --> 00:54:29,162
[CYRUS] air sinks. If it doesn't circulate, the bottom of the hold freezes and the top

821
00:54:29,162 --> 00:54:34,704
[CYRUS] warms up while the engine runs perfectly.

822
00:54:34,704 --> 00:54:40,647
[JEFF] Right. So — New Zealand. Eighteen eighty-one. A Scottish land company decides to try what

823
00:54:40,647 --> 00:54:46,590
[JEFF] Harrison failed at nine years earlier, on the same ocean, this time with the machine

824
00:54:46,590 --> 00:54:52,533
[JEFF] on board. They refit an iron sailing ship called the Dunedin with a Bell-Coleman plant

825
00:54:52,533 --> 00:54:58,476
[JEFF] and start freezing sheep. And the first thing that happens is the compressor's crankshaft breaks,

826
00:54:58,476 --> 00:55:04,419
[JEFF] and they lose six hundred-odd carcasses. A month to rebuild. Then on February fifteenth, eighteen

827
00:55:04,419 --> 00:55:10,362
[JEFF] eighty-two, the Dunedin sails from Port Chalmers with nearly five thousand carcasses on board. Mutton,

828
00:55:10,362 --> 00:55:16,304
[JEFF] lamb, kegs of butter, and — for some reason — twenty-two hundred sheep tongues.

829
00:55:16,304 --> 00:55:18,304
[CYRUS] Somebody wanted the tongues.

830
00:55:18,304 --> 00:55:23,804
[JEFF] Somebody did. And the engine burns three tons of coal a day — they only

831
00:55:23,804 --> 00:55:29,304
[JEFF] need to run it a few hours in cool weather. And then they hit the

832
00:55:29,304 --> 00:55:34,804
[JEFF] tropics, and the ship gets becalmed near the equator. And the company's own report says

833
00:55:34,804 --> 00:55:40,304
[JEFF] the heat was "hotter than Captain Whitson had before experienced."

834
00:55:40,304 --> 00:55:45,638
[CYRUS] And here's where the air matters. Because when the ship is moving in rough weather,

835
00:55:45,638 --> 00:55:50,971
[CYRUS] the cold air gets — their phrase — "tumbled about and mixed up." The motion

836
00:55:50,971 --> 00:55:56,304
[CYRUS] of the ship stirs the hold. Becalmed, nothing stirs it. The cold settles to the

837
00:55:56,304 --> 00:56:01,638
[CYRUS] bottom. And the moisture in the air freezes out as snow inside the main air

838
00:56:01,638 --> 00:56:06,971
[CYRUS] trunk and chokes it. So the temperature between decks starts rising while the engineers are

839
00:56:06,971 --> 00:56:12,304
[CYRUS] running the machine flat out.

840
00:56:12,304 --> 00:56:18,104
[JEFF] And the captain — John Whitson — goes down himself. An account from some years

841
00:56:18,104 --> 00:56:23,904
[JEFF] later says "the main air trunk had got snowed up, and the captain himself crawled

842
00:56:23,904 --> 00:56:29,704
[JEFF] down to clear it out, and was successful, but got so benumbed during the process

843
00:56:29,704 --> 00:56:35,504
[JEFF] that he had to be hauled out by the heels with a rope."

844
00:56:35,504 --> 00:56:37,104
[CYRUS] By the heels.

845
00:56:37,104 --> 00:56:41,904
[JEFF] Frozen stiff, inside his own cargo. And that wasn't even his only problem — on

846
00:56:41,904 --> 00:56:46,704
[JEFF] certain tacks he was in "constant dread" of the sparks from the refrigeration engine's funnel

847
00:56:46,704 --> 00:56:51,504
[JEFF] setting his sails on fire. He's captaining a sailing ship with a coal-fired engine bolted

848
00:56:51,504 --> 00:56:56,304
[JEFF] to the deck.

849
00:56:56,304 --> 00:56:59,104
[CYRUS] Nobody's ever seen this before.

850
00:56:59,104 --> 00:57:04,204
[JEFF] Right. Ninety-eight days. London, May twenty-fourth, eighteen eighty-two. They unload at night so the sheep

851
00:57:04,204 --> 00:57:09,304
[JEFF] are still hard frozen when the butchers come to Smithfield in the morning. And the

852
00:57:09,304 --> 00:57:14,404
[JEFF] sheep come out — quote — "as bright as newly-killed mutton." Out of nearly five

853
00:57:14,404 --> 00:57:19,504
[JEFF] thousand carcasses, one is condemned. One.

854
00:57:19,504 --> 00:57:21,904
[CYRUS] [incredulous] One out of five thousand?

855
00:57:21,904 --> 00:57:27,162
[JEFF] One. And the Times of London runs a leader that starts, "To-day we have to

856
00:57:27,162 --> 00:57:32,419
[JEFF] record such a triumph over physical difficulties as would have been incredible and even unimaginable

857
00:57:32,419 --> 00:57:37,676
[JEFF] a very few years ago." Five thousand dead sheep, from the other side of the

858
00:57:37,676 --> 00:57:42,933
[JEFF] world, "in as good condition as if they had been slaughtered in some suburban abattoir."

859
00:57:42,933 --> 00:57:48,190
[JEFF] And New Zealand becomes a country that sells lamb and butter to the world. Within

860
00:57:48,190 --> 00:57:53,447
[JEFF] a decade it's shipping about two million carcasses a year. Six million by the First

861
00:57:53,447 --> 00:57:58,704
[JEFF] World War.

862
00:57:58,704 --> 00:58:02,838
[CYRUS] That's the whole difference between eighteen seventy-three and eighteen eighty-two. Harrison packed his meat in

863
00:58:02,838 --> 00:58:06,971
[CYRUS] ice and hoped. Whitson had the machine on board — and cleared its throat by

864
00:58:06,971 --> 00:58:11,104
[CYRUS] hand.

865
00:58:11,104 --> 00:58:15,504
[JEFF] [reflective] And the Dunedin was lost with all hands in eighteen ninety, on that same

866
00:58:15,504 --> 00:58:19,904
[JEFF] run. Nobody knows what happened to her.

867
00:58:19,904 --> 00:58:25,504
[CYRUS] So here's my question, and it's the one the story turns on. Everything we just

868
00:58:25,504 --> 00:58:31,104
[CYRUS] described is obviously better. Cheaper meat, more meat, safer meat. Who fought it?

869
00:58:31,104 --> 00:58:36,819
[JEFF] A lot of people, and this is the part that genuinely surprised me. The railroads,

870
00:58:36,819 --> 00:58:42,533
[JEFF] we covered. But the big one is the butchers. And you have to understand what

871
00:58:42,533 --> 00:58:48,247
[JEFF] a butcher was in eighteen eighty. He wasn't a guy who sells you a steak.

872
00:58:48,247 --> 00:58:53,962
[JEFF] He bought live animals, slaughtered them himself, and sold the meat — a skilled trade,

873
00:58:53,962 --> 00:58:59,676
[JEFF] and in most towns a local monopoly. And Chicago beef is an existential threat to

874
00:58:59,676 --> 00:59:05,390
[JEFF] him. So the butchers organize nationally in the mid-eighteen eighties, and they make three arguments.

875
00:59:05,390 --> 00:59:11,104
[JEFF] One: Chicago's dressed beef is "diseased, tainted, or otherwise unwholesome."

876
00:59:11,104 --> 00:59:12,304
[CYRUS] [skeptical] Was it?

877
00:59:12,304 --> 00:59:18,304
[JEFF] Sometimes! Early on, yes — some of it was bad. Two: you cannot certify a

878
00:59:18,304 --> 00:59:24,304
[JEFF] carcass you never saw alive. Which is a real principle. And three: the Chicago packers

879
00:59:24,304 --> 00:59:30,304
[JEFF] are a cartel. Which was also true — they sold dressed beef below cost and

880
00:59:30,304 --> 00:59:36,304
[JEFF] made it back on the by-products. So all three arguments have something to them. And

881
00:59:36,304 --> 00:59:42,304
[JEFF] what the butchers do is clever. They don't just boycott. In eighteen eighty-nine they lobby

882
00:59:42,304 --> 00:59:48,304
[JEFF] twenty state legislatures for a law that says you can't sell fresh meat in the

883
00:59:48,304 --> 00:59:54,304
[JEFF] state unless the animal was inspected alive, in the state, within twenty-four hours of slaughter.

884
00:59:54,304 --> 00:59:58,704
[CYRUS] [deliberate] Which is a law that only Chicago beef can fail.

885
00:59:58,704 --> 01:00:04,647
[JEFF] Precisely. Minnesota passes it on April sixteenth, eighteen eighty-nine. And the text is remarkable —

886
01:00:04,647 --> 01:00:10,590
[JEFF] "The sale of any fresh beef, veal, mutton, lamb, or pork for human food in

887
01:00:10,590 --> 01:00:16,533
[JEFF] this state, except as hereinafter provided, is hereby prohibited." And the "provided" is the live

888
01:00:16,533 --> 01:00:22,476
[JEFF] inspection. So — here's the story. There's a meat dealer in Saint Paul named Henry

889
01:00:22,476 --> 01:00:28,419
[JEFF] Barber. And he sells a hundred pounds of fresh beef from an animal slaughtered in

890
01:00:28,419 --> 01:00:34,362
[JEFF] Illinois. And he gets convicted before a justice of the peace in Ramsey County. And

891
01:00:34,362 --> 01:00:40,305
[JEFF] the case goes all the way to the Supreme Court of the United States.

892
01:00:40,305 --> 01:00:43,105
[CYRUS] [incredulous] Over a hundred pounds of beef?

893
01:00:43,105 --> 01:00:48,647
[JEFF] Minnesota versus Barber. Decided May nineteenth, eighteen ninety. Justice Harlan writes for a unanimous Court

894
01:00:48,647 --> 01:00:54,190
[JEFF] — nine to nothing — and he doesn't buy the health argument for a second:

895
01:00:54,190 --> 01:00:59,733
[JEFF] "A state cannot make a law designed to guard against disease an inspection law, within

896
01:00:59,733 --> 01:01:05,276
[JEFF] the constitutional meaning of that word, by calling it so in the title." And the

897
01:01:05,276 --> 01:01:10,819
[JEFF] line that matters for the next hundred years of American commerce: "The enactment of a

898
01:01:10,819 --> 01:01:16,362
[JEFF] similar statute by each one of the states composing the Union would result in the

899
01:01:16,362 --> 01:01:21,905
[JEFF] destruction of commerce among the several states."

900
01:01:21,905 --> 01:01:25,505
[CYRUS] So the Commerce Clause makes the national market legal.

901
01:01:25,505 --> 01:01:31,505
[JEFF] Yeah. And the twenty state laws fall with it. A year later the Court strikes

902
01:01:31,505 --> 01:01:37,505
[JEFF] down Virginia's version over eighteen pounds of beef. And I think this is the general

903
01:01:37,505 --> 01:01:43,505
[JEFF] principle in almost every one of these stories: the resisters are right about the specifics

904
01:01:43,505 --> 01:01:49,505
[JEFF] of the moment — some of the meat was bad, the packers were colluding —

905
01:01:49,505 --> 01:01:55,505
[JEFF] and wrong about the trend. They lost in the one venue the packers couldn't buy.

906
01:01:55,505 --> 01:01:57,905
[CYRUS] And the man at the counter?

907
01:01:57,905 --> 01:02:03,733
[JEFF] [deliberate] Yeah. So here's the honest version of this. The system flourished. Meat got a

908
01:02:03,733 --> 01:02:09,562
[JEFF] third cheaper in a decade of deflation. More people ate beef, and better beef. The

909
01:02:09,562 --> 01:02:15,390
[JEFF] next generation of butchers grew up cutting Chicago's carcasses and did fine. And the man

910
01:02:15,390 --> 01:02:21,219
[JEFF] at the counter in eighteen eighty-five with thirty years of skill at slaughtering and dressing

911
01:02:21,219 --> 01:02:27,047
[JEFF] an animal — that man is done. His craft became a job. He has to

912
01:02:27,047 --> 01:02:32,876
[JEFF] find new work. That asymmetry is a real cost, and I don't think you have

913
01:02:32,876 --> 01:02:38,705
[JEFF] to apologize for the system to name it. Both things are true.

914
01:02:38,705 --> 01:02:44,705
[CYRUS] And the cattlemen get hit from the other side. Because once Chicago is the only

915
01:02:44,705 --> 01:02:50,705
[CYRUS] buyer, a rancher in Wyoming is selling into a market priced by four firms. And

916
01:02:50,705 --> 01:02:56,705
[CYRUS] those four firms did cartelize — the pool met weekly in the offices of their

917
01:02:56,705 --> 01:03:02,705
[CYRUS] lawyer, Henry Veeder. The Senate sends a committee out — Senator Vest, hearings in Saint

918
01:03:02,705 --> 01:03:08,705
[CYRUS] Louis in eighteen eighty-eight — and the report finds documented price-fixing incidents but no proof

919
01:03:08,705 --> 01:03:14,705
[CYRUS] of a formal conspiracy. What it does find is an "artificial and abnormal centralization of

920
01:03:14,705 --> 01:03:20,705
[CYRUS] markets." Cattle prices fell faster than retail beef did, and the difference went to Chicago.

921
01:03:20,705 --> 01:03:26,305
[JEFF] And Congress passes the Sherman Antitrust Act that same summer. July second, eighteen ninety.

922
01:03:26,305 --> 01:03:31,505
[CYRUS] Six weeks after Barber. And it takes fifteen years to actually win a case against

923
01:03:31,505 --> 01:03:36,705
[CYRUS] the packers — nineteen oh five, Swift versus the United States. But here's the nuance.

924
01:03:36,705 --> 01:03:41,905
[CYRUS] The Beef Trust never achieved a monopoly — the number of slaughterhouses in the country

925
01:03:41,905 --> 01:03:47,105
[CYRUS] went up in the eighties, not down. What concentrated was the buying end. That's where

926
01:03:47,105 --> 01:03:52,305
[CYRUS] the cattleman had zero leverage.

927
01:03:52,305 --> 01:03:56,705
[JEFF] And then there's the last holdout, which is the British consumer.

928
01:03:56,705 --> 01:03:58,305
[CYRUS] [dry] Who was wary.

929
01:03:58,305 --> 01:04:03,733
[JEFF] Deeply wary. In eighteen sixty-seven, one Briton in twelve was eating imported meat. By eighteen

930
01:04:03,733 --> 01:04:09,162
[JEFF] eighty-seven it's one in four. But they had to be talked into it — there

931
01:04:09,162 --> 01:04:14,590
[JEFF] was a real fear that a sheep that had been dead for six months would

932
01:04:14,590 --> 01:04:20,019
[JEFF] come out dry and tasteless. And the way the prejudice got overcome, honestly, is price.

933
01:04:20,019 --> 01:04:25,447
[JEFF] A magazine in eighteen ninety-seven just says it: "We import them because they are cheap."

934
01:04:25,447 --> 01:04:30,876
[JEFF] The working class of London ate it first; the prejudice, one observer said, was "mainly

935
01:04:30,876 --> 01:04:36,305
[JEFF] a middle-class one after all."

936
01:04:36,305 --> 01:04:40,105
[CYRUS] And now "fresh and local" is the high-status option again. A hundred and forty years

937
01:04:40,105 --> 01:04:43,905
[CYRUS] and it flipped completely.

938
01:04:43,905 --> 01:04:49,838
[JEFF] It really did. One more thing, because it's too good. In nineteen oh three the

939
01:04:49,838 --> 01:04:55,771
[JEFF] United Fruit Company puts its first refrigerated banana ship into service — the Venus —

940
01:04:55,771 --> 01:05:01,705
[JEFF] and builds a fleet, reportedly painted white to reflect the sun. The very next year,

941
01:05:01,705 --> 01:05:07,638
[JEFF] O Henry, who'd been hiding in Honduras from an embezzlement charge, coins the phrase "banana

942
01:05:07,638 --> 01:05:13,571
[JEFF] republic." So that phrase sits on a cold chain. A fruit that rots in a

943
01:05:13,571 --> 01:05:19,505
[JEFF] week can't be a country's economy — until you can chill it.

944
01:05:19,505 --> 01:05:24,625
[CYRUS] [deliberate] And one number starts moving in here. The steepest fall in infant mortality in

945
01:05:24,625 --> 01:05:29,745
[CYRUS] American history runs from about eighteen seventy-five to nineteen twenty-five — that one-baby-in-four figure starts

946
01:05:29,745 --> 01:05:34,865
[CYRUS] coming down. And I want to be careful about cold's share of that, because pasteurization

947
01:05:34,865 --> 01:05:39,985
[CYRUS] and sewers do most of the work. But cold is what lets clean milk reach

948
01:05:39,985 --> 01:05:45,105
[CYRUS] the tenement still clean.

949
01:05:45,105 --> 01:05:50,785
[JEFF] So by eighteen ninety the machine runs at industrial scale, the market is legal, and

950
01:05:50,785 --> 01:05:56,465
[JEFF] the chain crosses oceans. And two things are still true. Nobody trusts food that's been

951
01:05:56,465 --> 01:06:02,145
[JEFF] stored — "cold storage" is about to become a political villain. And nobody has one

952
01:06:02,145 --> 01:06:07,825
[JEFF] of these machines in their kitchen. They're the size of a room. They run on

953
01:06:07,825 --> 01:06:13,505
[JEFF] ammonia and sulfur dioxide. And when they leak, they kill you.

954
01:06:13,505 --> 01:06:17,105
[CYRUS] So the next part is about making cold something you'd trust. And then — something

955
01:06:17,105 --> 01:06:20,705
[CYRUS] you could own.

956
00:55:15,723 --> 00:55:21,163
[JEFF] So by nineteen oh seven the technology works. What doesn't work is trust. Nobody wants

957
00:55:21,163 --> 00:55:26,603
[JEFF] to eat an egg that's been sitting in a warehouse since April. "Cold storage" is

958
00:55:26,603 --> 00:55:32,043
[JEFF] a phrase people spit. And somebody had to write the rules that made stored food

959
00:55:32,043 --> 00:55:37,483
[JEFF] something you'd put in your mouth. And the person who did it had to be

960
00:55:37,483 --> 00:55:42,923
[JEFF] smuggled into the federal government under her initials.

961
00:55:42,923 --> 00:55:44,123
[CYRUS] [curious] Her initials?

962
00:55:44,123 --> 00:55:49,643
[JEFF] Yeah — M-E Pennington. And she's one of my favorite people in this whole story.

963
00:55:49,643 --> 00:55:55,163
[JEFF] Philadelphia Quaker. Fell in love with chemistry from a medical textbook at twelve. Goes to

964
00:55:55,163 --> 00:56:00,683
[JEFF] the University of Pennsylvania at eighteen, and in eighteen ninety-two she finishes every single requirement

965
00:56:00,683 --> 00:56:06,203
[JEFF] for a bachelor of science in chemistry. And Penn hands her a "certificate of proficiency."

966
00:56:06,203 --> 00:56:11,723
[JEFF] Because the trustees did not give degrees to women.

967
00:56:11,723 --> 00:56:14,523
[CYRUS] [incredulous] A certificate. For the same coursework.

968
00:56:14,523 --> 00:56:20,351
[JEFF] Same coursework, same exams. And here's the part that makes it almost funny — three

969
00:56:20,351 --> 00:56:26,180
[JEFF] years later, the same university gives her a doctorate. She's one of about a dozen

970
00:56:26,180 --> 00:56:32,008
[JEFF] American women with a chemistry doctorate before nineteen hundred. And then nobody will hire a

971
00:56:32,008 --> 00:56:37,837
[JEFF] woman chemist. So she starts her own lab, in eighteen ninety-eight, and sells subscriptions —

972
00:56:37,837 --> 00:56:43,665
[JEFF] fifty dollars a year — to Philadelphia doctors, and eventually to the city itself. And

973
00:56:43,665 --> 00:56:49,494
[JEFF] as the city's bacteriologist she spends years chasing bad milk and bad ice cream through

974
00:56:49,494 --> 00:56:55,322
[JEFF] Philadelphia's dairies. Not a small job, given what we said about milk.

975
00:56:55,322 --> 00:57:00,122
[CYRUS] [amused] So she's the person who knows what's actually in the milk.

976
00:57:00,122 --> 00:57:05,437
[JEFF] Which is exactly what gets her noticed. Harvey Wiley runs the Bureau of Chemistry at

977
00:57:05,437 --> 00:57:10,751
[JEFF] the Department of Agriculture — he's the guy behind the Pure Food and Drugs Act

978
00:57:10,751 --> 00:57:16,065
[JEFF] of nineteen oh six. And his next fight is perishables. Eggs, poultry, fish — and

979
00:57:16,065 --> 00:57:21,380
[JEFF] the new cold-storage warehouses nobody trusts. He wants her to run a new lab. Federal

980
00:57:21,380 --> 00:57:26,694
[JEFF] hiring goes through a civil-service exam and a board. So she takes the exam, under

981
00:57:26,694 --> 00:57:32,008
[JEFF] her own name, and gets the highest score. And then Wiley submits the hiring paperwork

982
00:57:32,008 --> 00:57:37,323
[JEFF] as "M-E Pennington."

983
00:57:37,323 --> 00:57:40,123
[CYRUS] So Wiley does the maneuver.

984
00:57:40,123 --> 00:57:45,323
[JEFF] He does. And when the Civil Service Commission figures out M-E is a woman, they

985
00:57:45,323 --> 00:57:50,523
[JEFF] try to rescind it. And Wiley's argument — which wins — is that the civil-service

986
00:57:50,523 --> 00:57:55,723
[JEFF] law does not give him the right to refuse the top scorer because she's a

987
00:57:55,723 --> 00:58:00,923
[JEFF] woman. So she's in. The first woman to run a lab there. And, by at

988
00:58:00,923 --> 00:58:06,123
[JEFF] least one account, the highest-paid woman in the federal government. And then she goes and

989
00:58:06,123 --> 00:58:11,323
[JEFF] does the work.

990
00:58:11,323 --> 00:58:12,923
[CYRUS] Which is what, exactly?

991
00:58:12,923 --> 00:58:18,603
[JEFF] She puts thermometers in railcars. Between nineteen oh nine and nineteen twelve, her lab tracks

992
00:58:18,603 --> 00:58:24,283
[JEFF] a hundred and twenty carloads of dressed poultry — a hundred and forty thousand miles

993
00:58:24,283 --> 00:58:29,963
[JEFF] of track, Iowa and Tennessee to New York — with recording thermographs down by the

994
00:58:29,963 --> 00:58:35,643
[JEFF] ice bunker and up at the top of the load. And she and her people

995
00:58:35,643 --> 00:58:41,323
[JEFF] ride with them — her own bulletin has a staffer riding carloads of poultry from

996
00:58:41,323 --> 00:58:47,003
[JEFF] Tennessee to New York for days at a stretch, reading the instruments. During the war

997
00:58:47,003 --> 00:58:52,683
[JEFF] she personally inspected hundreds of refrigerator cars. And out of that comes the national standard:

998
00:58:52,683 --> 00:58:58,363
[JEFF] keep dressed poultry below thirty-one degrees on a long haul. She writes the rules for

999
00:58:58,363 --> 00:59:04,043
[JEFF] how a refrigerator car has to be built. She solves humidity in cold storage. The

1000
00:59:04,043 --> 00:59:09,723
[JEFF] press eventually calls her "the Ice Woman."

1001
00:59:09,723 --> 00:59:12,923
[CYRUS] [dry] The Ice Woman. To Tudor's Ice King.

1002
00:59:12,923 --> 00:59:18,923
[JEFF] And she had a voice. Nineteen thirteen, on the public's panic about stored food: "A

1003
00:59:18,923 --> 00:59:24,923
[JEFF] bad egg is blamed on cold storage during the months of April and May, when

1004
00:59:24,923 --> 00:59:30,923
[JEFF] a storage egg is as rare a phenomenon as snow in the tropics." And this

1005
00:59:30,923 --> 00:59:36,923
[JEFF] one: "Cold storage cannot improve foods, except, perhaps, cheese." And my favorite — asked about

1006
00:59:36,923 --> 00:59:42,923
[JEFF] the suffrage movement, she said, "Chickens in cold storage are more interesting than women's suffrage."

1007
00:59:42,923 --> 00:59:46,523
[CYRUS] Which is a choice about how to fight.

1008
00:59:46,523 --> 00:59:52,043
[JEFF] It's a very deliberate choice about how to fight. She'd been credentialed sideways by every

1009
00:59:52,043 --> 00:59:57,563
[JEFF] institution she'd passed through, and her answer was to be so good at the work

1010
00:59:57,563 --> 01:00:03,083
[JEFF] that the institution couldn't look away. And the payoff is that the invisible system —

1011
01:00:03,083 --> 01:00:08,603
[JEFF] the warehouse you never see — becomes trustworthy because a woman who'd been told she

1012
01:00:08,603 --> 01:00:14,123
[JEFF] couldn't have a degree wrote the rules for it.

1013
01:00:14,123 --> 01:00:18,323
[CYRUS] [skeptical] Did the public buy it, though? Because there's a version of this where the

1014
01:00:18,323 --> 01:00:22,523
[CYRUS] science is right and nobody cares.

1015
01:00:22,523 --> 01:00:28,008
[JEFF] [amused] Well — the industry didn't wait to find out. Nineteen eleven, "cold storage" is

1016
01:00:28,008 --> 01:00:33,494
[JEFF] a national villain. It's the high-cost-of-living issue — the story is that warehouses are hoarding

1017
01:00:33,494 --> 01:00:38,980
[JEFF] eggs to jack up prices, and the food's old. And so on October twenty-third, nineteen

1018
01:00:38,980 --> 01:00:44,465
[JEFF] eleven, the poultry, butter, and egg trade association throws a banquet in the Louis the

1019
01:00:44,465 --> 01:00:49,951
[JEFF] Sixteenth room of the Hotel Sherman in Chicago. Four hundred guests, including the mayor and

1020
01:00:49,951 --> 01:00:55,437
[JEFF] the health commissioner. And every course — the poultry, the eggs, the butter — is

1021
01:00:55,437 --> 01:01:00,922
[JEFF] months out of a cold-storage warehouse.

1022
01:01:00,922 --> 01:01:03,322
[CYRUS] Prove it by eating it.

1023
01:01:03,322 --> 01:01:08,637
[JEFF] In front of the mayor! And the trade paper, the Egg Reporter, writes afterward that

1024
01:01:08,637 --> 01:01:13,951
[JEFF] the guests learned "that one could partake of a cold storage meal and still live."

1025
01:01:13,951 --> 01:01:19,265
[JEFF] And the funny thing is that the data backed them up. Massachusetts ran a commission

1026
01:01:19,265 --> 01:01:24,580
[JEFF] on this in nineteen twelve and found that in Boston, nearly forty percent of the

1027
01:01:24,580 --> 01:01:29,894
[JEFF] eggs and butter and forty-five percent of the poultry went through cold storage — and

1028
01:01:29,894 --> 01:01:35,208
[JEFF] the notion that food sat in there for years was, in their words, "refuted by

1029
01:01:35,208 --> 01:01:40,523
[JEFF] the statistical evidence."

1030
01:01:40,523 --> 01:01:43,723
[CYRUS] So the industry's answer to fear was theater.

1031
01:01:43,723 --> 01:01:49,323
[JEFF] And the legislature's answer was a date stamp. Massachusetts capped cold storage at twelve months

1032
01:01:49,323 --> 01:01:54,923
[JEFF] and made every store hang a sign — "Cold-storage goods sold here." New York required

1033
01:01:54,923 --> 01:02:00,523
[JEFF] every crate to be stamped with the date it went in, in letters at least

1034
01:02:00,523 --> 01:02:06,123
[JEFF] three-eighths of an inch high. Both half-worked. And by the nineteen twenties, the fight over

1035
01:02:06,123 --> 01:02:11,723
[JEFF] whether you can trust stored food is basically over. The fight that replaces it is

1036
01:02:11,723 --> 01:02:17,323
[JEFF] whether you can trust the machine in your kitchen.

1037
01:02:17,323 --> 01:02:23,234
[CYRUS] [deliberate] Right — because now the machine shrinks. And this is the part I want

1038
01:02:23,234 --> 01:02:29,145
[CYRUS] to walk through carefully, because it's a systems failure, not a villain story. Kelvinator puts

1039
01:02:29,145 --> 01:02:35,056
[CYRUS] a household unit on the market in nineteen eighteen. Frigidaire gets bought by General Motors

1040
01:02:35,056 --> 01:02:40,967
[CYRUS] in nineteen nineteen. And in nineteen twenty-seven, only about forty percent of American homes have

1041
01:02:40,967 --> 01:02:46,878
[CYRUS] any refrigeration at all — and almost all of that is ice. In April of

1042
01:02:46,878 --> 01:02:52,789
[CYRUS] nineteen twenty-nine, under five percent of homes have an electric refrigerator. So this is, like,

1043
01:02:52,789 --> 01:02:58,700
[CYRUS] a tiny, rich-people product. And the early ones look like this: a wooden cabinet, with

1044
01:02:58,700 --> 01:03:04,611
[CYRUS] a compressor either in the basement or bolted on top, driven by a belt, with

1045
01:03:04,611 --> 01:03:10,523
[CYRUS] a shaft passing through a wall — and a seal around that shaft.

1046
01:03:10,523 --> 01:03:12,523
[JEFF] Which is Linde's problem again.

1047
01:03:12,523 --> 01:03:18,456
[CYRUS] In your kitchen this time. Seals wear. Belts slip. And when the seal leaks, what

1048
01:03:18,456 --> 01:03:24,389
[CYRUS] leaks out is one of three fluids: ammonia, sulfur dioxide, or methyl chloride. And here's

1049
01:03:24,389 --> 01:03:30,323
[CYRUS] the systems failure. Those fluids were chosen for physics — for a boiling point and

1050
01:03:30,323 --> 01:03:36,256
[CYRUS] a pressure a small compressor could handle. Nobody chose them for what happens when they

1051
01:03:36,256 --> 01:03:42,189
[CYRUS] get out. Sulfur dioxide burns your eyes, which at least warns you. Methyl chloride is

1052
01:03:42,189 --> 01:03:48,123
[CYRUS] nearly odorless. And all three, in the quantities inside one refrigerator, can kill you.

1053
01:03:48,123 --> 01:03:49,323
[JEFF] And did.

1054
01:03:49,323 --> 01:03:54,523
[CYRUS] And did. Nineteen twenty-nine, Chicago. The American Medical Association counted twenty-nine methyl-chloride poisonings in that

1055
01:03:54,523 --> 01:03:59,723
[CYRUS] one city in that one year. Ten deaths. And the case everybody remembers is a

1056
01:03:59,723 --> 01:04:04,923
[CYRUS] family named Painter — two parents and their one-year-old — who died in their sleep

1057
01:04:04,923 --> 01:04:10,123
[CYRUS] in August from a leak in their apartment building's central refrigeration unit. The maintenance man

1058
01:04:10,123 --> 01:04:15,323
[CYRUS] had heard a hiss that sounded like a leak, couldn't find it, and left it

1059
01:04:15,323 --> 01:04:20,523
[CYRUS] for the night.

1060
01:04:20,523 --> 01:04:21,723
[JEFF] [exhales] One night.

1061
01:04:21,723 --> 01:04:27,189
[CYRUS] And the city immediately called for banning the gas. And I want to put one

1062
01:04:27,189 --> 01:04:32,656
[CYRUS] more thing in the room, because it's the same spring and the same fear. May

1063
01:04:32,656 --> 01:04:38,123
[CYRUS] fifteenth, nineteen twenty-nine, the Cleveland Clinic. In the basement, three or four tons of nitrocellulose

1064
01:04:38,123 --> 01:04:43,589
[CYRUS] X-ray film start to decompose — from heat off a light bulb or a steam

1065
01:04:43,589 --> 01:04:49,056
[CYRUS] pipe, the accounts differ — and the fumes go up through the building. A hundred

1066
01:04:49,056 --> 01:04:54,523
[CYRUS] and twenty-three people die, including one of the clinic's founders. Now — that was not

1067
01:04:54,523 --> 01:04:59,989
[CYRUS] a refrigerant. It was film. But it was poison gas in a building, in nineteen

1068
01:04:59,989 --> 01:05:05,456
[CYRUS] twenty-nine, on the front page. That is the air the electric refrigerator is trying to

1069
01:05:05,456 --> 01:05:10,922
[CYRUS] sell itself into.

1070
01:05:10,922 --> 01:05:16,522
[JEFF] So the box works, and people are afraid of it. And they should be.

1071
01:05:16,522 --> 01:05:20,922
[CYRUS] Which brings me to the two most famous physicists in Berlin.

1072
01:05:20,922 --> 01:05:24,522
[JEFF] [amused] Oh, I have been waiting for this one.

1073
01:05:24,522 --> 01:05:29,656
[CYRUS] [deliberate] So — this is reportedly how it starts. Winter of nineteen twenty-five, twenty-six. Albert

1074
01:05:29,656 --> 01:05:34,789
[CYRUS] Einstein, in Berlin, reads in the newspaper that a family has died in their sleep.

1075
01:05:34,789 --> 01:05:39,923
[CYRUS] Not a fire. Not a break-in. The refrigerator. Now — nobody has ever found that

1076
01:05:39,923 --> 01:05:45,056
[CYRUS] newspaper, or that family. The story comes down through Einstein's collaborator, so I'll say "reportedly."

1077
01:05:45,056 --> 01:05:50,189
[CYRUS] But we know what he said, because his collaborator told it: "There must be a

1078
01:05:50,189 --> 01:05:55,323
[CYRUS] better way."

1079
01:05:55,323 --> 01:05:57,323
[JEFF] And the collaborator is —

1080
01:05:57,323 --> 01:06:02,808
[CYRUS] Leó Szilárd. Hungarian physicist, twenty-two when they met, Einstein was forty-one. Szilárd's dissertation had extended

1081
01:06:02,808 --> 01:06:08,294
[CYRUS] thermodynamics in a way Einstein had said was impossible, and Einstein loved him for it.

1082
01:06:08,294 --> 01:06:13,780
[CYRUS] Total opposites — Szilárd is pushy and self-confident, Einstein is quiet. And by the mid-twenties

1083
01:06:13,780 --> 01:06:19,265
[CYRUS] Szilárd is about to become a lecturer with no salary, so — honestly — part

1084
01:06:19,265 --> 01:06:24,751
[CYRUS] of the motive is money. Patents could fund his career. And Einstein, who'd spent years

1085
01:06:24,751 --> 01:06:30,237
[CYRUS] in the Swiss patent office and said they were the best years of his life,

1086
01:06:30,237 --> 01:06:35,722
[CYRUS] was happy to draft them himself.

1087
01:06:35,722 --> 01:06:37,322
[JEFF] So what's the insight?

1088
01:06:37,322 --> 01:06:42,922
[CYRUS] The insight is that the problem isn't just the gas. It's the moving parts. Every

1089
01:06:42,922 --> 01:06:48,522
[CYRUS] seal, every bearing, every shaft through a wall is a place a leak can start.

1090
01:06:48,522 --> 01:06:54,122
[CYRUS] So design the moving parts out. And from thermodynamics they could see several ways to

1091
01:06:54,122 --> 01:06:59,722
[CYRUS] make cold with nothing moving. By the fall of nineteen twenty-six they've settled on three

1092
01:06:59,722 --> 01:07:05,322
[CYRUS] — one of them Carré's old idea, absorption, driven by heat. Szilárd writes his brother:

1093
01:07:05,322 --> 01:07:10,922
[CYRUS] "All three machines work without moving parts, and are hermetically sealed." They file patents in

1094
01:07:10,922 --> 01:07:16,522
[CYRUS] six countries. The American patent attorney writes back asking whether "Albert Einstein is the same

1095
01:07:16,522 --> 01:07:22,122
[CYRUS] person who propounded the theory of relativity."

1096
01:07:22,122 --> 01:07:23,322
[JEFF] Reasonable question.

1097
01:07:23,322 --> 01:07:29,122
[CYRUS] And it works! Sort of, and in stages. They sell the absorption design to Electrolux

1098
01:07:29,122 --> 01:07:34,922
[CYRUS] in nineteen twenty-seven for thirty-one hundred reichsmarks — seven hundred and fifty dollars. Electrolux's own

1099
01:07:34,922 --> 01:07:40,722
[CYRUS] files call it "very cheap," and they never build it; they bought it to protect

1100
01:07:40,722 --> 01:07:46,522
[CYRUS] their own patents. Then there's a little tap-water-powered cooler you drop in a glass —

1101
01:07:46,522 --> 01:07:52,322
[CYRUS] Einstein's idea — that dies because German water pressure varied from floor to floor in

1102
01:07:52,322 --> 01:07:58,122
[CYRUS] a building. And then the one that matters: the electromagnetic pump. No piston. A traveling

1103
01:07:58,122 --> 01:08:03,922
[CYRUS] magnetic field pushes a liquid metal — a potassium-sodium alloy — and the metal itself

1104
01:08:03,922 --> 01:08:09,722
[CYRUS] acts as the piston that compresses the refrigerant. Nothing mechanical moves.

1105
01:08:09,722 --> 01:08:12,522
[JEFF] [skeptical] Liquid metal? In a kitchen appliance?

1106
01:08:12,522 --> 01:08:18,232
[CYRUS] Yes. And the metal attacks the wire insulation, so Szilárd takes it to Einstein, and

1107
01:08:18,232 --> 01:08:23,941
[CYRUS] their engineer Korodi says, "Einstein thought a few minutes," and proposed driving it by induction

1108
01:08:23,941 --> 01:08:29,650
[CYRUS] from outside the tube. Korodi ran the numbers and knew it would be much less

1109
01:08:29,650 --> 01:08:35,359
[CYRUS] efficient than a normal compressor. But it would never leak. So the big German electrical

1110
01:08:35,359 --> 01:08:41,068
[CYRUS] company, A-E-G, funds a department. They pay Korodi five hundred reichsmarks a month — about

1111
01:08:41,068 --> 01:08:46,777
[CYRUS] a hundred and twenty dollars — which he called "a good salary at a time

1112
01:08:46,777 --> 01:08:52,486
[CYRUS] when a car, a Ford, cost three hundred dollars." And his own summary of working

1113
01:08:52,486 --> 01:08:58,195
[CYRUS] with the most famous physicist alive was, "I didn't talk to Einstein about physics." And

1114
01:08:58,195 --> 01:09:03,904
[CYRUS] the prototype pump howled. A physicist friend of theirs said it "howled like a jackal."

1115
01:09:03,904 --> 01:09:09,613
[CYRUS] Another said it wailed like a banshee. It was cavitation in the liquid metal, and

1116
01:09:09,613 --> 01:09:15,322
[CYRUS] they eventually got it down to acceptable.

1117
01:09:15,322 --> 01:09:17,722
[JEFF] A refrigerator that screams.

1118
01:09:17,722 --> 01:09:23,222
[CYRUS] Briefly. And by July thirty-first, nineteen thirty-one, there is an Einstein-Szilárd refrigerator running continuously in

1119
01:09:23,222 --> 01:09:28,722
[CYRUS] the A-E-G research institute — mounted in a General Electric cabinet — drawing a hundred

1120
01:09:28,722 --> 01:09:34,222
[CYRUS] and thirty-six watts. It worked. And then three things happen at once. The Depression guts

1121
01:09:34,222 --> 01:09:39,722
[CYRUS] A-E-G's budget. In nineteen thirty the Nazis take nearly twenty percent of the Reichstag, and

1122
01:09:39,722 --> 01:09:45,222
[CYRUS] Szilárd writes Einstein, "I don't know if it will be possible to build our refrigerator

1123
01:09:45,222 --> 01:09:50,722
[CYRUS] in Europe." And — from America — a chemist demonstrates a gas that can't burn

1124
01:09:50,722 --> 01:09:56,222
[CYRUS] and can't poison you. Which, in one stroke, erases the entire reason to build a

1125
01:09:56,222 --> 01:10:01,722
[CYRUS] refrigerator with no moving parts.

1126
01:10:01,722 --> 01:10:04,122
[JEFF] Because the selling point was safety.

1127
01:10:04,122 --> 01:10:09,678
[CYRUS] [deliberate] The only one. Their engineer wrote the final report in August of nineteen thirty-two

1128
01:10:09,678 --> 01:10:15,234
[CYRUS] — a hundred and four pages — and it went in a filing cabinet. A

1129
01:10:15,234 --> 01:10:20,789
[CYRUS] few months later Hitler is chancellor. More than forty-five patent filings in six countries, and

1130
01:10:20,789 --> 01:10:26,345
[CYRUS] no product. Two postscripts, though. The pump later found a job cooling breeder reactors, where

1131
01:10:26,345 --> 01:10:31,900
[CYRUS] "never leaks" matters more than efficiency. And the patent money carried Szilárd to England, where

1132
01:10:31,900 --> 01:10:37,456
[CYRUS] in nineteen thirty-three he worked out the nuclear chain reaction. In July of nineteen thirty-nine,

1133
01:10:37,456 --> 01:10:43,011
[CYRUS] he drove out to Long Island to convince Einstein to sign a letter to President

1134
01:10:43,011 --> 01:10:48,567
[CYRUS] Roosevelt about the atomic bomb — and the two of them spent part of that

1135
01:10:48,567 --> 01:10:54,123
[CYRUS] afternoon reminiscing about the refrigerators.

1136
01:10:54,123 --> 01:10:59,389
[JEFF] [reflective] That's remarkable. And I think the honest reading of that story is that nobody

1137
01:10:59,389 --> 01:11:04,656
[JEFF] chose wrong. Because the road they didn't take had real costs too. A gas-absorption fridge

1138
01:11:04,656 --> 01:11:09,923
[JEFF] needs a gas line and a flame in your kitchen. The prototype drew roughly twice

1139
01:11:09,923 --> 01:11:15,189
[JEFF] the electricity of a compressor box. Servel sold gas-absorption refrigerators in America for thirty years,

1140
01:11:15,189 --> 01:11:20,456
[JEFF] right up to nineteen fifty-six — and they carried their own hazard, carbon monoxide. The

1141
01:11:20,456 --> 01:11:25,723
[JEFF] "better" answer wasn't free.

1142
01:11:25,723 --> 01:11:31,608
[CYRUS] A hundred percent. Absorption lost on two fronts at once. The grid made a motor

1143
01:11:31,608 --> 01:11:37,494
[CYRUS] cheap — by nineteen twenty-nine, two out of three American homes have a wire. And

1144
01:11:37,494 --> 01:11:43,380
[CYRUS] the safe gas took away its only argument. So the compressor wins. And the compressor

1145
01:11:43,380 --> 01:11:49,265
[CYRUS] gets fixed two ways in three years — one mechanical, one chemical. The mechanical fix

1146
01:11:49,265 --> 01:11:55,151
[CYRUS] is a Danish-born G-E engineer named Christian Steenstrup, who in nineteen twenty-seven puts the motor

1147
01:11:55,151 --> 01:12:01,037
[CYRUS] and the compressor together inside one welded steel dome on top of the cabinet. No

1148
01:12:01,037 --> 01:12:06,923
[CYRUS] belt. No shaft through a wall. No seal to wear. Nothing to leak.

1149
01:12:06,923 --> 01:12:10,323
[JEFF] So Linde kept the ammonia in with a sealed gland, and G-E just welded the

1150
01:12:10,323 --> 01:12:13,723
[JEFF] box shut.

1151
01:12:13,723 --> 01:12:18,923
[CYRUS] Same problem, finished. It's called the Monitor-Top, because it looks like the turret on the

1152
01:12:18,923 --> 01:12:24,123
[CYRUS] Civil War ironclad, and it's the first refrigerator that really sells — over a million

1153
01:12:24,123 --> 01:12:29,323
[CYRUS] of them. Five hundred and twenty-five dollars at launch, which is a lot; a refrigerator

1154
01:12:29,323 --> 01:12:34,523
[CYRUS] in nineteen twenty cost about six hundred dollars, more than a Model T runabout. By

1155
01:12:34,523 --> 01:12:39,723
[CYRUS] nineteen twenty-nine the average advertised price is under three hundred. By nineteen forty, about a

1156
01:12:39,723 --> 01:12:44,923
[CYRUS] hundred and fifty.

1157
01:12:44,923 --> 01:12:50,683
[JEFF] [reflective] Okay, but here's the thing I want to spend a minute on, because it's

1158
01:12:50,683 --> 01:12:56,443
[JEFF] the part I genuinely couldn't get my head around going in. The refrigerator goes from

1159
01:12:56,443 --> 01:13:02,203
[JEFF] a rich person's toy to nearly half of American homes during the nineteen thirties. During

1160
01:13:02,203 --> 01:13:07,963
[JEFF] the Depression. Who's paying for it? Because the family with a wire to the house

1161
01:13:07,963 --> 01:13:13,723
[JEFF] in nineteen thirty-three doesn't have a hundred and fifty dollars sitting around.

1162
01:13:13,723 --> 01:13:19,208
[CYRUS] [deliberate] So this is my favorite business-model story in this whole thing, and the answer

1163
01:13:19,208 --> 01:13:24,694
[CYRUS] is: the electric company. And I want to actually do the math, because when I

1164
01:13:24,694 --> 01:13:30,180
[CYRUS] first read that the utilities were financing refrigerators, I assumed it was some kind of

1165
01:13:30,180 --> 01:13:35,665
[CYRUS] public-service thing. It is not. Start with what a house used. In the mid-nineteen thirties,

1166
01:13:35,665 --> 01:13:41,151
[CYRUS] a home with lights and a radio and a few small appliances draws something like

1167
01:13:41,151 --> 01:13:46,637
[CYRUS] fifteen to forty kilowatt-hours a month. A refrigerator of that era draws thirty to forty-five

1168
01:13:46,637 --> 01:13:52,123
[CYRUS] kilowatt-hours a month — by itself.

1169
01:13:52,123 --> 01:13:54,123
[JEFF] So it doubles the load.

1170
01:13:54,123 --> 01:13:59,723
[CYRUS] It doubles or triples the household's consumption. Forever. At the marginal rates of the time

1171
01:13:59,723 --> 01:14:05,323
[CYRUS] — four to six cents a kilowatt-hour — that's a dollar-twenty to two-seventy a month

1172
01:14:05,323 --> 01:14:10,923
[CYRUS] in new revenue, from one appliance, for as long as it runs. Over a ten-year

1173
01:14:10,923 --> 01:14:16,523
[CYRUS] life, that's a hundred and fifty to three hundred dollars — which is what the

1174
01:14:16,523 --> 01:14:22,123
[CYRUS] box cost. So the utility that helps you buy a refrigerator gets the price of

1175
01:14:22,123 --> 01:14:27,723
[CYRUS] the refrigerator back in electricity. And then keeps collecting.

1176
01:14:27,723 --> 01:14:31,723
[JEFF] So they'd happily eat the cost of the box.

1177
01:14:31,723 --> 01:14:37,234
[CYRUS] They would happily give away the razor to sell you the blades — except in

1178
01:14:37,234 --> 01:14:42,745
[CYRUS] this case the customer pays for the razor too, on installments, on the same bill

1179
01:14:42,745 --> 01:14:48,256
[CYRUS] as the blades. Commonwealth Edison's refrigeration sales supervisor in Chicago says it in his own

1180
01:14:48,256 --> 01:14:53,767
[CYRUS] words in nineteen thirty-five: "For Commonwealth Edison Company it is the terms that close the

1181
01:14:53,767 --> 01:14:59,278
[CYRUS] deal — thirty-five months to pay, with charges coming on the electric bill." And a

1182
01:14:59,278 --> 01:15:04,789
[CYRUS] Montana utility's appliance ad: "One dollar down and one dollar per month, to be paid

1183
01:15:04,789 --> 01:15:10,300
[CYRUS] with your electric light bill." A Chicago department store had even spent three years selling

1184
01:15:10,300 --> 01:15:15,811
[CYRUS] refrigerators with a coin meter on the box — you fed it nickels the way

1185
01:15:15,811 --> 01:15:21,323
[CYRUS] you'd paid the iceman.

1186
01:15:21,323 --> 01:15:22,923
[JEFF] Meter ice.

1187
01:15:22,923 --> 01:15:28,923
[CYRUS] And the trade press worried the terms were getting too easy. A nineteen thirty-four editorial

1188
01:15:28,923 --> 01:15:34,923
[CYRUS] frets that "the housewife will begin to regard ten-cents-a-day for refrigeration as a gross extravagance."

1189
01:15:34,923 --> 01:15:40,523
[JEFF] That is the free market being genuinely clever. The incentive is perfectly aligned — it

1190
01:15:40,523 --> 01:15:46,123
[JEFF] reminds me of every software company that rediscovered this eighty years later, where the box

1191
01:15:46,123 --> 01:15:51,723
[JEFF] is just the onboarding cost. And it's not the government doing it.

1192
01:15:51,723 --> 01:15:57,037
[CYRUS] Well — the government showed them how. Nineteen thirty-four, the New Deal sets up a

1193
01:15:57,037 --> 01:16:02,351
[CYRUS] thing called the Electric Home and Farm Authority: the dealer sells you the box, hands

1194
01:16:02,351 --> 01:16:07,665
[CYRUS] the contract to your utility, and the utility pays the dealer and collects from you

1195
01:16:07,665 --> 01:16:12,980
[CYRUS] on the light bill. They launched it in Tupelo, Mississippi, with an eighty-dollar refrigerator, and

1196
01:16:12,980 --> 01:16:18,294
[CYRUS] sold a hundred and thirty-seven of them in two weeks. The federal program itself stayed

1197
01:16:18,294 --> 01:16:23,608
[CYRUS] small — a few thousand contracts. What mattered is that every utility in the country

1198
01:16:23,608 --> 01:16:28,923
[CYRUS] saw the model.

1199
01:16:28,923 --> 01:16:30,523
[JEFF] And the sales pitch?

1200
01:16:30,523 --> 01:16:35,963
[CYRUS] The sales pitch is the best part. The salesmen worked evenings — three nights a

1201
01:16:35,963 --> 01:16:41,403
[CYRUS] week — because that's when both husband and wife were home; the trade press says

1202
01:16:41,403 --> 01:16:46,843
[CYRUS] "the husband and wife factor is essential." And they carried a worksheet to do the

1203
01:16:46,843 --> 01:16:52,283
[CYRUS] family's arithmetic. And the first line on the worksheet — the very first thing the

1204
01:16:52,283 --> 01:16:57,723
[CYRUS] salesman crosses out — is "Ice formerly purchased."

1205
01:16:57,723 --> 01:17:02,123
[JEFF] So the iceman is on the worksheet. As a savings.

1206
01:17:02,123 --> 01:17:06,789
[CYRUS] Line one. Then "food spoilage prevented," then "savings from buying a week's supply instead of

1207
01:17:06,789 --> 01:17:11,456
[CYRUS] one day or one meal at a time." Minus operating cost, equals net savings per

1208
01:17:11,456 --> 01:17:16,123
[CYRUS] month, versus your monthly payment.

1209
01:17:16,123 --> 01:17:21,780
[JEFF] [reflective] So let's talk about the iceman for a second. Because this is the pattern

1210
01:17:21,780 --> 01:17:27,437
[JEFF] in this story, right — the winner of the last round is the resister in

1211
01:17:27,437 --> 01:17:33,094
[JEFF] the next one. And the machine-ice industry in nineteen nineteen is at its all-time peak.

1212
01:17:33,094 --> 01:17:38,751
[JEFF] The census that year counts nearly three thousand ice plants, about thirty-eight thousand people, making

1213
01:17:38,751 --> 01:17:44,408
[JEFF] twenty-eight million tons of ice — six and a half times what they'd made twenty

1214
01:17:44,408 --> 01:17:50,065
[JEFF] years earlier. Machine ice had finally beaten the ponds. And at that exact moment, Kelvinator

1215
01:17:50,065 --> 01:17:55,723
[JEFF] and Frigidaire are shipping their first few thousand units.

1216
01:17:55,723 --> 01:17:57,723
[CYRUS] And the ice industry fights.

1217
01:17:57,723 --> 01:18:03,723
[JEFF] It does, and every argument it makes is true. Electric boxes are dangerous — true,

1218
01:18:03,723 --> 01:18:09,723
[JEFF] until nineteen thirty. They're expensive — true. They're unreliable — Commonwealth Edison tried selling them

1219
01:18:09,723 --> 01:18:15,723
[JEFF] in nineteen twenty-three and installed two hundred and seventy-nine in two years; the product wasn't

1220
01:18:15,723 --> 01:18:21,723
[JEFF] ready. And ice keeps food better — moist air, no drying out. And here's the

1221
01:18:21,723 --> 01:18:27,723
[JEFF] detail I found delicious. The ice industry's trade association underwrote the government's former cold-chain authority.

1222
01:18:27,723 --> 01:18:33,723
[JEFF] Mary Engle Pennington spent the nineteen twenties writing consumer pamphlets with the ice industry's backing.

1223
01:18:33,723 --> 01:18:37,323
[CYRUS] [incredulous] The Ice Woman worked for the ice men.

1224
01:18:37,323 --> 01:18:41,856
[JEFF] [amused] Right about every specific. Wrong about the trend. In nineteen forty, twenty-seven percent of

1225
01:18:41,856 --> 01:18:46,389
[JEFF] American homes still ran on ice. Within about a decade of the war, big-city home

1226
01:18:46,389 --> 01:18:50,923
[JEFF] ice delivery was gone.

1227
01:18:50,923 --> 01:18:54,123
[CYRUS] So — the curve. Let me read it, because I still don't think you believe

1228
01:18:54,123 --> 01:18:57,323
[CYRUS] it.

1229
01:18:57,323 --> 01:19:00,123
[JEFF] I don't. I read it three times.

1230
01:19:00,123 --> 01:19:05,056
[CYRUS] In nineteen thirty, roughly one American home in twelve has a refrigerator. The nineteen forty

1231
01:19:05,056 --> 01:19:09,989
[CYRUS] census — this is the census, not an estimate — forty-four percent mechanical. Twenty-seven percent

1232
01:19:09,989 --> 01:19:14,923
[CYRUS] still on ice. And twenty-seven percent nothing.

1233
01:19:14,923 --> 01:19:15,723
[JEFF] [skeptical] Nothing?

1234
01:19:15,723 --> 01:19:20,823
[CYRUS] Nothing. In nineteen forty, more than one American home in four had no refrigeration of

1235
01:19:20,823 --> 01:19:25,923
[CYRUS] any kind. Not a refrigerator, not an icebox. In the South, it's forty percent with

1236
01:19:25,923 --> 01:19:31,023
[CYRUS] nothing. Among Black households, half. And by nineteen fifty, the national number is about four

1237
01:19:31,023 --> 01:19:36,123
[CYRUS] homes in five with a refrigerator.

1238
01:19:36,123 --> 01:19:40,656
[JEFF] See, and this is what doesn't fit my mental model of the Depression. One in

1239
01:19:40,656 --> 01:19:45,189
[JEFF] twelve to nearly one in two, in the ten worst years in American economic history.

1240
01:19:45,189 --> 01:19:49,723
[JEFF] That doesn't seem possible.

1241
01:19:49,723 --> 01:19:54,923
[CYRUS] And the anchor makes it believable, I think. Radio went from about forty percent of

1242
01:19:54,923 --> 01:20:00,123
[CYRUS] homes in nineteen thirty to eighty-three percent in nineteen forty — the same pace, a

1243
01:20:00,123 --> 01:20:05,323
[CYRUS] decade earlier. Television was faster — two percent in nineteen forty-nine, fifty-nine percent five years

1244
01:20:05,323 --> 01:20:10,523
[CYRUS] later. And the telephone was way slower — it didn't reach half of American homes

1245
01:20:10,523 --> 01:20:15,723
[CYRUS] until after the Second World War, seventy years after Bell. So the fridge is radio-speed.

1246
01:20:15,723 --> 01:20:20,923
[CYRUS] Not a miracle.

1247
01:20:20,923 --> 01:20:23,723
[JEFF] But radio didn't cost six hundred dollars.

1248
01:20:23,723 --> 01:20:29,056
[CYRUS] Neither did the fridge by nineteen thirty-five. That's the answer to your question. Four things

1249
01:20:29,056 --> 01:20:34,389
[CYRUS] stacked. Deflation — the price of the box fell from six hundred dollars to a

1250
01:20:34,389 --> 01:20:39,723
[CYRUS] hundred and fifty, faster than wages fell. The sealed dome — no more service calls.

1251
01:20:39,723 --> 01:20:45,056
[CYRUS] The safe gas — no more dead families. And thirty-five months on the light bill.

1252
01:20:45,056 --> 01:20:50,389
[CYRUS] Any one of those and it's a luxury. All four and it's in half the

1253
01:20:50,389 --> 01:20:55,723
[CYRUS] country's kitchens in a decade.

1254
01:20:55,723 --> 01:21:01,473
[JEFF] Yeah. And I want to name the cost of that plainly, because I think we

1255
01:21:01,473 --> 01:21:07,223
[JEFF] owe it to the guy. The iceman had a route. He had a horse, later

1256
01:21:07,223 --> 01:21:12,973
[JEFF] a truck. He had customers who knew his name and kids who knew the sound

1257
01:21:12,973 --> 01:21:18,723
[JEFF] of his wagon. And the electric company sold the box that ended him, and profited

1258
01:21:18,723 --> 01:21:24,473
[JEFF] on both ends of it — the installment and the kilowatt-hours. A real person lost

1259
01:21:24,473 --> 01:21:30,223
[JEFF] his living. And the system flourished — cheaper food, safer milk, the weekly shop instead

1260
01:21:30,223 --> 01:21:35,973
[JEFF] of buying one meal at a time. Both of those are true. I don't think

1261
01:21:35,973 --> 01:21:41,723
[JEFF] you apologize for the second to say the first.

1262
01:21:41,723 --> 01:21:47,256
[CYRUS] No. And you don't skip the first to celebrate the second. And the shape of

1263
01:21:47,256 --> 01:21:52,789
[CYRUS] the kitchen changes with him, by the way. Daily marketing becomes the weekly shop. The

1264
01:21:52,789 --> 01:21:58,322
[CYRUS] first supermarket — King Kullen, in Queens — opens in August of nineteen thirty, ten

1265
01:21:58,322 --> 01:22:03,856
[CYRUS] times the size of an A and P, with a parking lot. And that same

1266
01:22:03,856 --> 01:22:09,389
[CYRUS] year, in March, in Springfield, Massachusetts, a man named Clarence Birdseye put twenty-seven frozen products

1267
01:22:09,389 --> 01:22:14,922
[CYRUS] into a grocery store for the first time.

1268
01:22:14,922 --> 01:22:19,722
[JEFF] [amused] Which is a whole other story for another day. Okay — so. The safe

1269
01:22:19,722 --> 01:22:24,522
[JEFF] gas. We've been walking around it for twenty minutes.

1270
01:22:24,522 --> 01:22:30,122
[CYRUS] [deliberate] So — General Motors owns Frigidaire, and Frigidaire has a problem, which is that

1271
01:22:30,122 --> 01:22:35,722
[CYRUS] its product occasionally kills its customers. Charles Kettering, who runs G-M research, has a chemist

1272
01:22:35,722 --> 01:22:41,322
[CYRUS] named Thomas Midgley. And Midgley is a character — Cornell mechanical engineer, not a chemist

1273
01:22:41,322 --> 01:22:46,922
[CYRUS] by training. As a high-school pitcher he went looking for a substance to make his

1274
01:22:46,922 --> 01:22:52,522
[CYRUS] spitball break harder and found slippery-elm bark. His prep-school chemistry teacher gave him a periodic

1275
01:22:52,522 --> 01:22:58,122
[CYRUS] table, and he carried a copy for the rest of his life. And in nineteen

1276
01:22:58,122 --> 01:23:03,722
[CYRUS] twenty-one he'd already given the world leaded gasoline.

1277
01:23:03,722 --> 01:23:07,722
[JEFF] [dry] So one for one on the environment so far.

1278
01:23:07,722 --> 01:23:13,472
[CYRUS] We'll come to that. Kettering says it "took a whole Saturday afternoon to sell Midge

1279
01:23:13,472 --> 01:23:19,222
[CYRUS] on the idea that this was quite an important project." Fall of nineteen twenty-eight, Midgley

1280
01:23:19,222 --> 01:23:24,972
[CYRUS] and a chemist named Albert Henne are in a lab at Ohio State, and Midgley

1281
01:23:24,972 --> 01:23:30,722
[CYRUS] starts from the table. Flammability drops as you move to the right across the periodic

1282
01:23:30,722 --> 01:23:36,472
[CYRUS] table. Toxicity drops as you move up. And the two arrows cross at one element:

1283
01:23:36,472 --> 01:23:42,222
[CYRUS] fluorine. Which everybody knew was hideously dangerous — Kettering says he went ahead "in spite

1284
01:23:42,222 --> 01:23:47,972
[CYRUS] of warnings about the hazardous nature of fluorine." And within days they've made a compound

1285
01:23:47,972 --> 01:23:53,722
[CYRUS] called dichlorodifluoromethane. Which nobody can say, so it becomes Freon.

1286
01:23:53,722 --> 01:23:56,122
[JEFF] [curious] And it just — worked?

1287
01:23:56,122 --> 01:24:02,056
[CYRUS] It nearly didn't. This is the part that gives me chills. They made three or

1288
01:24:02,056 --> 01:24:07,989
[CYRUS] four small batches with the raw material they had, and tested them on animals. Only

1289
01:24:07,989 --> 01:24:13,922
[CYRUS] the first batch was non-toxic. Every animal exposed to the others died — exactly the

1290
01:24:13,922 --> 01:24:19,856
[CYRUS] way everyone expected a fluorine compound to behave. It turned out to be an impurity

1291
01:24:19,856 --> 01:24:25,789
[CYRUS] in the ingredients, not the Freon. Kettering's line is: "Fortunately, though, it was the first

1292
01:24:25,789 --> 01:24:31,722
[CYRUS] batch which turned out well." If they'd made batch two first, they walk away.

1293
01:24:31,722 --> 01:24:35,722
[JEFF] That is truly nuts. I can't believe that's real.

1294
01:24:35,722 --> 01:24:41,242
[CYRUS] And then the demonstration. Nineteen thirty, American Chemical Society meeting. Midgley presents the first paper

1295
01:24:41,242 --> 01:24:46,762
[CYRUS] on fluorine refrigerants, and to prove the two properties at once — Kettering's words —

1296
01:24:46,762 --> 01:24:52,282
[CYRUS] "he demonstrated both their nontoxic and their noninflammable properties in one breath, so to speak."

1297
01:24:52,282 --> 01:24:57,802
[CYRUS] He takes a lungful of Freon vapor, and softly exhales it onto a candle burning

1298
01:24:57,802 --> 01:25:03,322
[CYRUS] in front of him. And the flame goes out.

1299
01:25:03,322 --> 01:25:08,522
[JEFF] [genuinely surprised] He breathed it in and blew out a candle with it.

1300
01:25:08,522 --> 01:25:13,402
[CYRUS] Non-toxic and non-flammable, in one breath. The two things every earlier refrigerant wasn't. Kettering called

1301
01:25:13,402 --> 01:25:18,282
[CYRUS] them "the only such compounds known which are stable, non-inflammable, and completely nontoxic." G-M and

1302
01:25:18,282 --> 01:25:23,162
[CYRUS] DuPont form a joint company to make it, and within five years the makers using

1303
01:25:23,162 --> 01:25:28,042
[CYRUS] Freon have sold something like eight million refrigerators. Before long, essentially every new box in

1304
01:25:28,042 --> 01:25:32,922
[CYRUS] America.

1305
01:25:32,922 --> 01:25:37,622
[JEFF] [reflective] So after a century of fluids that burned or poisoned — you finally have

1306
01:25:37,622 --> 01:25:42,322
[JEFF] one that does nothing. It boils at the right temperature. It carries heat. It doesn't

1307
01:25:42,322 --> 01:25:47,022
[JEFF] eat the metal. It doesn't burn. It doesn't kill you. It passed every test the

1308
01:25:47,022 --> 01:25:51,722
[JEFF] engineers wrote.

1309
01:25:51,722 --> 01:25:57,189
[CYRUS] Every test they'd thought to write. And by nineteen fifty, four out of five American

1310
01:25:57,189 --> 01:26:02,656
[CYRUS] kitchens have a sealed steel box humming in the corner, and the gas inside it

1311
01:26:02,656 --> 01:26:08,122
[CYRUS] is the safest chemical anyone has ever put in a home.

1312
01:26:08,122 --> 01:26:11,722
[JEFF] It failed one test. Nobody had written it yet.

1313
01:26:11,722 --> 01:26:15,722
[CYRUS] Don't last a century. And don't reach the sky.

1314
01:16:40,538 --> 01:16:45,658
[JEFF] [reflective] November second, nineteen forty-four. Worthington, Ohio. Thomas Midgley is fifty-five. Four years earlier he'd

1315
01:16:45,658 --> 01:16:50,778
[JEFF] caught polio — at fifty-one, which almost never happens — and it left him severely

1316
01:16:50,778 --> 01:16:55,898
[JEFF] disabled. So, being an engineer, he designed a system of ropes and pulleys to lift

1317
01:16:55,898 --> 01:17:01,018
[JEFF] himself out of bed. And that morning he was found strangled in it. Tangled in

1318
01:17:01,018 --> 01:17:06,138
[JEFF] the harness he'd built.

1319
01:17:06,138 --> 01:17:08,138
[CYRUS] In his own machine.

1320
01:17:08,138 --> 01:17:13,204
[JEFF] Yeah. He'd been elected president of the American Chemical Society that same year. He wrote

1321
01:17:13,204 --> 01:17:18,271
[JEFF] his own epitaph in his last address to them: "This one did a lot of

1322
01:17:18,271 --> 01:17:23,338
[JEFF] living in a mighty little while." He died believing he'd made the world safer twice

1323
01:17:23,338 --> 01:17:28,404
[JEFF] — leaded gasoline, and Freon. And an environmental historian named J-R McNeill would later write

1324
01:17:28,404 --> 01:17:33,471
[JEFF] that Midgley "had more impact on the atmosphere than any other single organism in earth

1325
01:17:33,471 --> 01:17:38,538
[JEFF] history."

1326
01:17:38,538 --> 01:17:42,938
[CYRUS] [exhales] That is a remarkable sentence to have written about you.

1327
01:17:42,938 --> 01:17:47,338
[JEFF] [reflective] It is. And to understand why it's true, you have to jump thirty years,

1328
01:17:47,338 --> 01:17:51,738
[JEFF] to a chemistry lab in Southern California.

1329
01:17:51,738 --> 01:17:57,738
[CYRUS] [deliberate] So here's the question that nobody had asked in forty-five years of making this

1330
01:17:57,738 --> 01:18:03,738
[CYRUS] stuff. Where does it go? A molecule of Freon doesn't burn. Doesn't react with anything.

1331
01:18:03,738 --> 01:18:09,738
[CYRUS] Doesn't dissolve in water. That's the whole point of it — that's why Midgley blew

1332
01:18:09,738 --> 01:18:15,738
[CYRUS] out a candle with it. So when it leaks out of your fridge, or you

1333
01:18:15,738 --> 01:18:21,738
[CYRUS] spray it out of a can of hairspray, it just... stays. And in nineteen seventy-two,

1334
01:18:21,738 --> 01:18:27,738
[CYRUS] a chemistry professor at U-C Irvine named Sherwood Rowland — everybody called him Sherry —

1335
01:18:27,738 --> 01:18:33,738
[CYRUS] is at a meeting where he hears a British scientist named Jim Lovelock present measurements.

1336
01:18:33,738 --> 01:18:39,738
[CYRUS] Lovelock had gone looking for these molecules in the air and found them everywhere. At

1337
01:18:39,738 --> 01:18:45,738
[CYRUS] levels suggesting that essentially every molecule of the stuff ever made was still up there.

1338
01:18:45,738 --> 01:18:47,338
[JEFF] [curious] All of it?

1339
01:18:47,338 --> 01:18:52,938
[CYRUS] Essentially all of it. And Rowland's reaction was — his words — "I knew that

1340
01:18:52,938 --> 01:18:58,538
[CYRUS] such a molecule could not remain inert in the atmosphere forever." Something eventually breaks it.

1341
01:18:58,538 --> 01:19:04,138
[CYRUS] So he puts it on his list of possible projects for a new postdoc. And

1342
01:19:04,138 --> 01:19:09,738
[CYRUS] the postdoc who picks it is Mario Molina. Thirty years old, born in Mexico City,

1343
01:19:09,738 --> 01:19:15,338
[CYRUS] fresh Berkeley doctorate in chemical lasers. And he'd walked away from lasers on purpose —

1344
01:19:15,338 --> 01:19:20,938
[CYRUS] he wrote later that he was "dismayed by the fact that high-power chemical lasers were

1345
01:19:20,938 --> 01:19:26,538
[CYRUS] being developed elsewhere as weapons." He wanted to work on something useful. And off Rowland's

1346
01:19:26,538 --> 01:19:32,138
[CYRUS] list he picks the boring one: finding out the environmental fate of these very inert

1347
01:19:32,138 --> 01:19:37,738
[CYRUS] industrial chemicals. Where do they go.

1348
01:19:37,738 --> 01:19:40,138
[JEFF] And how long does it take?

1349
01:19:40,138 --> 01:19:45,538
[CYRUS] Three months. Molina's own line about it later was, "We really sort of stumbled onto

1350
01:19:45,538 --> 01:19:50,938
[CYRUS] a problem of global proportions." Here's the chemistry. In the lower atmosphere nothing touches these

1351
01:19:50,938 --> 01:19:56,338
[CYRUS] molecules, so they drift upward for years. Eventually they get above most of the ozone

1352
01:19:56,338 --> 01:20:01,738
[CYRUS] layer, into the stratosphere, where for the first time they meet ultraviolet light hard enough

1353
01:20:01,738 --> 01:20:07,138
[CYRUS] to knock a chlorine atom loose. And that chlorine atom hits an ozone molecule and

1354
01:20:07,138 --> 01:20:12,538
[CYRUS] breaks it — takes one of its oxygens. Then it hits a free oxygen atom

1355
01:20:12,538 --> 01:20:17,938
[CYRUS] and gives that oxygen back. And now the chlorine is exactly as it was. Ready

1356
01:20:17,938 --> 01:20:23,338
[CYRUS] to go again.

1357
01:20:23,338 --> 01:20:26,538
[JEFF] [skeptical] Wait — so it's not used up?

1358
01:20:26,538 --> 01:20:32,423
[CYRUS] No. It's a catalyst. That's the whole horror of it. The E-P-A's figure is that

1359
01:20:32,423 --> 01:20:38,309
[CYRUS] one chlorine atom can destroy over a hundred thousand ozone molecules before something finally removes

1360
01:20:38,309 --> 01:20:44,195
[CYRUS] it. And the paper's own estimate for how long a C-F-C molecule lasts in the

1361
01:20:44,195 --> 01:20:50,080
[CYRUS] atmosphere was forty to a hundred and fifty years. Which means the sky is a

1362
01:20:50,080 --> 01:20:55,966
[CYRUS] cumulative account. A few thousand Freon fridges in nineteen thirty-two never mattered. Eighty percent of

1363
01:20:55,966 --> 01:21:01,852
[CYRUS] American kitchens, plus every car air conditioner, plus every spray can, for forty years —

1364
01:21:01,852 --> 01:21:07,738
[CYRUS] that mattered, and the bill was still arriving decades after the last can.

1365
01:21:07,738 --> 01:21:08,538
[JEFF] Scale.

1366
01:21:08,538 --> 01:21:13,204
[CYRUS] And that winter, Rowland comes home from the lab and — as his wife Joan

1367
01:21:13,204 --> 01:21:17,871
[CYRUS] later remembered it — tells her, "The work is going well, but it looks like

1368
01:21:17,871 --> 01:21:22,538
[CYRUS] the end of the world."

1369
01:21:22,538 --> 01:21:26,938
[JEFF] [genuinely surprised] That's — he said that at the dinner table?

1370
01:21:26,938 --> 01:21:31,871
[CYRUS] At home, to his wife. And Joan went through the house and threw out every

1371
01:21:31,871 --> 01:21:36,804
[CYRUS] spray can. That's the first policy response to ozone depletion. One woman, in a kitchen

1372
01:21:36,804 --> 01:21:41,738
[CYRUS] in Orange County, throwing out the hairspray.

1373
01:21:41,738 --> 01:21:44,938
[JEFF] And then the world catches up?

1374
01:21:44,938 --> 01:21:50,252
[CYRUS] Slowly. They publish in Nature in June of nineteen seventy-four. That September, at the American

1375
01:21:50,252 --> 01:21:55,566
[CYRUS] Chemical Society, they call for a complete ban on releasing the stuff. Which was not

1376
01:21:55,566 --> 01:22:00,880
[CYRUS] what chemists did — you published and you stopped. Rowland's answer, years later, was: "Is

1377
01:22:00,880 --> 01:22:06,195
[CYRUS] it enough for a scientist simply to publish a paper? If not us, who? If

1378
01:22:06,195 --> 01:22:11,509
[CYRUS] not now, when?" And the response is — he doesn't get invited to give a

1379
01:22:11,509 --> 01:22:16,823
[CYRUS] talk for a decade. Industry is furious. DuPont's chairman calls the theory "a science fiction

1380
01:22:16,823 --> 01:22:22,138
[CYRUS] tale... utter nonsense."

1381
01:22:22,138 --> 01:22:27,738
[JEFF] [reflective] And this is where I want to slow down, because I think it's easy

1382
01:22:27,738 --> 01:22:33,338
[JEFF] to make DuPont the villain here, and I think that's the wrong read — at

1383
01:22:33,338 --> 01:22:38,938
[JEFF] least in nineteen seventy-five. DuPont takes out a full-page ad. And the sentence in it

1384
01:22:38,938 --> 01:22:44,538
[JEFF] that I think is the important one is this: "Should reputable evidence show that some

1385
01:22:44,538 --> 01:22:50,138
[JEFF] fluorocarbons cause a health hazard through depletion of the ozone layer, we are prepared to

1386
01:22:50,138 --> 01:22:55,738
[JEFF] stop production of the offending compounds." Now — in nineteen seventy-five, nobody had measured any

1387
01:22:55,738 --> 01:23:01,338
[JEFF] ozone loss anywhere. The theory rested on lab rate constants and a model. The National

1388
01:23:01,338 --> 01:23:06,938
[JEFF] Academy of Sciences looked at it in nineteen seventy-six, said the theory was probably right,

1389
01:23:06,938 --> 01:23:12,538
[JEFF] and recommended waiting two more years for data before regulating. So DuPont's position was "show

1390
01:23:12,538 --> 01:23:18,138
[JEFF] us the evidence." And that's not a lie. That's — honestly — what science is

1391
01:23:18,138 --> 01:23:23,738
[JEFF] supposed to sound like.

1392
01:23:23,738 --> 01:23:28,538
[CYRUS] [skeptical] Except they also spent the next decade lobbying against regulating it.

1393
01:23:28,538 --> 01:23:33,852
[JEFF] Yeah, they did. And they were wrong about the trend. But here's the thing I

1394
01:23:33,852 --> 01:23:39,166
[JEFF] keep coming back to on this: when science turns out to have been incomplete, people

1395
01:23:39,166 --> 01:23:44,480
[JEFF] read that as science being wrong, or corrupt. And that's a fundamental misunderstanding of what

1396
01:23:44,480 --> 01:23:49,795
[JEFF] science is. It's a continual iteration on incomplete information. "Too early to draw conclusions" wasn't

1397
01:23:49,795 --> 01:23:55,109
[JEFF] a cop-out in nineteen seventy-five. It was accurate. The question is what you do when

1398
01:23:55,109 --> 01:24:00,423
[JEFF] the evidence changes. And hold onto that pledge, because we're going to find out whether

1399
01:24:00,423 --> 01:24:05,738
[JEFF] they kept it.

1400
01:24:05,738 --> 01:24:11,404
[CYRUS] So here's who moved first, and it wasn't governments. Consumers. Before there was any ban

1401
01:24:11,404 --> 01:24:17,071
[CYRUS] at all, aerosol sales fell sharply. People just stopped buying spray cans. The industry, in

1402
01:24:17,071 --> 01:24:22,738
[CYRUS] the words of one historian, "feeling invulnerable, was not prepared for such a strong public

1403
01:24:22,738 --> 01:24:28,404
[CYRUS] and political reaction." And the structural reason it could happen so fast is that hairspray

1404
01:24:28,404 --> 01:24:34,071
[CYRUS] is nonessential and there was a pump bottle on the shelf right next to it.

1405
01:24:34,071 --> 01:24:39,738
[CYRUS] Substitutes on the shelf, right? Culture moved faster than regulation.

1406
01:24:39,738 --> 01:24:41,338
[JEFF] And the regulation followed.

1407
01:24:41,338 --> 01:24:46,988
[CYRUS] The United States banned nonessential aerosol C-F-Cs in nineteen seventy-eight — less than five years

1408
01:24:46,988 --> 01:24:52,638
[CYRUS] from the paper. Canada and the Scandinavians did the same. And then it stalled. Because

1409
01:24:52,638 --> 01:24:58,288
[CYRUS] a refrigerator is not hairspray. Nobody was going to give up the box, there was

1410
01:24:58,288 --> 01:25:03,938
[CYRUS] no substitute on the shelf, and the chemical industry argued — persuasively — that it

1411
01:25:03,938 --> 01:25:09,588
[CYRUS] needed time to develop one. And Europe balked. France and Britain were major producers, and

1412
01:25:09,588 --> 01:25:15,238
[CYRUS] they'd just watched the United States kill its own supersonic transport partly on ozone grounds

1413
01:25:15,238 --> 01:25:20,888
[CYRUS] and then fight Concorde's landing rights. So they suspected an American trick. One historian's phrase

1414
01:25:20,888 --> 01:25:26,538
[CYRUS] is that they saw it as "environmental neocolonialism."

1415
01:25:26,538 --> 01:25:30,938
[JEFF] [reflective] Which, from where they sat, was not a crazy read.

1416
01:25:30,938 --> 01:25:36,458
[CYRUS] No, it wasn't. And DuPont's position in nineteen seventy-nine was: "No ozone depletion has ever

1417
01:25:36,458 --> 01:25:41,978
[CYRUS] been detected." Which was true. So by March nineteen eighty-five you get the Vienna Convention

1418
01:25:41,978 --> 01:25:47,498
[CYRUS] — forty-three countries agree that the ozone layer is important and that they should do

1419
01:25:47,498 --> 01:25:53,018
[CYRUS] something, with no actual controls. A framework with nothing in it. And two months later,

1420
01:25:53,018 --> 01:25:58,538
[CYRUS] three men on an ice shelf publish a paper.

1421
01:25:58,538 --> 01:26:01,738
[JEFF] [curious] This is your favorite part, isn't it.

1422
01:26:01,738 --> 01:26:07,649
[CYRUS] [warmly] This is one of my favorite stories in the history of science. Okay. Halley

1423
01:26:07,649 --> 01:26:13,560
[CYRUS] Research Station. British Antarctic Survey. It's on the Brunt Ice Shelf, in Antarctica, and since

1424
01:26:13,560 --> 01:26:19,471
[CYRUS] nineteen fifty-seven a physicist named Joe Farman has been running an instrument there called a

1425
01:26:19,471 --> 01:26:25,382
[CYRUS] Dobson spectrophotometer — a nineteen twenties design — that measures how much ozone is in

1426
01:26:25,382 --> 01:26:31,293
[CYRUS] the column of air overhead. And I need you to understand why it was there.

1427
01:26:31,293 --> 01:26:37,204
[CYRUS] It was not there to look for ozone depletion. It was set up to help

1428
01:26:37,204 --> 01:26:43,115
[CYRUS] with weather forecasting. And in the early nineteen eighties, the Survey was looking for things

1429
01:26:43,115 --> 01:26:49,026
[CYRUS] to cut, and the Halley ozone program was — Shanklin's phrase — "in the frame

1430
01:26:49,026 --> 01:26:54,938
[CYRUS] to be cut." Nothing seemed to be changing. Why keep paying for it?

1431
01:26:54,938 --> 01:26:55,738
[JEFF] Who's Shanklin?

1432
01:26:55,738 --> 01:27:01,288
[CYRUS] Jonathan Shanklin. In nineteen seventy-seven, the Survey posts a job ad: "Wanted: physicist with an

1433
01:27:01,288 --> 01:27:06,838
[CYRUS] interest in meteorology and programming skills." And he answers it, fresh out of Cambridge. And

1434
01:27:06,838 --> 01:27:12,388
[CYRUS] he writes later, "I had no background in meteorology, and no preconceived ideas of how

1435
01:27:12,388 --> 01:27:17,938
[CYRUS] the atmosphere behaved." His job is to clear a backlog. The Dobson readings were on

1436
01:27:17,938 --> 01:27:23,488
[CYRUS] handwritten sheets, and until then converting them into ozone numbers meant slide rules and log

1437
01:27:23,488 --> 01:27:29,038
[CYRUS] tables. So there's a decade of sheets nobody's processed. He supervises the digitizing and writes

1438
01:27:29,038 --> 01:27:34,588
[CYRUS] the code. And — his words — "By chance, the backlog covered the crucial decade

1439
01:27:34,588 --> 01:27:40,138
[CYRUS] when ozone levels began to drop."

1440
01:27:40,138 --> 01:27:42,138
[JEFF] [reflective] Nobody's looking for it.

1441
01:27:42,138 --> 01:27:47,838
[CYRUS] And this is the part. In nineteen eighty-three the Survey has a public open day,

1442
01:27:47,838 --> 01:27:53,538
[CYRUS] and Shanklin is asked to help because he has a teaching qualification. And the newspapers

1443
01:27:53,538 --> 01:27:59,238
[CYRUS] at the time are full of stories about spray cans and Concorde destroying the ozone

1444
01:27:59,238 --> 01:28:04,938
[CYRUS] layer. So he decides to make a graph to reassure the public. To show that

1445
01:28:04,938 --> 01:28:10,638
[CYRUS] the ozone over Halley that year is no different from twenty years earlier. And the

1446
01:28:10,638 --> 01:28:16,338
[CYRUS] graph does show that — overall. But he writes, "it seemed that the springtime values

1447
01:28:16,338 --> 01:28:22,038
[CYRUS] did look lower from one year to the next." His own line about it now

1448
01:28:22,038 --> 01:28:27,738
[CYRUS] is, "Being an ignorant physicist, I thought this unlikely."

1449
01:28:27,738 --> 01:28:33,338
[JEFF] He's trying to calm everybody down, and the data won't let him.

1450
01:28:33,338 --> 01:28:38,828
[CYRUS] And there's no eureka. He says that explicitly — "there was no real eureka moment

1451
01:28:38,828 --> 01:28:44,319
[CYRUS] in the discovery, more a combination of pieces falling into place." The theory said springtime

1452
01:28:44,319 --> 01:28:49,810
[CYRUS] Antarctic values were noisy, weather-dependent, don't read anything into them. Farman himself thought the instrument

1453
01:28:49,810 --> 01:28:55,301
[CYRUS] must be broken — until a replacement Dobson gave the same numbers. Shanklin, looking back

1454
01:28:55,301 --> 01:29:00,792
[CYRUS] on it recently: "The problem was the points were starting to fall off the top

1455
01:29:00,792 --> 01:29:06,283
[CYRUS] of the graph. Quite unusual in science, really." What finally convinced them was a plot

1456
01:29:06,283 --> 01:29:11,774
[CYRUS] of the lowest eleven-day average for each spring, which showed the decline was systematic. Farman

1457
01:29:11,774 --> 01:29:17,265
[CYRUS] worked out the chemistry connecting it to C-F-Cs. Brian Gardiner did the data quality. And

1458
01:29:17,265 --> 01:29:22,756
[CYRUS] they publish in Nature in May of nineteen eighty-five. Springtime ozone over Antarctica down about

1459
01:29:22,756 --> 01:29:28,247
[CYRUS] forty percent from the nineteen seventies. Where the models had predicted a few percent, globally,

1460
01:29:28,247 --> 01:29:33,738
[CYRUS] maybe.

1461
01:29:33,738 --> 01:29:39,438
[JEFF] Okay — so here's the question I actually wrote down when I read this, because

1462
01:29:39,438 --> 01:29:45,138
[JEFF] I've seen the version of this story where NASA's satellite had been seeing the hole

1463
01:29:45,138 --> 01:29:50,838
[JEFF] for years and the software was throwing the data away. Is that true? And if

1464
01:29:50,838 --> 01:29:56,538
[JEFF] it is — why was nobody following up on all those errors?

1465
01:29:56,538 --> 01:30:02,283
[CYRUS] [deliberate] So I chased this, because I'd heard that version too, and it's the kind

1466
01:30:02,283 --> 01:30:08,028
[CYRUS] of story that's too good. Here's what actually happened. NASA's satellite instrument did have a

1467
01:30:08,028 --> 01:30:13,774
[CYRUS] quality flag — any reading below a certain level got flagged as probably out of

1468
01:30:13,774 --> 01:30:19,519
[CYRUS] calibration. Flagged. Not deleted. The data was all still there. And in August of nineteen

1469
01:30:19,519 --> 01:30:25,265
[CYRUS] eighty-four, the team at Goddard processing the October eighty-three data noticed a huge spike in

1470
01:30:25,265 --> 01:30:31,010
[CYRUS] flagged readings over Antarctica, and they investigated. The obvious check was to compare against the

1471
01:30:31,010 --> 01:30:36,756
[CYRUS] ground readings from the South Pole station — and that ground data turned out to

1472
01:30:36,756 --> 01:30:42,501
[CYRUS] be bad, because the wrong channels had been read on the instrument. Meanwhile the rest

1473
01:30:42,501 --> 01:30:48,247
[CYRUS] of the world looked normal. So they had a puzzle they couldn't confirm. By December

1474
01:30:48,247 --> 01:30:53,992
[CYRUS] eighty-four they were confident enough to submit an abstract to a conference for the following

1475
01:30:53,992 --> 01:30:59,737
[CYRUS] August. And Farman's paper came out in May.

1476
01:30:59,737 --> 01:31:03,537
[JEFF] [amused] So they were more than a year behind three guys with an instrument designed

1477
01:31:03,537 --> 01:31:07,337
[JEFF] in the nineteen twenties.

1478
01:31:07,337 --> 01:31:12,995
[CYRUS] And no theory to protect — that's the real difference. The guy who wrote the

1479
01:31:12,995 --> 01:31:18,652
[CYRUS] best account of this called it "a tale of poor communications and siloed researchers." Siloes,

1480
01:31:18,652 --> 01:31:24,309
[CYRUS] not stupidity. And NASA then confirmed it — bigger. Their satellite map in nineteen eighty-six

1481
01:31:24,309 --> 01:31:29,966
[CYRUS] showed the hole was the size of a continent. Meanwhile Shanklin, after that nineteen eighty-three

1482
01:31:29,966 --> 01:31:35,623
[CYRUS] open day, had written a letter to the satellite data center asking whether they were

1483
01:31:35,623 --> 01:31:41,280
[CYRUS] seeing low values too. And I love his line about it: "I never received a

1484
01:31:41,280 --> 01:31:46,937
[CYRUS] reply — perhaps another lucky break for our team."

1485
01:31:46,937 --> 01:31:50,537
[JEFF] [amused] Nobody on the satellite side answered the mail.

1486
01:31:50,537 --> 01:31:56,177
[CYRUS] And one more piece, because it answers the obvious question — why Antarctica? Susan Solomon,

1487
01:31:56,177 --> 01:32:01,817
[CYRUS] a thirty-year-old chemist at NOAA, worked out in nineteen eighty-six that the answer was the

1488
01:32:01,817 --> 01:32:07,457
[CYRUS] clouds. Polar stratospheric clouds form only in the extreme cold of the Antarctic winter, and

1489
01:32:07,457 --> 01:32:13,097
[CYRUS] chemistry on the surface of those ice particles converts the "safe," stored forms of chlorine

1490
01:32:13,097 --> 01:32:18,737
[CYRUS] into the active form. So when the sun comes back in spring, the chlorine is

1491
01:32:18,737 --> 01:32:24,377
[CYRUS] primed and it goes through the ozone like a scythe. She led the expedition to

1492
01:32:24,377 --> 01:32:30,017
[CYRUS] McMurdo to measure it, and found chlorine oxide a hundred times higher than expected. Shanklin's

1493
01:32:30,017 --> 01:32:35,657
[CYRUS] own line about all this is that "it is humbling to think that industry across

1494
01:32:35,657 --> 01:32:41,297
[CYRUS] the world has had to change because of what seemed to be a small discovery

1495
01:32:41,297 --> 01:32:46,937
[CYRUS] over an obscure part of Antarctic."

1496
01:32:46,937 --> 01:32:52,537
[JEFF] [reflective] And then it moves fast. Which is the part I find genuinely astonishing. Because

1497
01:32:52,537 --> 01:32:58,137
[JEFF] two years — two years — after that paper, there's a treaty. And it very

1498
01:32:58,137 --> 01:33:03,737
[JEFF] nearly wasn't a strong one. Spring of nineteen eighty-seven, inside the Reagan administration, there's a

1499
01:33:03,737 --> 01:33:09,337
[JEFF] revolt. Interior, Commerce, Energy, the budget office — they want a freeze on production and

1500
01:33:09,337 --> 01:33:14,937
[JEFF] nothing more. And the Interior Secretary, Donald Hodel, floats what he calls a "personal protection

1501
01:33:14,937 --> 01:33:20,537
[JEFF] plan." Instead of a treaty — hats. Sunglasses. Sunscreen.

1502
01:33:20,537 --> 01:33:24,537
[CYRUS] [incredulous] He said that out loud? In a cabinet meeting?

1503
01:33:24,537 --> 01:33:30,337
[JEFF] Now — I want to give him his due for one second, because adaptation is

1504
01:33:30,337 --> 01:33:36,137
[JEFF] a real policy category, and his economists weren't wrong that a fifty-percent cut would cost

1505
01:33:36,137 --> 01:33:41,937
[JEFF] money. But the L-A Times gets hold of it in May, and the press christens

1506
01:33:41,937 --> 01:33:47,737
[JEFF] it the "Rayban Plan," and it dies on the dread test. Nobody wants to be

1507
01:33:47,737 --> 01:33:53,537
[JEFF] told the sky is the problem and the answer is a hat. And the other

1508
01:33:53,537 --> 01:33:59,337
[JEFF] side had a number — the E-P-A's own worst-case model, assuming no controls and sixty

1509
01:33:59,337 --> 01:34:05,137
[JEFF] more years of growth, projected well over a hundred million extra skin cancers in the

1510
01:34:05,137 --> 01:34:10,937
[JEFF] United States alone. That's a model, not a forecast. But it's the world the hat

1511
01:34:10,937 --> 01:34:16,737
[JEFF] was supposed to fix. The Senate passes a resolution for a fifty-percent cut almost unanimously.

1512
01:34:16,737 --> 01:34:22,537
[JEFF] And the Secretary of State, George Shultz, takes it to Reagan directly. And on June

1513
01:34:22,537 --> 01:34:28,337
[JEFF] twenty-fifth, nineteen eighty-seven, Reagan decides. Freeze at nineteen eighty-six levels, twenty-percent cut, then a further

1514
01:34:28,337 --> 01:34:34,137
[JEFF] thirty-percent cut. Against four of his own cabinet departments.

1515
01:34:34,137 --> 01:34:36,137
[CYRUS] [curious] Do we know why?

1516
01:34:36,137 --> 01:34:41,537
[JEFF] We know the decision memo is in Reagan's own handwriting. Beyond that — he'd had

1517
01:34:41,537 --> 01:34:46,937
[JEFF] skin cancer cut off his nose twice, in eighty-five and eighty-seven, and historians have wondered

1518
01:34:46,937 --> 01:34:52,337
[JEFF] whether that mattered. Nobody in the room wrote it down. But he did it. September

1519
01:34:52,337 --> 01:34:57,737
[JEFF] sixteenth, nineteen eighty-seven, two dozen nations sign the Montreal Protocol. Europe came along in part

1520
01:34:57,737 --> 01:35:03,137
[JEFF] because they feared American trade sanctions if they didn't. Reagan later called it "a model

1521
01:35:03,137 --> 01:35:08,537
[JEFF] of cooperation... a monumental achievement." And in two thousand nine the Montreal Protocol became the

1522
01:35:08,537 --> 01:35:13,937
[JEFF] first treaty in the history of the United Nations to be ratified by every single

1523
01:35:13,937 --> 01:35:19,337
[JEFF] country on earth.

1524
01:35:19,337 --> 01:35:25,237
[CYRUS] [deliberate] Which has never happened for anything else. And Shanklin — of all people —

1525
01:35:25,237 --> 01:35:31,137
[CYRUS] has the best analysis of why that worked, and I want to read it, because

1526
01:35:31,137 --> 01:35:37,037
[CYRUS] it's a scientist, not a pundit. He says the manufacturers were "able and willing, after

1527
01:35:37,037 --> 01:35:42,937
[CYRUS] some initial resistance, to produce substitutes." The evidence was clear. "The hole sounded threatening." There

1528
01:35:42,937 --> 01:35:48,837
[CYRUS] was a link to cancer. And then this: "the public did not feel bullied or

1529
01:35:48,837 --> 01:35:54,737
[CYRUS] threatened — no one was telling them to radically change their way of life. There

1530
01:35:54,737 --> 01:36:00,637
[CYRUS] was a problem, and something could be done about it." And elsewhere he puts it

1531
01:36:00,637 --> 01:36:06,537
[CYRUS] even more simply: with the ozone hole, "you could show politicians a picture."

1532
01:36:06,537 --> 01:36:12,537
[JEFF] And that last one is the whole game. Nobody had to give up their refrigerator.

1533
01:36:12,537 --> 01:36:18,537
[JEFF] Nobody had to change how they lived. The fix asked nothing of you. And the

1534
01:36:18,537 --> 01:36:24,537
[JEFF] other piece — no substitute, no treaty. DuPont had said in eighty-six that alternatives could

1535
01:36:24,537 --> 01:36:30,537
[JEFF] be ready within five years, given the right market conditions. And the treaty was the

1536
01:36:30,537 --> 01:36:36,537
[JEFF] market condition. It created the demand for the substitute. Which is a genuinely elegant thing

1537
01:36:36,537 --> 01:36:42,537
[JEFF] — a regulation that makes the market for the thing that makes the regulation possible.

1538
01:36:42,537 --> 01:36:44,137
[CYRUS] And now your pledge.

1539
01:36:44,137 --> 01:36:49,687
[JEFF] [amused] Now my pledge. March of nineteen eighty-eight. Twenty days that I think are one

1540
01:36:49,687 --> 01:36:55,237
[JEFF] of the best corporate stories I know. March fourth: DuPont's chairman, Richard Heckert, writes to

1541
01:36:55,237 --> 01:37:00,787
[JEFF] three senators — quote — "scientific evidence does not point to the need for dramatic

1542
01:37:00,787 --> 01:37:06,337
[JEFF] C-F-C emission reductions." March fifteenth: a NASA panel — the Ozone Trends Panel, an international

1543
01:37:06,337 --> 01:37:11,887
[JEFF] team of scientists — reports that it's not just Antarctica. Ozone over the northern mid-latitudes

1544
01:37:11,887 --> 01:37:17,437
[JEFF] — where the people live — is down two to three percent since nineteen sixty-nine,

1545
01:37:17,437 --> 01:37:22,987
[JEFF] up to six percent in winter, and it cannot be explained by anything natural. Where

1546
01:37:22,987 --> 01:37:28,537
[JEFF] the models had said essentially zero.

1547
01:37:28,537 --> 01:37:31,737
[CYRUS] And DuPont has a guy on that panel.

1548
01:37:31,737 --> 01:37:37,337
[JEFF] [mischievously] They do. Mack McFarland, their own atmospheric scientist — the only industry scientist on

1549
01:37:37,337 --> 01:37:42,937
[JEFF] it. He's read the draft. And he tells the company the link is now real.

1550
01:37:42,937 --> 01:37:48,537
[JEFF] March twenty-fourth — nine days later — DuPont, the world's largest producer of C-F-Cs, announces

1551
01:37:48,537 --> 01:37:54,137
[JEFF] it will phase them out entirely. Total phase-out. Twenty days from "the evidence doesn't point

1552
01:37:54,137 --> 01:37:59,737
[JEFF] to the need" to "we're getting out of the business."

1553
01:37:59,737 --> 01:38:05,737
[CYRUS] [genuinely surprised] Nine days after the report? That's not a corporate timeline. That's a memo.

1554
01:38:05,737 --> 01:38:11,497
[JEFF] And here's why I love it. That's not a confession. That's an update. The nineteen

1555
01:38:11,497 --> 01:38:17,257
[JEFF] seventy-five pledge — "should reputable evidence show, we are prepared to stop production" — they

1556
01:38:17,257 --> 01:38:23,017
[JEFF] kept it, on the first day the evidence was reputable enough for their own scientist.

1557
01:38:23,017 --> 01:38:28,777
[JEFF] Thirteen years late by one reading; exactly on time by another. And then they sold

1558
01:38:28,777 --> 01:38:34,537
[JEFF] the substitutes. Creative destruction, run inside one company, in one corporate calendar.

1559
01:38:34,537 --> 01:38:39,795
[CYRUS] [deliberate] And the substitutes are where the pattern comes back. Because the replacement — the

1560
01:38:39,795 --> 01:38:45,052
[CYRUS] fourth fluid, H-F-C one-thirty-four-a — was designed to break down before it reaches the stratosphere.

1561
01:38:45,052 --> 01:38:50,309
[CYRUS] No chlorine, no ozone problem. Solved. It's just that it's a greenhouse gas fourteen hundred

1562
01:38:50,309 --> 01:38:55,566
[CYRUS] times as powerful as carbon dioxide. Now — a home fridge holds only a few

1563
01:38:55,566 --> 01:39:00,823
[CYRUS] ounces. One leak is nothing. But hundreds of millions of boxes and cars and supermarket

1564
01:39:00,823 --> 01:39:06,080
[CYRUS] racks, leaking a few percent a year, for decades — that's a climate line item.

1565
01:39:06,080 --> 01:39:11,337
[CYRUS] Scale, again.

1566
01:39:11,337 --> 01:39:13,737
[JEFF] Of course it is.

1567
01:39:13,737 --> 01:39:18,937
[CYRUS] So in October two thousand sixteen, in Kigali, Rwanda, a hundred and ninety-seven countries amend

1568
01:39:18,937 --> 01:39:24,137
[CYRUS] the Montreal Protocol to phase down the replacement. And the scientific assessment says that amendment

1569
01:39:24,137 --> 01:39:29,337
[CYRUS] alone is expected to avoid somewhere between a third and half a degree Celsius of

1570
01:39:29,337 --> 01:39:34,537
[CYRUS] warming by twenty-one hundred. A treaty about what's inside your refrigerator is one of the

1571
01:39:34,537 --> 01:39:39,737
[CYRUS] largest single climate actions anyone has ever taken. And it ran on the ozone treaty's

1572
01:39:39,737 --> 01:39:44,937
[CYRUS] machinery, because that machinery worked. The U-S Senate ratified it in twenty twenty-two, sixty-nine to

1573
01:39:44,937 --> 01:39:50,137
[CYRUS] twenty-seven.

1574
01:39:50,137 --> 01:39:53,737
[JEFF] [genuinely surprised] Sixty-nine votes. I did not expect that.

1575
01:39:53,737 --> 01:39:58,937
[CYRUS] Neither did I. And here's where the fluids have landed. A new home refrigerator today

1576
01:39:58,937 --> 01:40:04,137
[CYRUS] runs on isobutane. Which is flammable — so the safety standard caps the charge at

1577
01:40:04,137 --> 01:40:09,337
[CYRUS] a hundred and fifty grams, a few ounces — and which is one of the

1578
01:40:09,337 --> 01:40:14,537
[CYRUS] fluids Freon displaced in the nineteen thirties. Supermarkets are going to carbon dioxide, at very

1579
01:40:14,537 --> 01:40:19,737
[CYRUS] high pressure. And industrial cold storage never left ammonia. Linde's fluid, a hundred and fifty

1580
01:40:19,737 --> 01:40:24,937
[CYRUS] years on. Four fluids in a century — and the fourth one is the first

1581
01:40:24,937 --> 01:40:30,137
[CYRUS] one.

1582
01:40:30,137 --> 01:40:31,737
[JEFF] [reflective] And the sky?

1583
01:40:31,737 --> 01:40:37,437
[CYRUS] Healing. Slowly. The C-F-Cs are gone — phased out in the rich world by ninety-six,

1584
01:40:37,437 --> 01:40:43,137
[CYRUS] everywhere by twenty-ten. The ozone layer is projected to be back to nineteen eighty levels

1585
01:40:43,137 --> 01:40:48,837
[CYRUS] around twenty-forty globally, and over Antarctica around twenty sixty-six. Which is after most of the

1586
01:40:48,837 --> 01:40:54,537
[CYRUS] people listening to this are dead. And it's not tidy — the latest assessment found

1587
01:40:54,537 --> 01:41:00,237
[CYRUS] emissions of one by-product gas, HFC-twenty-three, running up to eight times higher than expected, and

1588
01:41:00,237 --> 01:41:05,937
[CYRUS] somebody had to go find out where an outlawed C-F-C was still coming from. But

1589
01:41:05,937 --> 01:41:11,637
[CYRUS] Shanklin's line is that "perhaps the most startling lesson from the ozone hole is just

1590
01:41:11,637 --> 01:41:17,337
[CYRUS] how quickly our planet can change." In both directions.

1591
01:41:17,337 --> 01:41:22,804
[JEFF] So there's one more thing I want to put on the table before we step

1592
01:41:22,804 --> 01:41:28,271
[JEFF] back, because I think it's the thing we'd be most embarrassed to skip. Everything we've

1593
01:41:28,271 --> 01:41:33,737
[JEFF] described is the rich world. America, Europe, Japan. Where's everybody else?

1594
01:41:33,737 --> 01:41:39,297
[CYRUS] [deliberate] So this is the part I keep coming back to. If you picture the

1595
01:41:39,297 --> 01:41:44,857
[CYRUS] map filling in with color — the United States goes from one home in twelve

1596
01:41:44,857 --> 01:41:50,417
[CYRUS] to four in five between nineteen thirty and nineteen fifty. Then Europe, a generation later.

1597
01:41:50,417 --> 01:41:55,977
[CYRUS] Then Japan, which is incredible: one home in ten in nineteen sixty, nine in ten

1598
01:41:55,977 --> 01:42:01,537
[CYRUS] by nineteen seventy. America's twenty-year curve, run in ten. Then China's cities — single digits

1599
01:42:01,537 --> 01:42:07,097
[CYRUS] in the mid-eighties, nine in ten by the late two thousands. And then it slows

1600
01:42:07,097 --> 01:42:12,657
[CYRUS] way down. India today is about thirty-eight percent — but that's sixty-three percent in the

1601
01:42:12,657 --> 01:42:18,217
[CYRUS] cities and twenty-five percent in the villages. India's cities are where America was in nineteen

1602
01:42:18,217 --> 01:42:23,777
[CYRUS] fifty; its villages are where America was in nineteen thirty-five. And rural sub-Saharan Africa is

1603
01:42:23,777 --> 01:42:29,337
[CYRUS] at about four percent.

1604
01:42:29,337 --> 01:42:30,137
[JEFF] [incredulous] Four?

1605
01:42:30,137 --> 01:42:35,852
[CYRUS] Four homes in a hundred with a refrigerator. And the aid organizations put it this

1606
01:42:35,852 --> 01:42:41,566
[CYRUS] way: just over a billion people — roughly one in eight — are at high

1607
01:42:41,566 --> 01:42:47,280
[CYRUS] risk from lacking access to cooling. And I want to say that carefully, because "a

1608
01:42:47,280 --> 01:42:52,995
[CYRUS] billion" sounds like hype. It means no reliable cold for food, for medicine, or for

1609
01:42:52,995 --> 01:42:58,709
[CYRUS] your body, in a hot place. Not "no fridge." And nobody is resisting cold. This

1610
01:42:58,709 --> 01:43:04,423
[CYRUS] is not resistance. It's the same gates America hit in the nineteen thirties — price,

1611
01:43:04,423 --> 01:43:10,137
[CYRUS] a wire to the house, financing — a century later.

1612
01:43:10,137 --> 01:43:15,537
[JEFF] And I'd add that it's their framing, not ours. India's government wrote the first national

1613
01:43:15,537 --> 01:43:20,937
[JEFF] cooling plan in the world, in twenty nineteen, and the phrase it uses is that

1614
01:43:20,937 --> 01:43:26,337
[JEFF] cooling is "a developmental need." The Nigerian who runs the U-N's sustainable-energy effort puts it

1615
01:43:26,337 --> 01:43:31,737
[JEFF] more bluntly: cooling is "a make-or-break issue" for development.

1616
01:43:31,737 --> 01:43:37,337
[CYRUS] Which is their word, not an aid agency's. And the Kigali schedule encodes that, by

1617
01:43:37,337 --> 01:43:42,937
[CYRUS] the way — India and the Gulf states don't have to finish phasing down until

1618
01:43:42,937 --> 01:43:48,537
[CYRUS] twenty forty-seven, because the rich world built its cold on Freon first. So — a

1619
01:43:48,537 --> 01:43:54,137
[CYRUS] billion people are where New York's tenements were in nineteen hundred. Their governments call cold

1620
01:43:54,137 --> 01:43:59,737
[CYRUS] a developmental need. Montreal worked. Kigali is the encore. And the machine in your kitchen

1621
01:43:59,737 --> 01:44:05,337
[CYRUS] has been through four fluids in a hundred years.

1622
01:44:05,337 --> 01:44:08,937
[JEFF] [reflective] Four fluids. And every single one of them was the safe one, the day

1623
01:44:08,937 --> 01:44:12,537
[JEFF] it was chosen.

1624
01:35:47,815 --> 01:35:53,215
[JEFF] So we've walked two hundred years. And I think this is the moment to pull

1625
01:35:53,215 --> 01:35:58,615
[JEFF] back and look at what that story built. Because you've got a page of numbers

1626
01:35:58,615 --> 01:36:04,015
[JEFF] in front of you, and I think every one of them lands differently now than

1627
01:36:04,015 --> 01:36:09,415
[JEFF] it would have an hour and a half ago.

1628
01:36:09,415 --> 01:36:13,681
[CYRUS] [amused] I checked each of them three times. Because there are a lot of bad

1629
01:36:13,681 --> 01:36:17,948
[CYRUS] refrigerator statistics floating around out there and I didn't want to be the guy who

1630
01:36:17,948 --> 01:36:22,215
[CYRUS] read one.

1631
01:36:22,215 --> 01:36:23,815
[JEFF] [warmly] I appreciate that.

1632
01:36:23,815 --> 01:36:29,655
[CYRUS] So start with the curve, since we just lived through it. Nineteen thirty, roughly one

1633
01:36:29,655 --> 01:36:35,495
[CYRUS] American home in twelve has a refrigerator. Nineteen forty census, forty-four percent — with twenty-seven

1634
01:36:35,495 --> 01:36:41,335
[CYRUS] percent still on ice and twenty-seven percent with nothing. Nineteen fifty, about four in five.

1635
01:36:41,335 --> 01:36:47,175
[CYRUS] That's radio's pace, slower than television, and much faster than the telephone, which took about

1636
01:36:47,175 --> 01:36:53,015
[CYRUS] seventy years to reach half of homes. And it happened during the Depression.

1637
01:36:53,015 --> 01:36:56,615
[JEFF] Which we've established I still find hard to believe.

1638
01:36:56,615 --> 01:37:02,515
[CYRUS] Now here's the one that changes how you look at your kitchen. Nicola Twilley, who

1639
01:37:02,515 --> 01:37:08,415
[CYRUS] wrote the best modern book on this, puts it this way: roughly three-quarters of the

1640
01:37:08,415 --> 01:37:14,315
[CYRUS] food consumed in the United States is processed, shipped, stored, or sold under refrigeration. Three-quarters

1641
01:37:14,315 --> 01:37:20,215
[CYRUS] of everything on your plate has been cold somewhere you've never seen. And the somewhere

1642
01:37:20,215 --> 01:37:26,115
[CYRUS] is enormous — three and a half to five and a half billion cubic feet

1643
01:37:26,115 --> 01:37:32,015
[CYRUS] of refrigerated warehouse space in this country, depending on who counts. That's eleven to sixteen

1644
01:37:32,015 --> 01:37:37,915
[CYRUS] cubic feet per person. Roughly one home refrigerator's worth of industrial cold for every man,

1645
01:37:37,915 --> 01:37:43,815
[CYRUS] woman, and child in America, sitting in a building you've never been inside.

1646
01:37:43,815 --> 01:37:47,015
[JEFF] [genuinely surprised] A fridge per person, in warehouses.

1647
01:37:47,015 --> 01:37:52,135
[CYRUS] Right. And in your own house — one in three American homes has two or

1648
01:37:52,135 --> 01:37:57,255
[CYRUS] more refrigerators. About one home in two hundred has none. And then flip it over.

1649
01:37:57,255 --> 01:38:02,375
[CYRUS] Just over a billion people on earth — roughly one in eight — are at

1650
01:38:02,375 --> 01:38:07,495
[CYRUS] high risk from lacking access to cooling. In rural sub-Saharan Africa, four homes in a

1651
01:38:07,495 --> 01:38:12,615
[CYRUS] hundred have a refrigerator.

1652
01:38:12,615 --> 01:38:16,215
[JEFF] So the nickel piece didn't go away. It moved.

1653
01:38:16,215 --> 01:38:21,415
[CYRUS] And here's the one you asked me to double-check. The International Energy Agency worked out

1654
01:38:21,415 --> 01:38:26,615
[CYRUS] that the United States uses about as much energy cooling its buildings as the entire

1655
01:38:26,615 --> 01:38:31,815
[CYRUS] continent of Africa uses, in electricity, for everything. Three hundred and twenty-eight million Americans, versus

1656
01:38:31,815 --> 01:38:37,015
[CYRUS] one point two billion people — all their lights, all their factories, all their everything.

1657
01:38:37,015 --> 01:38:42,215
[CYRUS] And it's true, in that specific form. There's a version of that stat that's about

1658
01:38:42,215 --> 01:38:47,415
[CYRUS] global air conditioning versus Africa that I don't trust, so I'm not going to say

1659
01:38:47,415 --> 01:38:52,615
[CYRUS] it.

1660
01:38:52,615 --> 01:38:56,215
[JEFF] [reflective] Which — good. And that's the cost side. How big is the whole thing,

1661
01:38:56,215 --> 01:38:59,815
[JEFF] as an industry?

1662
01:38:59,815 --> 01:39:05,815
[CYRUS] Roughly five billion refrigeration, air-conditioning and heat-pump systems running on earth — one machine for

1663
01:39:05,815 --> 01:39:11,815
[CYRUS] every one and a half people alive — and something like fifteen million people employed

1664
01:39:11,815 --> 01:39:17,815
[CYRUS] keeping them running. The Hudson Valley's twenty thousand ice cutters, times seven hundred and fifty.

1665
01:39:17,815 --> 01:39:23,415
[JEFF] And is there a number on the other side of the ledger?

1666
